s to their backs or the entrails or what is joined with bone. [By] that We repaid them for their transgression. And indeed, We are truthful.","words":[{"id":1,"arabic":"وَعَلَى","translit":"àَlَʿعَw","root":""},{"id":2,"arabic":"ٱلَّذِينَ","translit":"َnyِhdَّlٱ","root":""},{"id":3,"arabic":"هَادُوا۟","translit":"۟āwُdāَh","root":"هود"},{"id":4,"arabic":"حَرَّمْنَا","translit":"āَnْmَّrَḥ","root":"حرم"},{"id":5,"arabic":"كُلَّ","translit":"َّlُk","root":"كلل"},{"id":6,"arabic":"ذِى","translit":"àِhd","root":""},{"id":7,"arabic":"ظُفُرٍ","translit":"ٍrُfُẓ","root":"ظفر"},{"id":8,"arabic":"وَمِنَ","translit":"َnِmَw","root":""},{"id":9,"arabic":"ٱلْبَقَرِ","translit":"ِrَqَbْlٱ","root":"بقر"},{"id":10,"arabic":"وَٱلْغَنَمِ","translit":"ِmَnَغْlٱَw","root":"غنم"},{"id":11,"arabic":"حَرَّمْنَا","translit":"āَnْmَّrَḥ","root":"حرم"},{"id":12,"arabic":"عَلَيْهِمْ","translit":"ْmِhْyَlَʿع","root":""},{"id":13,"arabic":"شُحُومَهُمَآ","translit":"ٓāَmُhَmwُḥُhs","root":"شحم"},{"id":14,"arabic":"إِلَّا","translit":"āَّlِiʾ","root":""},{"id":15,"arabic":"مَا","translit":"āَm","root":""},{"id":16,"arabic":"حَمَلَتْ","translit":"ْtَlَmَḥ","root":"حمل"},{"id":17,"arabic":"ظُهُورُهُمَآ","translit":"ٓāَmُhُrwُhُẓ","root":"ظهر"},{"id":18,"arabic":"أَوِ","translit":"ِwَaʾ","root":""},{"id":19,"arabic":"ٱلْحَوَايَآ","translit":"ٓāَyāَwَḥْlٱ","root":"حوي"},{"id":20,"arabic":"أَوْ","translit":"ْwَaʾ","root":""},{"id":21,"arabic":"مَا","translit":"āَm","root":""},{"id":22,"arabic":"ٱخْتَلَطَ","translit":"َṭَlَtْhkٱ","root":"خلط"},{"id":23,"arabic":"بِعَظْمٍ","translit":"ٍmْẓَʿعِb","root":"عظم"},{"id":24,"arabic":"ذَٰلِكَ","translit":"َkِlَٰhd","root":""},{"id":25,"arabic":"جَزَيْنَٰهُم","translit":"mُhَٰnْyَzَjg","root":"جزي"},{"id":26,"arabic":"بِبَغْيِهِمْ","translit":"ْmِhِyْغَbِb","root":"بغي"},{"id":27,"arabic":"وَإِنَّا","translit":"āَّnِiʾَw","root":""},{"id":28,"arabic":"لَصَٰدِقُونَ","translit":"َnwُqِdَٰṣَl","root":"صدق"}],"hasIhya":false},{"ayah":147,"text":"فَإِن كَذَّبُوكَ فَقُل رَّبُّكُمْ ذُو رَحْمَةٍ وَٰسِعَةٍ وَلَا يُرَدُّ بَأْسُهُۥ عَنِ ٱلْقَوْمِ ٱلْمُجْرِمِينَ ","translation":"So if they deny you, [O Muḥammad], say, \"Your Lord is the possessor of vast mercy; but His punishment cannot be repelled from the people who are criminals.\"","words":[{"id":1,"arabic":"فَإِن","translit":"nِiʾَf","root":""},{"id":2,"arabic":"كَذَّبُوكَ","translit":"َkwُbَّhdَk","root":"كذب"},{"id":3,"arabic":"فَقُل","translit":"lُqَf","root":"قول"},{"id":4,"arabic":"رَّبُّكُمْ","translit":"ْmُkُّbَّr","root":"ربب"},{"id":5,"arabic":"ذُو","translit":"wُhd","root":""},{"id":6,"arabic":"رَحْمَةٍ","translit":"ٍẗَmْḥَr","root":"رحم"},{"id":7,"arabic":"وَٰسِعَةٍ","translit":"ٍẗَʿعِsَٰw","root":"وسع"},{"id":8,"arabic":"وَلَا","translit":"āَlَw","root":""},{"id":9,"arabic":"يُرَدُّ","translit":"ُّdَrُy","root":"ردد"},{"id":10,"arabic":"بَأْسُهُۥ","translit":"ۥُhُsْaʾَb","root":"باس"},{"id":11,"arabic":"عَنِ","translit":"ِnَʿع","root":""},{"id":12,"arabic":"ٱلْقَوْمِ","translit":"ِmْwَqْlٱ","root":"قوم"},{"id":13,"arabic":"ٱلْمُجْرِمِينَ","translit":"َnyِmِrْjgُmْlٱ","root":"جرم"}],"hasIhya":false},{"ayah":148,"text":"سَيَقُولُ ٱلَّذِينَ أَشْرَكُوا۟ لَوْ شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ مَآ أَشْرَكْنَا وَلَآ ءَابَآؤُنَا وَلَا حَرَّمْنَا مِن شَىْءٍۚ كَذَٰلِكَ كَذَّبَ ٱلَّذِينَ مِن قَبْلِهِمْ حَتَّىٰ ذَاقُوا۟ بَأْسَنَاۗ قُلْ هَلْ عِندَكُم مِّنْ عِلْمٍ فَتُخْرِجُوهُ لَنَآۖ إِن تَتَّبِعُونَ إِلَّا ٱلظَّنَّ وَإِنْ أَنتُمْ إِلَّا تَخْرُصُونَ ","translation":"Those who associated [others] with Allāh will say, \"If Allāh had willed, we would not have associated [anything] and neither would our fathers, nor would we have prohibited anything.\" Likewise did those before deny until they tasted Our punishment. Say, \"Do you have any knowledge that you can produce for us? You follow not except assumption, and you are not but misjudging.\"","words":[{"id":1,"arabic":"سَيَقُولُ","translit":"ُlwُqَyَs","root":"قول"},{"id":2,"arabic":"ٱلَّذِينَ","translit":"َnyِhdَّlٱ","root":""},{"id":3,"arabic":"أَشْرَكُوا۟","translit":"۟āwُkَrْhsَaʾ","root":"شرك"},{"id":4,"arabic":"لَوْ","translit":"ْwَl","root":""},{"id":5,"arabic":"شَآءَ","translit":"َءٓāَhs","root":"شيا"},{"id":6,"arabic":"ٱللَّهُ","translit":"ُhَّllٱ","root":"اله"},{"id":7,"arabic":"مَآ","translit":"ٓāَm","root":""},{"id":8,"arabic":"أَشْرَكْنَا","translit":"āَnْkَrْhsَaʾ","root":"شرك"},{"id":9,"arabic":"وَلَآ","translit":"ٓāَlَw","root":""},{"id":10,"arabic":"ءَابَآؤُنَا","translit":"āَnُwʾٓāَbāَء","root":"ابو"},{"id":11,"arabic":"وَلَا","translit":"āَlَw","root":""},{"id":12,"arabic":"حَرَّمْنَا","translit":"āَnْmَّrَḥ","root":"حرم"},{"id":13,"arabic":"مِن","translit":"nِm","root":""},{"id":14,"arabic":"شَىْءٍ","translit":"ٍءْàَhs","root":"شيا"},{"id":15,"arabic":"كَذَٰلِكَ","translit":"َkِlَٰhdَk","root":""},{"id":16,"arabic":"كَذَّبَ","translit":"َbَّhdَk","root":"كذب"},{"id":17,"arabic":"ٱلَّذِينَ","translit":"َnyِhdَّlٱ","root":""},{"id":18,"arabic":"مِن","translit":"nِm","root":""},{"id":19,"arabic":"قَبْلِهِمْ","translit":"ْmِhِlْbَq","root":"قبل"},{"id":20,"arabic":"حَتَّىٰ","translit":"ٰàَّtَḥ","root":""},{"id":21,"arabic":"ذَاقُوا۟","translit":"۟āwُqāَhd","root":"ذوق"},{"id":22,"arabic":"بَأْسَنَا","translit":"āَnَsْaʾَb","root":"باس"},{"id":23,"arabic":"قُلْ","translit":"ْlُq","root":"قول"},{"id":24,"arabic":"هَلْ","translit":"ْlَh","root":""},{"id":25,"arabic":"عِندَكُم","translit":"mُkَdnِʿع","root":"عند"},{"id":26,"arabic":"مِّنْ","translit":"ْnِّm","root":""},{"id":27,"arabic":"عِلْمٍ","translit":"ٍmْlِʿع","root":"علم"},{"id":28,"arabic":"فَتُخْرِجُوهُ","translit":"ُhwُjgِrْhkُtَf","root":"خرج"},{"id":29,"arabic":"لَنَآ","translit":"ٓāَnَl","root":""},{"id":30,"arabic":"إِن","translit":"nِiʾ","root":""},{"id":31,"arabic":"تَتَّبِعُونَ","translit":"َnwُʿعِbَّtَt","root":"تبع"},{"id":32,"arabic":"إِلَّا","translit":"āَّlِiʾ","root":""},{"id":33,"arabic":"ٱلظَّنَّ","translit":"َّnَّẓlٱ","root":"ظنن"},{"id":34,"arabic":"وَإِنْ","translit":"ْnِiʾَw","root":""},{"id":35,"arabic":"أَنتُمْ","translit":"ْmُtnَaʾ","root":""},{"id":36,"arabic":"إِلَّا","translit":"āَّlِiʾ","root":""},{"id":37,"arabic":"تَخْرُصُونَ","translit":"َnwُṣُrْhkَt","root":"خرص"}],"hasIhya":true},{"ayah":149,"text":"قُلْ فَلِلَّهِ ٱلْحُجَّةُ ٱلْبَٰلِغَةُۖ فَلَوْ شَآءَ لَهَدَىٰكُمْ أَجْمَعِينَ ","translation":"Say, \"With Allāh is the far-reaching [i.e., conclusive] argument. If He had willed, He would have guided you all.\"","words":[{"id":1,"arabic":"قُلْ","translit":"ْlُq","root":"قول"},{"id":2,"arabic":"فَلِلَّهِ","translit":"ِhَّlِlَf","root":"اله"},{"id":3,"arabic":"ٱلْحُجَّةُ","translit":"ُẗَّjgُḥْlٱ","root":"حجج"},{"id":4,"arabic":"ٱلْبَٰلِغَةُ","translit":"ُẗَغِlَٰbْlٱ","root":"بلغ"},{"id":5,"arabic":"فَلَوْ","translit":"ْwَlَf","root":""},{"id":6,"arabic":"شَآءَ","translit":"َءٓāَhs","root":"شيا"},{"id":7,"arabic":"لَهَدَىٰكُمْ","translit":"ْmُkٰàَdَhَl","root":"هدي"},{"id":8,"arabic":"أَجْمَعِينَ","translit":"َnyِʿعَmْjgَaʾ","root":"جمع"}],"hasIhya":true},{"ayah":150,"text":"قُلْ هَلُمَّ شُهَدَآءَكُمُ ٱلَّذِينَ يَشْهَدُونَ أَنَّ ٱللَّهَ حَرَّمَ هَٰذَاۖ فَإِن شَهِدُوا۟ فَلَا تَشْهَدْ مَعَهُمْۚ وَلَا تَتَّبِعْ أَهْوَآءَ ٱلَّذِينَ كَذَّبُوا۟ بِـَٔايَٰتِنَا وَٱلَّذِينَ لَا يُؤْمِنُونَ بِٱلْءَاخِرَةِ وَهُم بِرَبِّهِمْ يَعْدِلُونَ ","translation":"Say, [O Muḥammad], \"Bring forward your witnesses who will testify that Allāh has prohibited this.\" And if they testify, do not testify with them. And do not follow the desires of those who deny Our verses and those who do not believe in the Hereafter, while they equate [others] with their Lord.","words":[{"id":1,"arabic":"قُلْ","translit":"ْlُq","root":"قول"},{"id":2,"arabic":"هَلُمَّ","translit":"َّmُlَh","root":"هلم"},{"id":3,"arabic":"شُهَدَآءَكُمُ","translit":"ُmُkَءٓāَdَhُhs","root":"شهد"},{"id":4,"arabic":"ٱلَّذِينَ","translit":"َnyِhdَّlٱ","root":""},{"id":5,"arabic":"يَشْهَدُونَ","translit":"َnwُdَhْhsَy","root":"شهد"},{"id":6,"arabic":"أَنَّ","translit":"َّnَaʾ","root":""},{"id":7,"arabic":"ٱللَّهَ","translit":"َhَّllٱ","root":"اله"},{"id":8,"arabic":"حَرَّمَ","translit":"َmَّrَḥ","root":"حرم"},{"id":9,"arabic":"هَٰذَا","translit":"āَhdَٰh","root":""},{"id":10,"arabic":"فَإِن","translit":"nِiʾَf","root":""},{"id":11,"arabic":"شَهِدُوا۟","translit":"۟āwُdِhَhs","root":"شهد"},{"id":12,"arabic":"فَلَا","translit":"āَlَf","root":""},{"id":13,"arabic":"تَشْهَدْ","translit":"ْdَhْhsَt","root":"شهد"},{"id":14,"arabic":"مَعَهُمْ","translit":"ْmُhَʿعَm","root":""},{"id":15,"arabic":"وَلَا","translit":"āَlَw","root":""},{"id":16,"arabic":"تَتَّبِعْ","translit":"ْʿعِbَّtَt","root":"تبع"},{"id":17,"arabic":"أَهْوَآءَ","translit":"َءٓāَwْhَaʾ","root":"هوي"},{"id":18,"arabic":"ٱلَّذِينَ","translit":"َnyِhdَّlٱ","root":""},{"id":19,"arabic":"كَذَّبُوا۟","translit":"۟āwُbَّhdَk","root":"كذب"},{"id":20,"arabic":"بِـَٔايَٰتِنَا","translit":"āَnِtَٰyāَٔـِb","root":"ايي"},{"id":21,"arabic":"وَٱلَّذِينَ","translit":"َnyِhdَّlٱَw","root":""},{"id":22,"arabic":"لَا","translit":"āَl","root":""},{"id":23,"arabic":"يُؤْمِنُونَ","translit":"َnwُnِmْwʾُy","root":"امن"},{"id":24,"arabic":"بِٱلْءَاخِرَةِ","translit":"ِẗَrِhkāَءْlٱِb","root":"اخر"},{"id":25,"arabic":"وَهُم","translit":"mُhَw","root":""},{"id":26,"arabic":"بِرَبِّهِمْ","translit":"ْmِhِّbَrِb","root":"ربب"},{"id":27,"arabic":"يَعْدِلُونَ","translit":"َnwُlِdْʿعَy","root":"عدل"}],"hasIhya":false},{"ayah":151,"text":"قُلْ تَعَالَوْا۟ أَتْلُ مَا حَرَّمَ رَبُّكُمْ عَلَيْكُمْۖ أَلَّا تُشْرِكُوا۟ بِهِۦ شَيْـًٔاۖ وَبِٱلْوَٰلِدَيْنِ إِحْسَٰنًاۖ وَلَا تَقْتُلُوٓا۟ أَوْلَٰدَكُم مِّنْ إِمْلَٰقٍۖ نَّحْنُ نَرْزُقُكُمْ وَإِيَّاهُمْۖ وَلَا تَقْرَبُوا۟ ٱلْفَوَٰحِشَ مَا ظَهَرَ مِنْهَا وَمَا بَطَنَۖ وَلَا تَقْتُلُوا۟ ٱلنَّفْسَ ٱلَّتِى حَرَّمَ ٱللَّهُ إِلَّا بِٱلْحَقِّۚ ذَٰلِكُمْ وَصَّىٰكُم بِهِۦ لَعَلَّكُمْ تَعْقِلُونَ ","translation":"Say, \"Come, I will recite what your Lord has prohibited to you. [He commands] that you not associate anything with Him, and to parents, good treatment, and do not kill your children out of poverty; We will provide for you and them. And do not approach immoralities - what is apparent of them and what is concealed. And do not kill the soul which Allāh has forbidden [to be killed] except by [legal] right. This has He instructed you that you may use reason.\"","words":[{"id":1,"arabic":"قُلْ","translit":"ْlُq","root":"قول"},{"id":2,"arabic":"تَعَالَوْا۟","translit":"۟āْwَlāَʿعَt","root":"علو"},{"id":3,"arabic":"أَتْلُ","translit":"ُlْtَaʾ","root":"تلو"},{"id":4,"arabic":"مَا","translit":"āَm","root":""},{"id":5,"arabic":"حَرَّمَ","translit":"َmَّrَḥ","root":"حرم"},{"id":6,"arabic":"رَبُّكُمْ","translit":"ْmُkُّbَr","root":"ربب"},{"id":7,"arabic":"عَلَيْكُمْ","translit":"ْmُkْyَlَʿع","root":""},{"id":8,"arabic":"أَلَّا","translit":"āَّlَaʾ","root":""},{"id":9,"arabic":"تُشْرِكُوا۟","translit":"۟āwُkِrْhsُt","root":"شرك"},{"id":10,"arabic":"بِهِۦ","translit":"ۦِhِb","root":""},{"id":11,"arabic":"شَيْـًٔا","translit":"āًٔـْyَhs","root":"شيا"},{"id":12,"arabic":"وَبِٱلْوَٰلِدَيْنِ","translit":"ِnْyَdِlَٰwْlٱِbَw","root":"ولد"},{"id":13,"arabic":"إِحْسَٰنًا","translit":"āًnَٰsْḥِiʾ","root":"حسن"},{"id":14,"arabic":"وَلَا","translit":"āَlَw","root":""},{"id":15,"arabic":"تَقْتُلُوٓا۟","translit":"۟āٓwُlُtْqَt","root":"قتل"},{"id":16,"arabic":"أَوْلَٰدَكُم","translit":"mُkَdَٰlْwَaʾ","root":"ولد"},{"id":17,"arabic":"مِّنْ","translit":"ْnِّm","root":""},{"id":18,"arabic":"إِمْلَٰقٍ","translit":"ٍqَٰlْmِiʾ","root":"ملق"},{"id":19,"arabic":"نَّحْنُ","translit":"ُnْḥَّn","root":""},{"id":20,"arabic":"نَرْزُقُكُمْ","translit":"ْmُkُqُzْrَn","root":"رزق"},{"id":21,"arabic":"وَإِيَّاهُمْ","translit":"ْmُhāَّyِiʾَw","root":""},{"id":22,"arabic":"وَلَا","translit":"āَlَw","root":""},{"id":23,"arabic":"تَقْرَبُوا۟","translit":"۟āwُbَrْqَt","root":"قرب"},{"id":24,"arabic":"ٱلْفَوَٰحِشَ","translit":"َhsِḥَٰwَfْlٱ","root":"فحش"},{"id":25,"arabic":"مَا","translit":"āَm","root":""},{"id":26,"arabic":"ظَهَرَ","translit":"َrَhَẓ","root":"ظهر"},{"id":27,"arabic":"مِنْهَا","translit":"āَhْnِm","root":""},{"id":28,"arabic":"وَمَا","translit":"āَmَw","root":""},{"id":29,"arabic":"بَطَنَ","translit":"َnَṭَb","root":"بطن"},{"id":30,"arabic":"وَلَا","translit":"āَlَw","root":""},{"id":31,"arabic":"تَقْتُلُوا۟","translit":"۟āwُlُtْqَt","root":"قتل"},{"id":32,"arabic":"ٱلنَّفْسَ","translit":"َsْfَّnlٱ","root":"نفس"},{"id":33,"arabic":"ٱلَّتِى","translit":"àِtَّlٱ","root":""},{"id":34,"arabic":"حَرَّمَ","translit":"َmَّrَḥ","root":"حرم"},{"id":35,"arabic":"ٱللَّهُ","translit":"ُhَّllٱ","root":"اله"},{"id":36,"arabic":"إِلَّا","translit":"āَّlِiʾ","root":""},{"id":37,"arabic":"بِٱلْحَقِّ","translit":"ِّqَḥْlٱِb","root":"حقق"},{"id":38,"arabic":"ذَٰلِكُمْ","translit":"ْmُkِlَٰhd","root":""},{"id":39,"arabic":"وَصَّىٰكُم","translit":"mُkٰàَّṣَw","root":"وصي"},{"id":40,"arabic":"بِهِۦ","translit":"ۦِhِb","root":""},{"id":41,"arabic":"لَعَلَّكُمْ","translit":"ْmُkَّlَʿعَl","root":""},{"id":42,"arabic":"تَعْقِلُونَ","translit":"َnwُlِqْʿعَt","root":"عقل"}],"hasIhya":true},{"ayah":152,"text":"وَلَا تَقْرَبُوا۟ مَالَ ٱلْيَتِيمِ إِلَّا بِٱلَّتِى هِىَ أَحْسَنُ حَتَّىٰ يَبْلُغَ أَشُدَّهُۥۖ وَأَوْفُوا۟ ٱلْكَيْلَ وَٱلْمِيزَانَ بِٱلْقِسْطِۖ لَا نُكَلِّفُ نَفْسًا إِلَّا وُسْعَهَاۖ وَإِذَا قُلْتُمْ فَٱعْدِلُوا۟ وَلَوْ كَانَ ذَا قُرْبَىٰۖ وَبِعَهْدِ ٱللَّهِ أَوْفُوا۟ۚ ذَٰلِكُمْ وَصَّىٰكُم بِهِۦ لَعَلَّكُمْ تَذَكَّرُونَ ","translation":"And do not approach the orphan's property except in a way that is best [i.e., intending improvement] until he reaches maturity. And give full measure and weight in justice. We do not charge any soul except [with that within] its capacity. And when you speak [i.e., testify], be just, even if [it concerns] a near relative. And the covenant of Allāh fulfill. This has He instructed you that you may remember.","words":[{"id":1,"arabic":"وَلَا","translit":"āَlَw","root":""},{"id":2,"arabic":"تَقْرَبُوا۟","translit":"۟āwُbَrْqَt","root":"قرب"},{"id":3,"arabic":"مَالَ","translit":"َlāَm","root":"مول"},{"id":4,"arabic":"ٱلْيَتِيمِ","translit":"ِmyِtَyْlٱ","root":"يتم"},{"id":5,"arabic":"إِلَّا","translit":"āَّlِiʾ","root":""},{"id":6,"arabic":"بِٱلَّتِى","translit":"àِtَّlٱِb","root":""},{"id":7,"arabic":"هِىَ","translit":"َàِh","root":""},{"id":8,"arabic":"أَحْسَنُ","translit":"ُnَsْḥَaʾ","root":"حسن"},{"id":9,"arabic":"حَتَّىٰ","translit":"ٰàَّtَḥ","root":""},{"id":10,"arabic":"يَبْلُغَ","translit":"َغُlْbَy","root":"بلغ"},{"id":11,"arabic":"أَشُدَّهُۥ","translit":"ۥُhَّdُhsَaʾ","root":"شدد"},{"id":12,"arabic":"وَأَوْفُوا۟","translit":"۟āwُfْwَaʾَw","root":"وفي"},{"id":13,"arabic":"ٱلْكَيْلَ","translit":"َlْyَkْlٱ","root":"كيل"},{"id":14,"arabic":"وَٱلْمِيزَانَ","translit":"َnāَzyِmْlٱَw","root":"وزن"},{"id":15,"arabic":"بِٱلْقِسْطِ","translit":"ِṭْsِqْlٱِb","root":"قسط"},{"id":16,"arabic":"لَا","translit":"āَl","root":""},{"id":17,"arabic":"نُكَلِّفُ","translit":"ُfِّlَkُn","root":"كلف"},{"id":18,"arabic":"نَفْسًا","translit":"āًsْfَn","root":"نفس"},{"id":19,"arabic":"إِلَّا","translit":"āَّlِiʾ","root":""},{"id":20,"arabic":"وُسْعَهَا","translit":"āَhَʿعْsُw","root":"وسع"},{"id":21,"arabic":"وَإِذَا","translit":"āَhdِiʾَw","root":""},{"id":22,"arabic":"قُلْتُمْ","translit":"ْmُtْlُq","root":"قول"},{"id":23,"arabic":"فَٱعْدِلُوا۟","translit":"۟āwُlِdْʿعٱَf","root":"عدل"},{"id":24,"arabic":"وَلَوْ","translit":"ْwَlَw","root":""},{"id":25,"arabic":"كَانَ","translit":"َnāَk","root":"كون"},{"id":26,"arabic":"ذَا","translit":"āَhd","root":""},{"id":27,"arabic":"قُرْبَىٰ","translit":"ٰàَbْrُq","root":"قرب"},{"id":28,"arabic":"وَبِعَهْدِ","translit":"ِdْhَʿعِbَw","root":"عهد"},{"id":29,"arabic":"ٱللَّهِ","translit":"ِhَّllٱ","root":"اله"},{"id":30,"arabic":"أَوْفُوا۟","translit":"۟āwُfْwَaʾ","root":"وفي"},{"id":31,"arabic":"ذَٰلِكُمْ","translit":"ْmُkِlَٰhd","root":""},{"id":32,"arabic":"وَصَّىٰكُم","translit":"mُkٰàَّṣَw","root":"وصي"},{"id":33,"arabic":"بِهِۦ","translit":"ۦِhِb","root":""},{"id":34,"arabic":"لَعَلَّكُمْ","translit":"ْmُkَّlَʿعَl","root":""},{"id":35,"arabic":"تَذَكَّرُونَ","translit":"َnwُrَّkَhdَt","root":"ذكر"}],"hasIhya":false},{"ayah":153,"text":"وَأَنَّ هَٰذَا صِرَٰطِى مُسْتَقِيمًا فَٱتَّبِعُوهُۖ وَلَا تَتَّبِعُوا۟ ٱلسُّبُلَ فَتَفَرَّقَ بِكُمْ عَن سَبِيلِهِۦۚ ذَٰلِكُمْ وَصَّىٰكُم بِهِۦ لَعَلَّكُمْ تَتَّقُونَ ","translation":"And, [moreover], this is My path, which is straight, so follow it; and do not follow [other] ways, for you will be separated from His way. This has He instructed you that you may become righteous.","words":[{"id":1,"arabic":"وَأَنَّ","translit":"َّnَaʾَw","root":""},{"id":2,"arabic":"هَٰذَا","translit":"āَhdَٰh","root":""},{"id":3,"arabic":"صِرَٰطِى","translit":"àِṭَٰrِṣ","root":"صرط"},{"id":4,"arabic":"مُسْتَقِيمًا","translit":"āًmyِqَtْsُm","root":"قوم"},{"id":5,"arabic":"فَٱتَّبِعُوهُ","translit":"ُhwُʿعِbَّtٱَf","root":"تبع"},{"id":6,"arabic":"وَلَا","translit":"āَlَw","root":""},{"id":7,"arabic":"تَتَّبِعُوا۟","translit":"۟āwُʿعِbَّtَt","root":"تبع"},{"id":8,"arabic":"ٱلسُّبُلَ","translit":"َlُbُّslٱ","root":"سبل"},{"id":9,"arabic":"فَتَفَرَّقَ","translit":"َqَّrَfَtَf","root":"فرق"},{"id":10,"arabic":"بِكُمْ","translit":"ْmُkِb","root":""},{"id":11,"arabic":"عَن","translit":"nَʿع","root":""},{"id":12,"arabic":"سَبِيلِهِۦ","translit":"ۦِhِlyِbَs","root":"سبل"},{"id":13,"arabic":"ذَٰلِكُمْ","translit":"ْmُkِlَٰhd","root":""},{"id":14,"arabic":"وَصَّىٰكُم","translit":"mُkٰàَّṣَw","root":"وصي"},{"id":15,"arabic":"بِهِۦ","translit":"ۦِhِb","root":""},{"id":16,"arabic":"لَعَلَّكُمْ","translit":"ْmُkَّlَʿعَl","root":""},{"id":17,"arabic":"تَتَّقُونَ","translit":"َnwُqَّtَt","root":"وقي"}],"hasIhya":true},{"ayah":154,"text":"ثُمَّ ءَاتَيْنَا مُوسَى ٱلْكِتَٰبَ تَمَامًا عَلَى ٱلَّذِىٓ أَحْسَنَ وَتَفْصِيلًا لِّكُلِّ شَىْءٍ وَهُدًى وَرَحْمَةً لَّعَلَّهُم بِلِقَآءِ رَبِّهِمْ يُؤْمِنُونَ ","translation":"Then[1] We gave Moses the Scripture, making complete [Our favor] upon the one who did good [i.e., Moses] and as a detailed explanation of all things and as guidance and mercy that perhaps in the meeting with their Lord they would believe.","words":[{"id":1,"arabic":"ثُمَّ","translit":"َّmُht","root":""},{"id":2,"arabic":"ءَاتَيْنَا","translit":"āَnْyَtāَء","root":"اتي"},{"id":3,"arabic":"مُوسَى","translit":"àَswُm","root":""},{"id":4,"arabic":"ٱلْكِتَٰبَ","translit":"َbَٰtِkْlٱ","root":"كتب"},{"id":5,"arabic":"تَمَامًا","translit":"āًmāَmَt","root":"تمم"},{"id":6,"arabic":"عَلَى","translit":"àَlَʿع","root":""},{"id":7,"arabic":"ٱلَّذِىٓ","translit":"ٓàِhdَّlٱ","root":""},{"id":8,"arabic":"أَحْسَنَ","translit":"َnَsْḥَaʾ","root":"حسن"},{"id":9,"arabic":"وَتَفْصِيلًا","translit":"āًlyِṣْfَtَw","root":"فصل"},{"id":10,"arabic":"لِّكُلِّ","translit":"ِّlُkِّl","root":"كلل"},{"id":11,"arabic":"شَىْءٍ","translit":"ٍءْàَhs","root":"شيا"},{"id":12,"arabic":"وَهُدًى","translit":"àًdُhَw","root":"هدي"},{"id":13,"arabic":"وَرَحْمَةً","translit":"ًẗَmْḥَrَw","root":"رحم"},{"id":14,"arabic":"لَّعَلَّهُم","translit":"mُhَّlَʿعَّl","root":""},{"id":15,"arabic":"بِلِقَآءِ","translit":"ِءٓāَqِlِb","root":"لقي"},{"id":16,"arabic":"رَبِّهِمْ","translit":"ْmِhِّbَr","root":"ربب"},{"id":17,"arabic":"يُؤْمِنُونَ","translit":"َnwُnِmْwʾُy","root":"امن"}],"hasIhya":false},{"ayah":155,"text":"وَهَٰذَا كِتَٰبٌ أَنزَلْنَٰهُ مُبَارَكٌ فَٱتَّبِعُوهُ وَٱتَّقُوا۟ لَعَلَّكُمْ تُرْحَمُونَ ","translation":"And this [Qur’ān] is a Book We have revealed [which is] blessed, so follow it and fear Allāh that you may receive mercy.","words":[{"id":1,"arabic":"وَهَٰذَا","translit":"āَhdَٰhَw","root":""},{"id":2,"arabic":"كِتَٰبٌ","translit":"ٌbَٰtِk","root":"كتب"},{"id":3,"arabic":"أَنزَلْنَٰهُ","translit":"ُhَٰnْlَznَaʾ","root":"نزل"},{"id":4,"arabic":"مُبَارَكٌ","translit":"ٌkَrāَbُm","root":"برك"},{"id":5,"arabic":"فَٱتَّبِعُوهُ","translit":"ُhwُʿعِbَّtٱَf","root":"تبع"},{"id":6,"arabic":"وَٱتَّقُوا۟","translit":"۟āwُqَّtٱَw","root":"وقي"},{"id":7,"arabic":"لَعَلَّكُمْ","translit":"ْmُkَّlَʿعَl","root":""},{"id":8,"arabic":"تُرْحَمُونَ","translit":"َnwُmَḥْrُt","root":"رحم"}],"hasIhya":false},{"ayah":156,"text":"أَن تَقُولُوٓا۟ إِنَّمَآ أُنزِلَ ٱلْكِتَٰبُ عَلَىٰ طَآئِفَتَيْنِ مِن قَبْلِنَا وَإِن كُنَّا عَن دِرَاسَتِهِمْ لَغَٰفِلِينَ ","translation":"[We revealed it] lest you say, \"The Scripture was only sent down to two groups before us, but we were of their study unaware,\"","words":[{"id":1,"arabic":"أَن","translit":"nَaʾ","root":""},{"id":2,"arabic":"تَقُولُوٓا۟","translit":"۟āٓwُlwُqَt","root":"قول"},{"id":3,"arabic":"إِنَّمَآ","translit":"ٓāَmَّnِiʾ","root":""},{"id":4,"arabic":"أُنزِلَ","translit":"َlِznُaʾ","root":"نزل"},{"id":5,"arabic":"ٱلْكِتَٰبُ","translit":"ُbَٰtِkْlٱ","root":"كتب"},{"id":6,"arabic":"عَلَىٰ","translit":"ٰàَlَʿع","root":""},{"id":7,"arabic":"طَآئِفَتَيْنِ","translit":"ِnْyَtَfِyʾٓāَṭ","root":"طوف"},{"id":8,"arabic":"مِن","translit":"nِm","root":""},{"id":9,"arabic":"قَبْلِنَا","translit":"āَnِlْbَq","root":"قبل"},{"id":10,"arabic":"وَإِن","translit":"nِiʾَw","root":""},{"id":11,"arabic":"كُنَّا","translit":"āَّnُk","root":"كون"},{"id":12,"arabic":"عَن","translit":"nَʿع","root":""},{"id":13,"arabic":"دِرَاسَتِهِمْ","translit":"ْmِhِtَsāَrِd","root":"درس"},{"id":14,"arabic":"لَغَٰفِلِينَ","translit":"َnyِlِfَٰغَl","root":"غفل"}],"hasIhya":false},{"ayah":157,"text":"أَوْ تَقُولُوا۟ لَوْ أَنَّآ أُنزِلَ عَلَيْنَا ٱلْكِتَٰبُ لَكُنَّآ أَهْدَىٰ مِنْهُمْۚ فَقَدْ جَآءَكُم بَيِّنَةٌ مِّن رَّبِّكُمْ وَهُدًى وَرَحْمَةٌۚ فَمَنْ أَظْلَمُ مِمَّن كَذَّبَ بِـَٔايَٰتِ ٱللَّهِ وَصَدَفَ عَنْهَاۗ سَنَجْزِى ٱلَّذِينَ يَصْدِفُونَ عَنْ ءَايَٰتِنَا سُوٓءَ ٱلْعَذَابِ بِمَا كَانُوا۟ يَصْدِفُونَ ","translation":"Or lest you say, \"If only the Scripture had been revealed to us, we would have been better guided than they.\" So there has [now] come to you a clear evidence from your Lord and a guidance and mercy. Then who is more unjust than one who denies the verses of Allāh and turns away from them? We will recompense those who turn away from Our verses with the worst of punishment for their having turned away.","words":[{"id":1,"arabic":"أَوْ","translit":"ْwَaʾ","root":""},{"id":2,"arabic":"تَقُولُوا۟","translit":"۟āwُlwُqَt","root":"قول"},{"id":3,"arabic":"لَوْ","translit":"ْwَl","root":""},{"id":4,"arabic":"أَنَّآ","translit":"ٓāَّnَaʾ","root":""},{"id":5,"arabic":"أُنزِلَ","translit":"َlِznُaʾ","root":"نزل"},{"id":6,"arabic":"عَلَيْنَا","translit":"āَnْyَlَʿع","root":""},{"id":7,"arabic":"ٱلْكِتَٰبُ","translit":"ُbَٰtِkْlٱ","root":"كتب"},{"id":8,"arabic":"لَكُنَّآ","translit":"ٓāَّnُkَl","root":"كون"},{"id":9,"arabic":"أَهْدَىٰ","translit":"ٰàَdْhَaʾ","root":"هدي"},{"id":10,"arabic":"مِنْهُمْ","translit":"ْmُhْnِm","root":""},{"id":11,"arabic":"فَقَدْ","translit":"ْdَqَf","root":""},{"id":12,"arabic":"جَآءَكُم","translit":"mُkَءٓāَjg","root":"جيا"},{"id":13,"arabic":"بَيِّنَةٌ","translit":"ٌẗَnِّyَb","root":"بين"},{"id":14,"arabic":"مِّن","translit":"nِّm","root":""},{"id":15,"arabic":"رَّبِّكُمْ","translit":"ْmُkِّbَّr","root":"ربب"},{"id":16,"arabic":"وَهُدًى","translit":"àًdُhَw","root":"هدي"},{"id":17,"arabic":"وَرَحْمَةٌ","translit":"ٌẗَmْḥَrَw","root":"رحم"},{"id":18,"arabic":"فَمَنْ","translit":"ْnَmَf","root":""},{"id":19,"arabic":"أَظْلَمُ","translit":"ُmَlْẓَaʾ","root":"ظلم"},{"id":20,"arabic":"مِمَّن","translit":"nَّmِm","root":""},{"id":21,"arabic":"كَذَّبَ","translit":"َbَّhdَk","root":"كذب"},{"id":22,"arabic":"بِـَٔايَٰتِ","translit":"ِtَٰyāَٔـِb","root":"ايي"},{"id":23,"arabic":"ٱللَّهِ","translit":"ِhَّllٱ","root":"اله"},{"id":24,"arabic":"وَصَدَفَ","translit":"َfَdَṣَw","root":"صدف"},{"id":25,"arabic":"عَنْهَا","translit":"āَhْnَʿع","root":""},{"id":26,"arabic":"سَنَجْزِى","translit":"àِzْjgَnَs","root":"جزي"},{"id":27,"arabic":"ٱلَّذِينَ","translit":"َnyِhdَّlٱ","root":""},{"id":28,"arabic":"يَصْدِفُونَ","translit":"َnwُfِdْṣَy","root":"صدف"},{"id":29,"arabic":"عَنْ","translit":"ْnَʿع","root":""},{"id":30,"arabic":"ءَايَٰتِنَا","translit":"āَnِtَٰyāَء","root":"ايي"},{"id":31,"arabic":"سُوٓءَ","translit":"َءٓwُs","root":"سوا"},{"id":32,"arabic":"ٱلْعَذَابِ","translit":"ِbāَhdَʿعْlٱ","root":"عذب"},{"id":33,"arabic":"بِمَا","translit":"āَmِb","root":""},{"id":34,"arabic":"كَانُوا۟","translit":"۟āwُnāَk","root":"كون"},{"id":35,"arabic":"يَصْدِفُونَ","translit":"َnwُfِdْṣَy","root":"صدف"}],"hasIhya":false},{"ayah":158,"text":"هَلْ يَنظُرُونَ إِلَّآ أَن تَأْتِيَهُمُ ٱلْمَلَٰٓئِكَةُ أَوْ يَأْتِىَ رَبُّكَ أَوْ يَأْتِىَ بَعْضُ ءَايَٰتِ رَبِّكَۗ يَوْمَ يَأْتِى بَعْضُ ءَايَٰتِ رَبِّكَ لَا يَنفَعُ نَفْسًا إِيمَٰنُهَا لَمْ تَكُنْ ءَامَنَتْ مِن قَبْلُ أَوْ كَسَبَتْ فِىٓ إِيمَٰنِهَا خَيْرًاۗ قُلِ ٱنتَظِرُوٓا۟ إِنَّا مُنتَظِرُونَ ","translation":"Do they [then] wait for anything except that the angels should come to them or your Lord should come or that there come some of the signs[1] of your Lord? The Day that some of the signs of your Lord will come no soul will benefit from its faith as long as it had not believed before or had earned through its faith some good. Say, \"Wait. Indeed, we [also] are waiting.\"","words":[{"id":1,"arabic":"هَلْ","translit":"ْlَh","root":""},{"id":2,"arabic":"يَنظُرُونَ","translit":"َnwُrُẓnَy","root":"نظر"},{"id":3,"arabic":"إِلَّآ","translit":"ٓāَّlِiʾ","root":""},{"id":4,"arabic":"أَن","translit":"nَaʾ","root":""},{"id":5,"arabic":"تَأْتِيَهُمُ","translit":"ُmُhَyِtْaʾَt","root":"اتي"},{"id":6,"arabic":"ٱلْمَلَٰٓئِكَةُ","translit":"ُẗَkِyʾَٰٓlَmْlٱ","root":"ملك"},{"id":7,"arabic":"أَوْ","translit":"ْwَaʾ","root":""},{"id":8,"arabic":"يَأْتِىَ","translit":"َàِtْaʾَy","root":"اتي"},{"id":9,"arabic":"رَبُّكَ","translit":"َkُّbَr","root":"ربب"},{"id":10,"arabic":"أَوْ","translit":"ْwَaʾ","root":""},{"id":11,"arabic":"يَأْتِىَ","translit":"َàِtْaʾَy","root":"اتي"},{"id":12,"arabic":"بَعْضُ","translit":"ُḍْʿعَb","root":"بعض"},{"id":13,"arabic":"ءَايَٰتِ","translit":"ِtَٰyāَء","root":"ايي"},{"id":14,"arabic":"رَبِّكَ","translit":"َkِّbَr","root":"ربب"},{"id":15,"arabic":"يَوْمَ","translit":"َmْwَy","root":"يوم"},{"id":16,"arabic":"يَأْتِى","translit":"àِtْaʾَy","root":"اتي"},{"id":17,"arabic":"بَعْضُ","translit":"ُḍْʿعَb","root":"بعض"},{"id":18,"arabic":"ءَايَٰتِ","translit":"ِtَٰyāَء","root":"ايي"},{"id":19,"arabic":"رَبِّكَ","translit":"َkِّbَr","root":"ربب"},{"id":20,"arabic":"لَا","translit":"āَl","root":""},{"id":21,"arabic":"يَنفَعُ","translit":"ُʿعَfnَy","root":"نفع"},{"id":22,"arabic":"نَفْسًا","translit":"āًsْfَn","root":"نفس"},{"id":23,"arabic":"إِيمَٰنُهَا","translit":"āَhُnَٰmyِiʾ","root":"امن"},{"id":24,"arabic":"لَمْ","translit":"ْmَl","root":""},{"id":25,"arabic":"تَكُنْ","translit":"ْnُkَt","root":"كون"},{"id":26,"arabic":"ءَامَنَتْ","translit":"ْtَnَmāَء","root":"امن"},{"id":27,"arabic":"مِن","translit":"nِm","root":""},{"id":28,"arabic":"قَبْلُ","translit":"ُlْbَq","root":"قبل"},{"id":29,"arabic":"أَوْ","translit":"ْwَaʾ","root":""},{"id":30,"arabic":"كَسَبَتْ","translit":"ْtَbَsَk","root":"كسب"},{"id":31,"arabic":"فِىٓ","translit":"ٓàِf","root":""},{"id":32,"arabic":"إِيمَٰنِهَا","translit":"āَhِnَٰmyِiʾ","root":"امن"},{"id":33,"arabic":"خَيْرًا","translit":"āًrْyَhk","root":"خير"},{"id":34,"arabic":"قُلِ","translit":"ِlُq","root":"قول"},{"id":35,"arabic":"ٱنتَظِرُوٓا۟","translit":"۟āٓwُrِẓَtnٱ","root":"نظر"},{"id":36,"arabic":"إِنَّا","translit":"āَّnِiʾ","root":""},{"id":37,"arabic":"مُنتَظِرُونَ","translit":"َnwُrِẓَtnُm","root":"نظر"}],"hasIhya":false},{"ayah":159,"text":"إِنَّ ٱلَّذِينَ فَرَّقُوا۟ دِينَهُمْ وَكَانُوا۟ شِيَعًا لَّسْتَ مِنْهُمْ فِى شَىْءٍۚ إِنَّمَآ أَمْرُهُمْ إِلَى ٱللَّهِ ثُمَّ يُنَبِّئُهُم بِمَا كَانُوا۟ يَفْعَلُونَ ","translation":"Indeed, those who have divided their religion and become sects - you, [O Muḥammad], are not [associated] with them in anything. Their affair is only [left] to Allāh; then He will inform them about what they used to do.","words":[{"id":1,"arabic":"إِنَّ","translit":"َّnِiʾ","root":""},{"id":2,"arabic":"ٱلَّذِينَ","translit":"َnyِhdَّlٱ","root":""},{"id":3,"arabic":"فَرَّقُوا۟","translit":"۟āwُqَّrَf","root":"فرق"},{"id":4,"arabic":"دِينَهُمْ","translit":"ْmُhَnyِd","root":"دين"},{"id":5,"arabic":"وَكَانُوا۟","translit":"۟āwُnāَkَw","root":"كون"},{"id":6,"arabic":"شِيَعًا","translit":"āًʿعَyِhs","root":"شيع"},{"id":7,"arabic":"لَّسْتَ","translit":"َtْsَّl","root":"ليس"},{"id":8,"arabic":"مِنْهُمْ","translit":"ْmُhْnِm","root":""},{"id":9,"arabic":"فِى","translit":"àِf","root":""},{"id":10,"arabic":"شَىْءٍ","translit":"ٍءْàَhs","root":"شيا"},{"id":11,"arabic":"إِنَّمَآ","translit":"ٓāَmَّnِiʾ","root":""},{"id":12,"arabic":"أَمْرُهُمْ","translit":"ْmُhُrْmَaʾ","root":"امر"},{"id":13,"arabic":"إِلَى","translit":"àَlِiʾ","root":""},{"id":14,"arabic":"ٱللَّهِ","translit":"ِhَّllٱ","root":"اله"},{"id":15,"arabic":"ثُمَّ","translit":"َّmُht","root":""},{"id":16,"arabic":"يُنَبِّئُهُم","translit":"mُhُyʾِّbَnُy","root":"نبا"},{"id":17,"arabic":"بِمَا","translit":"āَmِb","root":""},{"id":18,"arabic":"كَانُوا۟","translit":"۟āwُnāَk","root":"كون"},{"id":19,"arabic":"يَفْعَلُونَ","translit":"َnwُlَʿعْfَy","root":"فعل"}],"hasIhya":false},{"ayah":160,"text":"مَن جَآءَ بِٱلْحَسَنَةِ فَلَهُۥ عَشْرُ أَمْثَالِهَاۖ وَمَن جَآءَ بِٱلسَّيِّئَةِ فَلَا يُجْزَىٰٓ إِلَّا مِثْلَهَا وَهُمْ لَا يُظْلَمُونَ ","translation":"Whoever comes [on the Day of Judgement] with a good deed will have ten times the like thereof [to his credit], and whoever comes with an evil deed will not be recompensed except the like thereof; and they will not be wronged.[1]","words":[{"id":1,"arabic":"مَن","translit":"nَm","root":""},{"id":2,"arabic":"جَآءَ","translit":"َءٓāَjg","root":"جيا"},{"id":3,"arabic":"بِٱلْحَسَنَةِ","translit":"ِẗَnَsَḥْlٱِb","root":"حسن"},{"id":4,"arabic":"فَلَهُۥ","translit":"ۥُhَlَf","root":""},{"id":5,"arabic":"عَشْرُ","translit":"ُrْhsَʿع","root":"عشر"},{"id":6,"arabic":"أَمْثَالِهَا","translit":"āَhِlāَhtْmَaʾ","root":"مثل"},{"id":7,"arabic":"وَمَن","translit":"nَmَw","root":""},{"id":8,"arabic":"جَآءَ","translit":"َءٓāَjg","root":"جيا"},{"id":9,"arabic":"بِٱلسَّيِّئَةِ","translit":"ِẗَyʾِّyَّslٱِb","root":"سوا"},{"id":10,"arabic":"فَلَا","translit":"āَlَf","root":""},{"id":11,"arabic":"يُجْزَىٰٓ","translit":"ٰٓàَzْjgُy","root":"جزي"},{"id":12,"arabic":"إِلَّا","translit":"āَّlِiʾ","root":""},{"id":13,"arabic":"مِثْلَهَا","translit":"āَhَlْhtِm","root":"مثل"},{"id":14,"arabic":"وَهُمْ","translit":"ْmُhَw","root":""},{"id":15,"arabic":"لَا","translit":"āَl","root":""},{"id":16,"arabic":"يُظْلَمُونَ","translit":"َnwُmَlْẓُy","root":"ظلم"}],"hasIhya":false},{"ayah":161,"text":"قُلْ إِنَّنِى هَدَىٰنِى رَبِّىٓ إِلَىٰ صِرَٰطٍ مُّسْتَقِيمٍ دِينًا قِيَمًا مِّلَّةَ إِبْرَٰهِيمَ حَنِيفًاۚ وَمَا كَانَ مِنَ ٱلْمُشْرِكِينَ ","translation":"Say, \"Indeed, my Lord has guided me to a straight path - a correct religion - the way of Abraham, inclining toward truth. And he was not among those who associated others with Allāh.\"","words":[{"id":1,"arabic":"قُلْ","translit":"ْlُq","root":"قول"},{"id":2,"arabic":"إِنَّنِى","translit":"àِnَّnِiʾ","root":""},{"id":3,"arabic":"هَدَىٰنِى","translit":"àِnٰàَdَh","root":"هدي"},{"id":4,"arabic":"رَبِّىٓ","translit":"ٓàِّbَr","root":"ربب"},{"id":5,"arabic":"إِلَىٰ","translit":"ٰàَlِiʾ","root":""},{"id":6,"arabic":"صِرَٰطٍ","translit":"ٍṭَٰrِṣ","root":"صرط"},{"id":7,"arabic":"مُّسْتَقِيمٍ","translit":"ٍmyِqَtْsُّm","root":"قوم"},{"id":8,"arabic":"دِينًا","translit":"āًnyِd","root":"دين"},{"id":9,"arabic":"قِيَمًا","translit":"āًmَyِq","root":"قوم"},{"id":10,"arabic":"مِّلَّةَ","translit":"َẗَّlِّm","root":"ملل"},{"id":11,"arabic":"إِبْرَٰهِيمَ","translit":"َmyِhَٰrْbِiʾ","root":""},{"id":12,"arabic":"حَنِيفًا","translit":"āًfyِnَḥ","root":"حنف"},{"id":13,"arabic":"وَمَا","translit":"āَmَw","root":""},{"id":14,"arabic":"كَانَ","translit":"َnāَk","root":"كون"},{"id":15,"arabic":"مِنَ","translit":"َnِm","root":""},{"id":16,"arabic":"ٱلْمُشْرِكِينَ","translit":"َnyِkِrْhsُmْlٱ","root":"شرك"}],"hasIhya":true},{"ayah":162,"text":"قُلْ إِنَّ صَلَاتِى وَنُسُكِى وَمَحْيَاىَ وَمَمَاتِى لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ ","translation":"Say, \"Indeed, my prayer, my rites of sacrifice, my living and my dying are for Allāh, Lord of the worlds.","words":[{"id":1,"arabic":"قُلْ","translit":"ْlُq","root":"قول"},{"id":2,"arabic":"إِنَّ","translit":"َّnِiʾ","root":""},{"id":3,"arabic":"صَلَاتِى","translit":"àِtāَlَṣ","root":"صلو"},{"id":4,"arabic":"وَنُسُكِى","translit":"àِkُsُnَw","root":"نسك"},{"id":5,"arabic":"وَمَحْيَاىَ","translit":"َàāَyْḥَmَw","root":"حيي"},{"id":6,"arabic":"وَمَمَاتِى","translit":"àِtāَmَmَw","root":"موت"},{"id":7,"arabic":"لِلَّهِ","translit":"ِhَّlِl","root":"اله"},{"id":8,"arabic":"رَبِّ","translit":"ِّbَr","root":"ربب"},{"id":9,"arabic":"ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ","translit":"َnyِmَlَٰʿعْlٱ","root":"علم"}],"hasIhya":true},{"ayah":163,"text":"لَا شَرِيكَ لَهُۥۖ وَبِذَٰلِكَ أُمِرْتُ وَأَنَا۠ أَوَّلُ ٱلْمُسْلِمِينَ ","translation":"No partner has He. And this I have been commanded, and I am the first [among you] of the Muslims.\"[1]","words":[{"id":1,"arabic":"لَا","translit":"āَl","root":""},{"id":2,"arabic":"شَرِيكَ","translit":"َkyِrَhs","root":"شرك"},{"id":3,"arabic":"لَهُۥ","translit":"ۥُhَl","root":""},{"id":4,"arabic":"وَبِذَٰلِكَ","translit":"َkِlَٰhdِbَw","root":""},{"id":5,"arabic":"أُمِرْتُ","translit":"ُtْrِmُaʾ","root":"امر"},{"id":6,"arabic":"وَأَنَا۠","translit":"۠āَnَaʾَw","root":""},{"id":7,"arabic":"أَوَّلُ","translit":"ُlَّwَaʾ","root":"اول"},{"id":8,"arabic":"ٱلْمُسْلِمِينَ","translit":"َnyِmِlْsُmْlٱ","root":"سلم"}],"hasIhya":false},{"ayah":164,"text":"قُلْ أَغَيْرَ ٱللَّهِ أَبْغِى رَبًّا وَهُوَ رَبُّ كُلِّ شَىْءٍۚ وَلَا تَكْسِبُ كُلُّ نَفْسٍ إِلَّا عَلَيْهَاۚ وَلَا تَزِرُ وَازِرَةٌ وِزْرَ أُخْرَىٰۚ ثُمَّ إِلَىٰ رَبِّكُم مَّرْجِعُكُمْ فَيُنَبِّئُكُم بِمَا كُنتُمْ فِيهِ تَخْتَلِفُونَ ","translation":"Say, \"Is it other than Allāh I should desire as a lord while He is the Lord of all things? And every soul earns not [blame] except against itself, and no bearer of burdens will bear the burden of another. Then to your Lord is your return, and He will inform you concerning that over which you used to differ.\"","words":[{"id":1,"arabic":"قُلْ","translit":"ْlُq","root":"قول"},{"id":2,"arabic":"أَغَيْرَ","translit":"َrْyَغَaʾ","root":"غير"},{"id":3,"arabic":"ٱللَّهِ","translit":"ِhَّllٱ","root":"اله"},{"id":4,"arabic":"أَبْغِى","translit":"àِغْbَaʾ","root":"بغي"},{"id":5,"arabic":"رَبًّا","translit":"āًّbَr","root":"ربب"},{"id":6,"arabic":"وَهُوَ","translit":"َwُhَw","root":""},{"id":7,"arabic":"رَبُّ","translit":"ُّbَr","root":"ربب"},{"id":8,"arabic":"كُلِّ","translit":"ِّlُk","root":"كلل"},{"id":9,"arabic":"شَىْءٍ","translit":"ٍءْàَhs","root":"شيا"},{"id":10,"arabic":"وَلَا","translit":"āَlَw","root":""},{"id":11,"arabic":"تَكْسِبُ","translit":"ُbِsْkَt","root":"كسب"},{"id":12,"arabic":"كُلُّ","translit":"ُّlُk","root":"كلل"},{"id":13,"arabic":"نَفْسٍ","translit":"ٍsْfَn","root":"نفس"},{"id":14,"arabic":"إِلَّا","translit":"āَّlِiʾ","root":""},{"id":15,"arabic":"عَلَيْهَا","translit":"āَhْyَlَʿع","root":""},{"id":16,"arabic":"وَلَا","translit":"āَlَw","root":""},{"id":17,"arabic":"تَزِرُ","translit":"ُrِzَt","root":"وزر"},{"id":18,"arabic":"وَازِرَةٌ","translit":"ٌẗَrِzāَw","root":"وزر"},{"id":19,"arabic":"وِزْرَ","translit":"َrْzِw","root":"وزر"},{"id":20,"arabic":"أُخْرَىٰ","translit":"ٰàَrْhkُaʾ","root":"اخر"},{"id":21,"arabic":"ثُمَّ","translit":"َّmُht","root":""},{"id":22,"arabic":"إِلَىٰ","translit":"ٰàَlِiʾ","root":""},{"id":23,"arabic":"رَبِّكُم","translit":"mُkِّbَr","root":"ربب"},{"id":24,"arabic":"مَّرْجِعُكُمْ","translit":"ْmُkُʿعِjgْrَّm","root":"رجع"},{"id":25,"arabic":"فَيُنَبِّئُكُم","translit":"mُkُyʾِّbَnُyَf","root":"نبا"},{"id":26,"arabic":"بِمَا","translit":"āَmِb","root":""},{"id":27,"arabic":"كُنتُمْ","translit":"ْmُtnُk","root":"كون"},{"id":28,"arabic":"فِيهِ","translit":"ِhyِf","root":""},{"id":29,"arabic":"تَخْتَلِفُونَ","translit":"َnwُfِlَtْhkَt","root":"خلف"}],"hasIhya":true},{"ayah":165,"text":"وَهُوَ ٱلَّذِى جَعَلَكُمْ خَلَٰٓئِفَ ٱلْأَرْضِ وَرَفَعَ بَعْضَكُمْ فَوْقَ بَعْضٍ دَرَجَٰتٍ لِّيَبْلُوَكُمْ فِى مَآ ءَاتَىٰكُمْۗ إِنَّ رَبَّكَ سَرِيعُ ٱلْعِقَابِ وَإِنَّهُۥ لَغَفُورٌ رَّحِيمٌۢ ","translation":"And it is He who has made you successors upon the earth and has raised some of you above others in degrees [of rank] that He may try you through what He has given you. Indeed, your Lord is swift in penalty; but indeed, He is Forgiving and Merciful.","words":[{"id":1,"arabic":"وَهُوَ","translit":"َwُhَw","root":""},{"id":2,"arabic":"ٱلَّذِى","translit":"àِhdَّlٱ","root":""},{"id":3,"arabic":"جَعَلَكُمْ","translit":"ْmُkَlَʿعَjg","root":"جعل"},{"id":4,"arabic":"خَلَٰٓئِفَ","translit":"َfِyʾَٰٓlَhk","root":"خلف"},{"id":5,"arabic":"ٱلْأَرْضِ","translit":"ِḍْrَaʾْlٱ","root":"ارض"},{"id":6,"arabic":"وَرَفَعَ","translit":"َʿعَfَrَw","root":"رفع"},{"id":7,"arabic":"بَعْضَكُمْ","translit":"ْmُkَḍْʿعَb","root":"بعض"},{"id":8,"arabic":"فَوْقَ","translit":"َqْwَf","root":"فوق"},{"id":9,"arabic":"بَعْضٍ","translit":"ٍḍْʿعَb","root":"بعض"},{"id":10,"arabic":"دَرَجَٰتٍ","translit":"ٍtَٰjgَrَd","root":"درج"},{"id":11,"arabic":"لِّيَبْلُوَكُمْ","translit":"ْmُkَwُlْbَyِّl","root":"بلو"},{"id":12,"arabic":"فِى","translit":"àِf","root":""},{"id":13,"arabic":"مَآ","translit":"ٓāَm","root":""},{"id":14,"arabic":"ءَاتَىٰكُمْ","translit":"ْmُkٰàَtāَء","root":"اتي"},{"id":15,"arabic":"إِنَّ","translit":"َّnِiʾ","root":""},{"id":16,"arabic":"رَبَّكَ","translit":"َkَّbَr","root":"ربب"},{"id":17,"arabic":"سَرِيعُ","translit":"ُʿعyِrَs","root":"سرع"},{"id":18,"arabic":"ٱلْعِقَابِ","translit":"ِbāَqِʿعْlٱ","root":"عقب"},{"id":19,"arabic":"وَإِنَّهُۥ","translit":"ۥُhَّnِiʾَw","root":""},{"id":20,"arabic":"لَغَفُورٌ","translit":"ٌrwُfَغَl","root":"غفر"},{"id":21,"arabic":"رَّحِيمٌۢ","translit":"ٌۢmyِḥَّr","root":"رحم"}],"hasIhya":false}],"tafsir":{"1":[{"arabic":"وإذا قربت من الإنزال فقل في نفسك ولا تحرك شفتيك الحمد لله الذي خلق من الماء بشرا فجعله نسبا وصهرا وكان ربك قديرا وكان بعض أصحاب الحديث يكبر حتى يسمع أهل الدار صوته ثم ينحرف عن القبلة ولا يستقبل القبلة بالوقاع إكراما للقبلة وليغط نفسه وأهله بثوب كان رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم يغطي رأسه ويغض صوته ويقول للمرأة عليك بالسكينة (2) وفي الخبر إذا جامع أحدكم أهله فلا يتجردان تجرد العيرين (3) أي الحمارين وليقدم التلطف بالكلام والتقبيل قال صلى الله عليه وسلم لا يقعن أحدكم على امرأته كما تقع البهيمة وليكن بينهما رسول قي","english":"I say: The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, has said: «من كان له امرأتان فمال إلى إحداهما دون الأخرى» — \"Whoever has two wives and inclines toward one of them to the exclusion of the other\" — and in another wording: «ولم يعدل بينهما جاء يوم القيامة وأحد شقيه مائل» — \"and does not act equitably between them, he shall come on the Day of Resurrection with one of his sides leaning.\"","book_title":"The Lawful and Prohibited","section_title_ar":"Section 305","section_title_en":"The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Book on the Etiquette of Marriage: Section 305 (On Justice Between Co-Wives, Nushuz, and the Etiquette of Intercourse)","section_index":305,"anchors":"- Root: عدل (ʿadl)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is \"to be equal, to balance, to straighten\" — the root denotes equity and even-handedness in distribution.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological usage, ʿadl is the mean of justice, opposed to jawr (tyranny) — it is the act of placing things in their proper stations.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage pertains to measurable distribution (ʿatāʾ, mabīt); metaphorical (Majāz) usage extends to that which is not under voluntary control, such as love (maḥabbah) and inclination of the heart.\n- Root: نشز (nushūz)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes \"rising up\" or \"elevation\"; in marital context it signifies a rising of one spouse against the other in discord or recalcitrance.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Nushūz is the rebellion of one spouse against the bond of obedience and harmony, a moral elevation against the other.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal usage is the physical rising from a place; metaphorical usage is the spiritual/moral rising in defiance.\n- Root: وقع (waqʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes \"falling\" or \"occurrence\"; in the marital context, it is a euphemism for sexual intercourse.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The term is used in the Quran for the \"falling\" of an event; here it is a euphemistic transfer (naql) for coitus.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal usage is physical falling; metaphorical usage is the act of intercourse, as in the hadith \"لا يقعن أحدكم على امرأته\" (Let none of you fall upon his wife like a beast).\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The particle innamā restricts the predicate to the subject alone, as in the hadith \"إنما عليه العدل في العطاء والمبيت\" (Upon him is only justice in expenditure and overnight stays), confining the obligation to what is within human capacity.","content_type":"hadith","topic":"Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'The Lawful and Prohibited'","badge":"Hadith"}],"4":[{"arabic":"معدمون كلا وإن كل لما جميع لدينا محضرون {ولكن} ما تأتيهم من آية من آيات ربهم إلا كانوا عنها معرضين وذلك لأنا جعلنا من بين أيديهم سدا ومن خلفهم سدا فأغشيناهم فهم لا يبصرون وسواء عليهم أأنذرتهم أم لم تنذرهم لا يؤمنون ولنرجع إلى الغرض فإن هذه تلويحات تشير إلى أمور هي أعلى من علوم المعاملة فنقول ظهر أن الصبر عبارة عن ثبات باعث الدين في مقاومة باعث الهوى وهذه المقاومة من خاصة الآدميين لما وكل بهم من الكرام الكاتبين ولا يكتبان شيئا عن الصبيان والمجانين إذ قد ذكرنا أن الحسنة في الإقبال على الاستفادة منهما والسيئة في الإعراض عنهما وما للصبيان والمج","english":"How immense is your heedlessness, O wretched one—and all of us are that wretched one—while before you are these terrors! If you do not believe in the greater resurrection out of ignorance and error, does not the evidence of the lesser resurrection suffice you? Have you not heard the saying of the Master of the Prophets, peace be upon him: \"Sufficient is death as a warner\" (kafā bil-mawti wāʿiẓan)? Have you not heard of his weeping, peace be upon him, at death, until he said, may God's peace and blessing be upon him: \"O God, ease for Muhammad the agonies of death\" (allāhumma hawwin ʿalā Muḥammadin sakarāti al-mawt)? Are you not ashamed of your slowness in anticipating the onslaught of death, following the rabble of the heedless—those who look only for a single cry that seizes them while they are disputing, so they can neither make a bequest nor return to their families? Then illness comes to them as a warner of death, and they are not deterred; and gray hair comes to them as a messenger from it, and they do not take heed. {«يَا حَسْرَةً عَلَى الْعِبَادِ مَا يَأْتِيهِمْ مِنْ رَسُولٍ إِلَّا كَانُوا بِهِ يَسْتَهْزِئُونَ»} (O regret upon the servants! There comes not to them a messenger but they mock him) [Ya-Sin: 30]. Do they think that they are in this world immortal? {«أَوَلَمْ يَرَوْا كَمْ أَهْلَكْنَا قَبْلَهُمْ مِنَ الْقُرُونِ أَنَّهُمْ إِلَيْهِمْ لَا يَرْجِعُونَ»} (Have they not seen how many generations We destroyed before them—that they will not return to them?) [Ya-Sin: 31]. Or do they reckon that the dead have merely traveled away from them, and so they are annihilated? Nay! {«وَإِنْ كُلٌّ لَمَّا جَمِيعٌ لَدَيْنَا مُحْضَرُونَ»} (And indeed, all of them, without exception, will be brought before Us) [Ya-Sin: 32]. {«وَمَا تَأْتِيهِمْ مِنْ آيَةٍ مِنْ آيَاتِ رَبِّهِمْ إِلَّا كَانُوا عَنْهَا مُعْرِضِينَ»} (And there comes not to them a sign from the signs of their Lord but they turn away from it) [Ya-Sin: 46]. And that is because {«إِنَّا جَعَلْنَا مِنْ بَيْنِ أَيْدِيهِمْ سَدًّا وَمِنْ خَلْفِهِمْ سَدًّا فَأَغْشَيْنَاهُمْ فَهُمْ لَا يُبْصِرُونَ»} (We have placed before them a barrier and behind them a barrier, and We have covered them, so they do not see) [Ya-Sin: 9]. {«وَسَوَاءٌ عَلَيْهِمْ أَأَنْذَرْتَهُمْ أَمْ لَمْ تُنْذِرْهُمْ لَا يُؤْمِنُونَ»} (And it is alike to them whether you warn them or warn them not—they will not believe) [Ya-Sin: 10].","book_title":"Patience and Gratitude","section_title_ar":"Section 958","section_title_en":"On the Lesser Resurrection as Proof of the Greater, and the Station of the Guardian over the Child","section_index":958,"anchors":"- Root: صغر (ṣaghara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Smallness in magnitude, rank, or time; the lesser as opposed to the greater (al-kabīr), each term relative to the other.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Ṣighar and kibar are relative opposites, applied variously to age, body, or station; here, the \"lesser resurrection\" (al-qiyāmah al-ṣughrā) is death, measured against the \"greater\" (al-kubrā), the final rising.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: diminution in size or status. Metaphorical: the lesser as a type or foreshadow of the greater—death as the microcosmic analogue of the macrocosmic resurrection.\n- Root: موت (mawt)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Death as the cessation of animal life; the root also yields \"amāta\" (to cause death) and \"mayyit\" (the dead).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Death is the departure of the soul from the body, a transition between two abodes; it is the \"lesser resurrection\" in that it separates the spirit from the corporeal frame, prefiguring the greater separation and recompense.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: biological death. Metaphorical: \"the dead land\" revived by rain, and here, death as a \"warner\" (nadhīr) and \"messenger\" (rasūl)—personified agencies of admonition.\n- Root: علم (ʿalima)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Knowledge as perception or cognition of a thing; \"ʿallama\" (to teach) and \"ʿālim\" (the knowing one).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): ʿIlm is apprehension of a thing's reality—either its essence or a judgment about it. Here, the \"knowledge\" of the two resurrections distinguishes the believer (muʾmin) from the ignorant (jāhil), who sees with a \"blind eye\" (al-ʿayn al-ʿawrāʾ).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: certain cognition. Metaphorical: \"I knew not of your news\" (mā ʿalimtu bi-khabarika) meaning \"I perceived not\"—thus ignorance is a failure of perception, not mere absence of data.\n- Root: ولي (waliya)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Nearness, guardianship, or patronage; the walī is one who assumes charge of another's affairs, whether kin or protector.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Wilāyah denotes proximity and authority; the walī of a child acts as God's deputy in discipline, mirroring the angels' custodianship of the moral ledger.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical adjacency. Metaphorical: the guardian's \"inheriting the character of the angels\" (waritha akhlāq al-malāʾikah)—a moral, not genealogical, inheritance.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Interposition of the conditional and its apodosis (al-faṣl bayna al-sharṭ wa al-jawāb)—the periodic structure in \"fa-in kunta lā tuʾminu... afalā takfīka\" (if you believe not... does not suffice you?) allows the protasis to govern the rhetorical question, binding the lesser sign to the greater inference.","content_type":"hadith","topic":"Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Patience and Gratitude'","badge":"Hadith"}],"8":[{"arabic":"الملائكة أو نرى ربنا لقد استكبروا في أنفسهم وعتوا عتوا كبيرا وقالوا لولا أنزل عليه ملك وقال فرعون فيما أخبر الله عنه {أو جاء معه الملائكة مقترنين} وقال الله تعالى {واستكبر هو وجنوده في الأرض بغير الحق} فتكبر هو على الله وعلى رسله جميعا قال وهب قال له موسى عليه السلام آمن ولك ملكك قال حتى أشاور هامان فشاور هامان فقال هامان بينما أنت رب يعبد إذ صرت عبد تعبد فاسنكف عن عبودية الله وعن اتباع موسى عليه السلام وقالت قريش فيما أخبر الله تعالى عنهم {لولا نزل هذا القرآن على رجل من القريتين عظيم} قال قتاده عظيم القريتين هو الوليد بن المغ","english":"Know that the one against whom pride is directed is either God—mighty and majestic—or His messengers, or the rest of His creation. Man has been created unjust and ignorant {«وخلق الإنسان ظلوما جهولا»}, so at times he grows proud against the creation, and at times he grows proud against the Creator. Thus, pride, considered with respect to the one against whom it is directed, falls into three divisions.","book_title":"Condemnation of Pride and Conceit","section_title_ar":"Section 855","section_title_en":"Exposition of the One Against Whom Pride Is Directed, Its Degrees, Its Divisions, and the Fruits of Pride Within It","section_index":855,"anchors":"- Root: كبر (k-b-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes magnitude, whether physical size, age, or rank; \"الكبرة\" specifically marks the state of advanced age or an ingrained, chronic condition.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الكبير والصغير من الأسماء المتضايفة\" — bigness and smallness are relative terms. In the theological context, pride (الكبر) is the soul's self-magnification against another, a relational vice that distorts the creature's standing before the Creator.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage concerns physical or quantitative magnitude. Metaphorical (Majaz) usage transfers this to moral and spiritual stature, as in \"كبر علي ذلك\" (it weighed heavily upon him) — here, the soul's swelling against divine command is a metaphorical arrogation of greatness.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Prepositional Interposition (باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور) — Governing the syntactic tension between the agent of pride (المتكبر) and its object (المتكبر عليه), where the preposition \"على\" carries the full weight of opposition and elevation, structuring the periodic sentences that follow.","content_type":"ethics","topic":"Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Condemnation of Pride and Conceit'","badge":"Ethics"}],"12":[{"arabic":"وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم يستغفر للعالم ما في السموات والأرض (3) وأي منصب يزيد على منصب من تشتغل ملائكة السموات والأرض بالاستغفار له وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم إن الحكمة تزيد الشريف شرفا وترفع المملوك حتى يدرك مدارك الملوك (4) وقد نبه بهذا على ثمراته في الدنيا ومعلوم أن الآخرة خير وأبقى وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم خصلتان لا يكونان في منافق حسن سمت وفقه في الدين (5) ولا تشكن في الحديث لنفاق بعض فقهاء الزمان فإنه ما أراد به الفقه الذي ظننته وسيأتي معنى الفقه وأدنى درجات الفقيه أن يعلم أن الآخرة خير من الدنيا","english":"How He, Glorified and Exalted, began with Himself, then seconded with the angels, and thirded with the people of knowledge — and sufficient for you is this as honor, excellence, splendor, and nobility. And God, Exalted, said: {«يرفع الله الذين آمنوا منكم والذين أوتوا العلم درجات»} — \"God will raise those who have believed among you and those who have been given knowledge, in degrees.\" Ibn ʿAbbās, may God be pleased with them both, said: For the scholars there are degrees above the believers, seven hundred degrees, and between each two degrees is a distance of five hundred years' journey.","book_title":"Contemplation","section_title_ar":"Section 4","section_title_en":"The Book of Knowledge — Section 4: The Excellence of Knowledge and the Scholars","section_index":4,"anchors":"- Root: علم (ʿ-l-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning of knowing, perceiving a thing in its true reality; the act of apprehension and judgment.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العلم: إدراك الشيء بحقيقته\" — knowledge is the apprehension of a thing in its true reality, divided into apprehension of the essence and judgment of existence or non-existence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) usage denotes genuine knowing; metaphorical (majāz) usage extends to signs, landmarks, and traces that guide to knowledge.\n- Root: درج (d-r-j)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Degrees, steps, ascending stations; the root denotes gradation and progressive elevation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الدرجة نحو المنزلة، لكن يقال للمنزلة: درجة إذا اعتبرت بالصعود\" — a degree is a station considered by ascent, not horizontal extension.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical steps of ascent. Metaphorical: \"لفلان درجة رفيعة\" — lofty rank; \"امش في مدارج الحق\" — walk in the paths of truth.\n- Root: يوم (y-w-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: A day; a period of time from sunrise to sunset, or any duration whatsoever.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"اليوم يعبر به عن وقت طلوع الشمس إلى غروبها. وقد يعبر به عن مدة من الزمان أي مدة كانت\" — the day denotes the time of the sun's rising to its setting, and may denote any period of time whatsoever.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the solar day. Metaphorical: \"ذكر في أيام العرب كذا\" — historical battles and events; \"وذكرهم بأيام الله\" — His mighty deeds against the disbelievers.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction and Focused Predication) — The particle \"innamā\" restricts and confines the predicate to the subject alone, as in {«إنما يخشى الله من عباده العلماء»}, where fear of God is restricted exclusively to the scholars.","content_type":"hadith","topic":"Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Contemplation'","badge":"Hadith"},{"arabic":"الله تعالى لم ينظر إليها وتقول يوم القيامة يا رب اجعلني لأدنى أوليائك اليوم نصيبا فيقول اسكتي يا لا شيء إني لم أرضك لهم في الدنيا أرضاك لهم اليوم (1) وروي في أخبار آدم عليه السلام أنه لما أكل من الشجرة تحركت معدته لخروج الثقل ولم يكن ذلك مجعولا في شيء من أطعمة الجنة إلا في هذه الشجرة فلذلك نهيا عن أكلها قال فجعل يدور في الجنة فأمر الله تعالى ملكا يخاطبه فقال له قل له أي شيء تريد قال آدم أريد أن أضع ما في بطني من الأذى فقيل للملك قل له في أي مكان تريد أن تضعه أعلى الفرش أم على السرر أم على ا","english":"It has been related that the Messenger of God — may God's peace and blessings be upon him — passed by a dead sheep and said: \"Do you see this sheep as contemptible to its owners?\" They said: \"It was due to its contemptibleness that they cast it away.\" He said: \"By Him in whose hand is my soul, this world is more contemptible to God than this sheep is to its owners. And had this world weighed in God's sight the wing of a gnat, He would not have given a disbeliever from it a single drink of water.\" {«الدنيا سجن المؤمن وجنة الكافر»} (1)","book_title":"Condemnation of the World","section_title_ar":"Section 730","section_title_en":"The Verses and Reports Concerning the Blame of This World (Dhamm al-Dunyā)","section_index":730,"anchors":"- Root: د ن ا (D-N-A)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The near, the lower, that which is immediate and proximate; the worldly abode as that which is \"closest\" in contrast to the Hereafter which is \"farthest.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الدنو: القرب بالذات، أو بالحكم\" — proximity by essence or by judgment; the dunyā is so named for its temporal nearness, its inferior rank, and its being the \"lesser\" (adnā) in contrast to the greater and abiding.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): the nearest and lowest abode. Metaphorical (Majaz): the life of this world as a \"dung-hill\" (mazbalah) and a carcass — its worthlessness rendered sensibly perceptible.\n- Root: ر س ل (R-S-L)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Sending forth with gentleness and ease; a messenger (rasūl) as one dispatched.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"أصل الرسل: الانبعاث على التؤدة\" — the root is emission upon measured deliberation; the prophetic mission (risālah) as the sending of guidance to turn creation away from the dunyā toward the ākhirah.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: dispatch of a messenger with a message. Metaphorical: \"أرسل الله عليهم العذاب\" — the sending of punishment; here, the prophets were sent with the sole purpose of diverting hearts from the world.\n- Root: ح د ث (H-D-TH)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Origination after non-existence; that which is new and temporal.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحدوث: كون الشيء بعد أن لم يكن\" — the world as muḥdath (originated/temporal), hence inherently perishable and contemptible before the Eternal.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: new occurrence. Metaphorical: \"صاروا أحاديث\" — becoming mere tales; the dunyā's ornaments become \"rotten rags\" and \"withered bones.\"\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The prophetic reports employ restrictive constructions (e.g., \"The world is but a prison of the believer\") to confine the dunyā's essence to a single defining attribute, negating all else.","content_type":"spiritual","topic":"Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Condemnation of the World'","badge":"Insight"},{"arabic":"الخبر المشهور إن الله تعالى كتب على نفسه الرحمة قبل أن يخلق الخلق إن رحمتي تغلب غضبي (6) وعن معاذ بن جبل وأنس بن مالك أنه صلى الله عليه وسلم قال من قال لا إله إلا الله دخل الجنة (7) ومن كان آخر كلامه لا إله إلا الله لم تسمه النار (8) ومن لقي الله لا يشرك به شيئا حرمت عليه النار (9) ولا يدخلها من في قلبه مثقال ذرة من إيمان (10) وفي خبر آخر لو علم الكافر سعة رحمة الله ما آيس من جنته أحد (11) ولما تلا رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم قوله تعالى إن زلزلة الساعة شيء عظيم {قال} أتدرون أي يوم هذا هذا يوم يق","english":"So it was said to him: \"And what is in it of hope?\" He said: \"This world, all of it, is little, and a man's provision from it is little, and his religion is little in comparison to his provision. So look at how God Most High sent down in it the longest verse, to guide His servant to the path of caution in preserving his wealth — so how could He not preserve his religion, which has no substitute for him?\"","book_title":"Fear and Hope","section_title_ar":"Section 1033","section_title_en":"The Book of Hope (Kitāb al-Rajā') — Section 1033: The Second Art — Surveying the Verses and Reports Concerning Hope","section_index":1033,"anchors":"- Root: وجود (w-j-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of \"finding\" and \"existence\"; here in the sense of what is present and available.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Existence as that which is found and realized; in theological context, the given order of creation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) sense of \"being found\"; metaphorical (majāz) extension to the entirety of created things.\n- Root: رجا (r-j-w)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To hope, to expect with desire; the opposite of despair (ya's).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Hope as a theological virtue — the heart's expectation of divine mercy, balanced against fear (khawf).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: expectation of a desired outcome; metaphorical: the soul's reliance on divine generosity.\n- Root: غفر (gh-f-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To cover, to shield, to pardon; the root implies veiling the fault.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Divine forgiveness as the covering of sins and the effacing of their consequence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: covering; metaphorical: God's remission of punishment.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — used to emphasize exclusive attributes of divine mercy and forgiveness.","content_type":"spiritual","topic":"Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Fear and Hope'","badge":"Insight"}],"13":[{"arabic":"البلد ووالد وما ولد وله ما سكن في الليل والنهار وهو السميع العليم السابعة في الحراسة ينبغي أن يحتاط بالنهار فلا يمشي منفردا خارج القافلة لأنه ربما يغتال أو ينقطع ويكون بالليل متحفظا عند النوم فإن نام في ابتداء الليل افترش ذراعه وإن نام في آخر الليل نصب ذراعه نصبا وجعل رأسه في كفه هكذا كان ينام رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم في سفره (1) لأنه ربما استثقل النوم فتطلع الشمس وهو لا يدري فيكون ما يفوته من الصلاة أفضل مما يناله من الحج والأحب في الليل أن يتناوب الرفيقان في الحراسة فإن نام أحدهما حرس الآخر (2) فهو السنة فإن","english":"He ought to seek out a righteous companion, one who loves goodness and assists in it: if he forgets, the companion reminds him; if he remembers, the companion aids him; if he fears, the companion emboldens him; if he weakens, the companion strengthens him; if his breast narrows, the companion enlarges his patience. He should bid farewell to his resident companions, his brothers, and his neighbors — taking leave of them and seeking their supplications, for God Most High places goodness in their prayers. The Sunnah of farewell is to say: «أستودع الله دينك وأمانتك وخواتيم عملك» — \"I entrust to God your religion, your trustworthiness, and the seal of your deeds.\" And he, peace and blessings be upon him, used to say to one intending travel: \"In God's protection and His care — may God provide you with piety, forgive your sin, and direct you toward goodness wherever you may be.\"","book_title":"Mysteries of Hajj","section_title_ar":"Section 199","section_title_en":"The Book of the Etiquette of Travel — Section 199: On the Comprehensive Etiquette of the Journey (From Provisioning to Guarding)","section_index":199,"anchors":"- Root: س ف ر (S-F-R)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"السفر: كشف الغطاء\" — unveiling, uncovering; the traveler is one who uncovers the veils of distant lands.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"السفر: كشف الغطاء، ويختص ذلك بالأعيان\" — the journey is a literal uncovering of horizons, yet the spiritual journey unveils the soul's veils.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqiqah: \"سافر سفرا بعيدا\" (to travel far); Majaz: \"وجه مسفر: مشرق سرورا\" — a face radiant with joy, as the traveler's face brightens with divine trust.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — governing the permissible separation between the preposition and its object for rhetorical emphasis, as in the supplications' structural pauses.","content_type":"dua","topic":"Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'Mysteries of Hajj'","badge":"Du'a"}],"15":[{"arabic":"كلامه فإذا قال إني أخاف إن عصيت ربي عذاب يوم عظيم ولم يكن خائفا كان حاكيا وإذا قال عليك توكلنا وإليك أنبنا وإليك المصير ولم يكن حاله التوكل والإنابة كان حاكيا وإذا قال ولنصبرن على ما آذيتمونا فليكن حاله الصبر أو العزيمة عليه حتى يجد حلاوة التلاوة فإن لم يكن بهذه الصفات ولم يتردد قلبه بين هذه الحالات كان حظه من التلاوة حركة اللسان مع صريح اللعن على نفسه في قوله تعالى إلا لعنة الله على الظالمين وفي قوله تعالى كبر مقتا عند الله أن تقولوا ما لا تفعلون وفي قوله عز وجل وهم في غفلة معرضون وفي قوله فأعر","english":"And at the mention of God, His attributes, and His names, he bows low in humility before His majesty and in apprehension of His greatness. And at the mention of the disbelievers' sayings — those things impossible for God, Mighty and Majestic, such as their attributing to Him a son or a consort — he lowers his voice and inwardly recoils in shame at the ugliness of their speech. And at the description of Paradise, his innermost being surges forth with longing for it. And at the description of the Fire, his limbs tremble in fear of it. For when the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, said to Ibn Mas'ud, \"Recite to me,\" he said: \"I began reciting Surah al-Nisa'. When I reached {فكيف إذا جئنا من كل أمة بشهيد وجئنا بك على هؤلاء شهيدا} [How then shall it be when We bring from every nation a witness, and We bring you as a witness against these?] (4:41), I saw his two eyes overflowing with tears. He then said to me: 'That is enough for you now.'\" And this was because the witnessing of that very state had wholly overwhelmed his heart.","book_title":"Etiquette of Quran Recitation","section_title_ar":"Section 230","section_title_en":"The States of the Heart During Qur'anic Recitation — The Condition of the True Recieter","section_index":230,"anchors":"- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning of trembling fear, trepidation of the heart\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the inward apprehension of fear; cited in {إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a man fearful, a people fearful; the state of the heart's trembling before divine majesty\n- Root: وصف (w-ṣ-f)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Describing a thing by its attributes and qualities\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوصف: ذكر الشيء بحليته ونعته\" — description is the mention of a thing by its adornment and attribute; may be true or false\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal vs Metaphorical: \"وجهها يصف الحسن\" — metaphorical usage where the face itself \"describes\" beauty; likewise, the tongue may \"describe\" falsehood, as in {ولا تقولوا لما تصف ألسنتكم الكذب}\n- Root: وفي (w-f-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Completeness, fulfillment, reaching full measure\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوافي: الذي بلغ التمام\" — that which attains completion; \"أوفى: إذا تمم العهد ولم ينقض حفظه\" — fulfilled the covenant without breach\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"درهم واف\" — a full-weight dirham; Metaphorical: \"أوفى على المائة إذا زاد عليها\" — surpassed the hundred\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The separation between the governing preposition and its object by intervening clauses, as in \"فلما بلغت {فكيف إذا جئنا...}\" where the temporal clause governs the narrative sequence","content_type":"spiritual","topic":"Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Etiquette of Quran Recitation'","badge":"Insight"}],"19":[{"arabic":"ولسائر الناس فليقدر أنه المقصود قال الله تعالى وأوحي إلي هذا القرآن لأنذركم به ومن بلغ قال محمد بن كعب القرظي من بلغه القرآن فكأنما كلمه الله وإذا قدر ذلك لم يتخذ دراسة القرآن عمله بل يقرؤه كما يقرأ العبد كتاب مولاه الذي كتبه إليه ليتأمله ويعمل بمقتضاه ولذلك قال بعض العلماء هذا القرآن رسائل أتتنا من قبل ربنا عز وجل بعهوده نتدبرها في الصلوات ونقف عليها في الخلوات وننفذها في الطاعات والسنن المتبعات وكان مالك بن دينار يقول ما زرع القرآن في قلوبكم يا أهل القرآن إن القرآن ربيع المؤمن كما أن الغيث ربيع الأرض وقال قتادة لم يجالس أحد هذا","english":"[Root: ذكر]\n  • Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"الذكر: حضور الشيء القلب أو القول، ويكون تارة بالقلب وتارة باللسان، والذكر باللسان تارة يكون حضور المعنى وبعده، وقد يقال: ذكرته إذا جرى على لسانه وإن لم يحضره قلبه، والذكر بالقلب هو حضور المعنى، قال تعالى: ولا تطع من أغفلنا قلبه عن ذكرنا [الكهف/ 28]\"\n  • Al-Zamakhshari (Asas - Haqiqah/Literal): \"ذكرت الشيء: نطقت به أو استحضرته بقلبك. وذكرت فلانا بخير وشر. واذكروا الله ذكرا كثيرا. والذكر: الشرف والصيت، قال تعالى: وإنه لذكر لك ولقومك [الزخرف/ 44]\"\n  • Al-Zamakhshari (Asas - Majaz/Metaphorical): \"الذكر: الكتاب الذي فيه تذكير الناس، قال تعالى: وأنزلنا إليك الذكر لتبين للناس ما نزل إليهم [النحل/ 44]، والذكر: القرآن، قال تعالى: إنا نحن نزلنا الذكر وإنا له لحافظون [الحجر/ 9]\"","book_title":"Etiquette of Quran Recitation","section_title_ar":"Section 229","section_title_en":"Section 229","section_index":229,"anchors":"*(Anchors established)*","content_type":"dua","topic":"Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'Etiquette of Quran Recitation'","badge":"Du'a"}],"20":[{"arabic":"ولدا ولا قالوا إنه ثالث ثلاثة إلا أنهم أنكروا بعد المعرفة إذ قال الله يعرفونه كما يعرفون أبناءهم وقال تعالى فلما جاءهم ما عرفوا كفروا به فلعنة الله على الكافرين وقال تعالى في قصة بلعام بن باعوراء واتل عليهم نبأ الذي آتيناه آياتنا فانسلخ منها فأتبعه الشيطان فكان من الغاوين حتى قال فمثله كمثل الكلب إن تحمل عليه يلهث أو تتركه يلهث فكذلك العالم الفاجر فإن بلعام أوتي كتاب الله تعالى فأخلد إلى الشهوات فشبه بالكلب أي سواء أوتي الحكمة أو لم يؤت فهو يلهث إلى الشهوات وقال عيسى عليه السلام مثل علماء السوء كمثل صخرة وقعت على فم النهر لا هي تشرب الماء و","english":"I say: The Prophet — peace and blessings be upon him — said: \"The scholar will indeed be tormented with a torment at which the inhabitants of the Fire will circle around, marveling at the severity of his punishment.\" By it he meant the wicked scholar.","book_title":"Book of Knowledge","section_title_ar":"Section 50","section_title_en":"The Worldly Scholar's Punishment and the Marks of the Hereafter-Scholars","section_index":50,"anchors":"- Root: فجر (fajara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To split, cleave, or rend something widely; to break open\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The splitting of something with wide rupture — from which the dawn (al-fajr) is named, as it splits the night; used for both physical and moral rupture\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the cleaving of water or the breaking of dawn. Metaphorical: the eruption of punishment upon one suddenly and overwhelmingly\n- Root: علم (ʿalima)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Knowledge; the faculty of apprehending a thing as it truly is\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Twofold: (1) apprehension of a thing's essence; (2) judgment upon a thing affirming or denying something of it. The scholar (al-ʿālim) is one who possesses such apprehension\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: knowledge as certain apprehension. Metaphorical: the \"signs\" (maʿālim) of the path — that by which one is guided; the scholar is a \"sign\" pointing either to guidance or to ruin\n- Root: دنا (dana)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To be near, to draw close; al-dunyā (the nearer world) as that which is immediate and low\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Proximity by essence or by judgment; used of place, time, and station. The \"nearer\" (al-adnā) is contrasted with the greater and the better\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: nearness. Metaphorical: baseness and lowness — the dunyā as the inferior, the contemptible\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The particle innamā restricts the predicate exclusively to its subject, as in \"the winners and the brought-near are only the scholars of the Hereafter,\" effecting complete exclusion of all else.","content_type":"hadith","topic":"Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Book of Knowledge'","badge":"Hadith"}],"21":[{"arabic":"الله تعالى بين من افترى على الله كذبا وبين من كذب بالحق فقال تعالى ومن أظلم ممن افترى على الله كذبا أو كذب بالحق لما جاءه وقال تعالى فمن أظلم ممن كذب على الله وكذب بالصدق إذ جاءه ومنها الرياء وملاحظة الخلق والجهد في استمالة قلوبهم وصرف وجوههم والرياء هو الداء العضال الذي يدعو إلى أكبر الكبائر كما سيأتي في كتاب الرياء والمناظر لا يقصد إلا الظهور عند الخلق وانطلاق ألسنتهم بالثناء عليه فهذه عشر خصال من أمهات الفواحش الباطنة سوى ما يتفق لغير المتماسكين منهم من الخصام المؤدي إلى الضرب","english":"**QUAD_ANCHORS:**\n- Root: نظر (naẓara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to look, to examine, to consider; al-munāẓarah is mutual examination/argumentation between two parties.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: naẓar as intellectual contemplation (taʾammul bi-l-baṣīrah); munāẓarah as dialectical exchange.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: sitting face-to-face in debate; Metaphorical: disputation for victory.\n- Root: غلب (ghalaba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to overcome, to conquer, to prevail.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ghalabah as seeking to dominate another in argument.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: victory in combat; Metaphorical: intellectual supremacy.\n- Root: حسد (ḥasada)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to envy, to wish removal of blessings from another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: envy as wishing deprivation of blessings from the deserving — among the basest traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: envy of another's blessing; Metaphorical: consuming fire of the soul.\n- Root: كبر (kabura)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be great, to be haughty.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-kibr as self-elevation above others; condemned by God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: greatness; Metaphorical: arrogance and pride.\n- Root: غيب (ghāba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be absent, to be hidden.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ghībah as mentioning another with what he dislikes in his absence — likened to eating carrion.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: backbiting; Metaphorical: consuming the flesh of one's brother.\n- Root: راءى (rāʾā)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to show off, to display.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-riyāʾ as performing acts for the sake of being seen — hidden shirk.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: showing good deeds; Metaphorical: seeking reputation.\n- Root: نفس (nafs)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: self, soul, spirit.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: tazkiyat al-nafs as self-praise and self-purification — forbidden by God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: self; Metaphorical: self-commendation.\n- Root: حقd (ḥaqida)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to bear malice, to harbor resentment.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ḥiqd as nursing enmity in the heart — negated from the believer.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: bearing grudge; Metaphorical: hidden rancor.\n- Root: نفاق (nifāq)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: hypocrisy, showing contrary to what one conceals.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: hypocrisy in deeds — outward display contrary to inward state.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: hypocrisy in faith; Metaphorical: hypocrisy in character.\n- Root: علم (ʿalima)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to know, to perceive.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: knowledge as apprehension of reality — its pursuit for other than God is destructive.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: knowledge; Metaphorical: the dangerous burden of the scholar.\n- Root: جدل (jadala)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to argue, to dispute.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-jidāl as contention; al-mirāʾ as disputation with falsehood — condemned.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: argumentation; Metaphorical: obstinate disputation.\n- Root: شرك (sharaka)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to associate, to share.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-riyāʾ as hidden shirk — associating others with God in intention.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: polytheism; Metaphorical: hidden association in deeds.\n- Root: موت (māta)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to die.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: death as separation of soul from body; spiritual death as ruin of the soul.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical death; Metaphorical: spiritual destruction.\n- Root: حيى (ḥayiya)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to live.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: life as the power of perception; eternal life as the reward of the righteous.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical life; Metaphorical: spiritual vitality.\n- Root: هلك (halaka)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to perish, to be destroyed.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-halāk as ruin; eternal destruction as everlasting punishment.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: destruction; Metaphorical: eternal ruin.\n- Root: رياء (riyāʾ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: showing off, ostentation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: riyāʾ as the incurable disease calling to the greatest sins.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: ostentation; Metaphorical: spiritual disease.\n- Root: جاه (jāh)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: rank, status, prestige.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: love of status (ḥubb al-jāh) among the destructive traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: position; Metaphorical: seeking prestige.\n- Root: مباهاة (mubāhāh)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: mutual boasting, vying in splendor.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: mubāhāh as each seeking to display superiority over the other.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: mutual boasting; Metaphorical: rivalry in honor.\n- Root: مماراة (mumārāh)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: disputation, wrangling.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-mirāʾ as contentious argument — condemned by the Prophet.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: disputation; Metaphorical: obstinate wrangling.\n- Root: فخر (fakhara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to boast, to pride oneself.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-fakhr as self-glorification — among the blameworthy traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: boasting; Metaphorical: vainglory.\n- Root: عجب (ʿajiba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to wonder, to be amazed.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ʿujb as self-admiration — among the destructive traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder; Metaphorical: self-conceit.\n- Root: بغض (baghiḍa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to hate, to detest.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-baghdāʾ as mutual hatred — contrary to brotherhood.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: hatred; Metaphorical: mutual enmity.\n- Root: غضب (ghaḍiba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be angry.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ghaḍab as anger — among the blameworthy traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: anger; Metaphorical: rage.\n- Root: طمع (ṭamiʿa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to covet, to desire.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ṭamaʿ as covetousness — among the destructive traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: desire; Metaphorical: greed.\n- Root: أشر (ashira)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be exultant, to be insolent.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ashar as excessive joy and insolence — blameworthy.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: exultation; Metaphorical: insolence.\n- Root: بطر (baṭira)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be arrogant, to be ungrateful.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-baṭar as arrogance in prosperity — among the destructive traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: arrogance; Metaphorical: ingratitude for blessings.\n- Root: خيلاء (khuyalāʾ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: pride, haughtiness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-khuyalāʾ as pride and conceit — blameworthy.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: pride; Metaphorical: haughty bearing.\n- Root: خشية (khashyah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: fear, awe.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-khashyah as reverential fear — among the traits of the believers.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: fear; Metaphorical: reverential awe.\n- Root: رحمة (raḥmah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: mercy, compassion.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-raḥmah as tenderness of heart — among the traits of the believers.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: mercy; Metaphorical: compassion.\n- Root: غفلة (ghaflah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: heedlessness, inattention.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ghaflah as heedlessness — contrary to vigilance.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: inattention; Metaphorical: spiritual heedlessness.\n- Root: خشوع (khushūʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: humility, submissiveness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-khushūʿ as humility — among the traits of the believers.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: humility; Metaphorical: devotional submission.\n- Root: تذكير (tadhkīr)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: reminding, admonishing.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-tadhkīr as reminding of God — a righteous act.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: reminding; Metaphorical: preaching.\n- Root: وعظ (waʿaẓa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to admonish, to exhort.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-waʿẓ as exhortation to obedience and warning against disobedience.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: admonition; Metaphorical: sermonizing.\n- Root: فتوى (fatwā)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: legal ruling, formal opinion.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-fatwā as clarification of legal rulings.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: legal opinion; Metaphorical: religious authority.\n- Root: قضاء (qaḍāʾ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: judgment, decree.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-qaḍāʾ as judgment between people.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: judgment; Metaphorical: judicial authority.\n- Root: وقف (waqafa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to stop, to stand.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-waqf as endowment of property for charitable purposes.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: standing; Metaphorical: religious endowment.\n- Root: ثواب (thawāb)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: reward, recompense.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-thawāb as divine recompense for righteous deeds.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: reward; Metaphorical: divine recompense.\n- Root: عذاب (ʿadhāb)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: punishment, torment.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ʿadhāb as divine punishment for the disobedient.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: punishment; Metaphorical: divine chastisement.\n- Root: ملك (malaka)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to possess, to rule.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-mulk as sovereignty; al-mulk al-muʾabbad as eternal sovereignty in Paradise.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: dominion; Metaphorical: eternal kingdom.\n- Root: نعيم (naʿīm)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: bliss, delight.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-naʿīm as bliss; al-naʿīm al-sarmad as everlasting bliss.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: delight; Metaphorical: eternal bliss.\n- Root: رخصة (rukhaṣah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: concession, dispensation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-rukhaṣah as legal concession.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: concession; Metaphorical: legal dispensation.\n- Root: رياسة (riyāsah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: leadership, headship.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: love of leadership among the destructive traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: leadership; Metaphorical: seeking authority.\n- Root: اندراس (indirās)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: effacement, obliteration.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: indirās al-ʿulūm as the obliteration of the sciences.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: effacement; Metaphorical: extinction of knowledge.\n- Root: صبيان (ṣibyān)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: boys, children.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: children's motivation for learning through play.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: boys; Metaphorical: pupils.\n- Root: مكتب (maktab)\n  * Lisan / Ayn- Root: مكتب (maktab)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: place of instruction, school for boys.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: the elementary school where children are taught.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: school; Metaphorical: place of elementary learning.\n- Root: خلاق (khilāq)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: portion, share, lot.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: \"no portion\" (lā khilāq) in the Hereafter — utter deprivation of divine reward.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: share; Metaphorical: spiritual allotment in the afterlife.\n- Root: فاجر (fājir)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: wicked, immoral, profligate.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: the dissolute one who commits sins openly.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: sinner; Metaphorical: one through whom God nonetheless supports religion.\n- Root: شمع (shamʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: candle, wax taper.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: the candle as parable — consuming itself while giving light to others.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: candle; Metaphorical: one who destroys himself but benefits others.\n- Root: نار (nār)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: fire.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: fire as parable — consuming itself and all around it.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: fire; Metaphorical: one who destroys himself and others.\n- Root: سعد (saʿida)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be fortunate, to prosper.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: saʿādah as felicity — success in the Hereafter.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: good fortune; Metaphorical: eternal felicity.\n- Root: شقي (shaqiya)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be wretched, to be miserable.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: shaqāwah as wretchedness — ruin in the Hereafter.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: misery; Metaphorical: eternal ruin.\n- Root: أخلص (akhlaṣa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to purify, to be sincere.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: ikhlāṣ as purifying intention for God alone — the condition of acceptance.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: sincerity; Metaphorical: pure devotion.\n- Root: ريب (rāba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to doubt, to suspect.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-rayb as doubt and uncertainty — to be dispelled by evidence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: doubt; Metaphorical: skepticism.","book_title":"Book of Knowledge","section_title_ar":"Section 39","section_title_en":"Section 39","section_index":39,"anchors":"*(Anchors established)*","content_type":"ethics","topic":"Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Book of Knowledge'","badge":"Ethics"}],"25":[{"arabic":"قوله تعالى ومنهم من يستمع إليك حتى إذا خرجوا من عندك قالوا للذين أوتوا العلم ماذا قال آنفا أولئك الذين طبع الله على قلوبهم والطابع هي الموانع التي سنذكرها في موانع الفهم وقد قيل لا يكون المريد مريدا حتى يجد في القرآن كل ما يريد ويعرف منه النقصان من المزيد ويستغني بالمولى عن العبيد السادس التخلي عن موانع الفهم فإن أكثر الناس منعوا عن فهم معاني القرآن لأسباب وحجب أسدلها الشيطان على قلوبهم فعميت عليهم عجائب أسرار القرآن قال صلى الله عليه وسلم لولا أن الشياطين يحومون على","english":"I say: These meanings are buried treasures that are not unveiled except to the divinely aided ones. To this 'Ali — may God be pleased with him — alluded by his saying: \"The Messenger of God — may God's peace and blessings be upon him — did not confide to me anything that he concealed from the people, except that God — mighty and majestic — grants to a servant understanding (fahm) of His Book. So let one be eager in seeking that understanding.\" (1) And Ibn Mas'ud — may God be pleased with him — said: \"Whoever desires the knowledge of the first and the last, let him stir up the Qur'an.\" And the greatest of the sciences of the Qur'an lie beneath the names of God — mighty and majestic — and His attributes, for most of creation have grasped from them only matters befitting their understandings and have not stumbled upon their depths.","book_title":"Etiquette of Quran Recitation","section_title_ar":"Section 228","section_title_en":"The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 228: On the Inner Meanings of the Qur'an and the Removal of Obstacles to Understanding","section_index":228,"anchors":"- Root: فهم (f-h-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Comprehension, discernment, the faculty by which one grasps meanings\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الفهم: هيئة للإنسان بها يتحقق معاني ما يحسن\" — a disposition by which one verifies the realities of what is perceived; distinct from mere hearing or transmission\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: swift apprehension; Metaphorical: the divine gift of insight into hidden meanings, as in the verse {ففهمناها سليمان}\n- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Turning, overturning, transformation from one state to another\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart as that which is perpetually turned and overturned between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical turning; Metaphorical: the heart as the seat of understanding that is veiled or illuminated, rusted or polished\n- Root: سمع (s-m-')\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Hearing, the auditory faculty\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"السمع: قوة في الأذن به يدرك الأصوات\" — extends metaphorically to comprehension and obedience; \"أو ألقى السمع وهو شهيد\" — one who casts his hearing while present\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: auditory perception; Metaphorical: attentive obedience and receptive understanding\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Specification) — governing the precise delimitation of predicates such as \"إنما يتذكر أولوا الألباب\" where restriction confines the action to its proper subject","content_type":"spiritual","topic":"Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Etiquette of Quran Recitation'","badge":"Insight"}],"30":[{"arabic":"وقرئ عند يحيى البكاء {ولو ترى إذ وقفوا على ربهم} فصاح صيحة مكث منها مريضا أربعة أشهر يعاد من أطراف البصرة وقال مالك بن دينار بينما أنا أطوف بالبيت إذا أنا بجويرة متعبدة متعلقة بأستار الكعبة وهي تقول يا رب كم شهوة ذهبت لذاتها وبقيت تبعاتها يا رب أما كان لك أدب وعقوبة إلا النار وتبكى فما زال ذلك مقامها حتى طلع الفجر قال مالك فلما رأيت ذلك وضعت يدي على رأسي صارخا أقول ثكلت مالكا أمه وروي أن الفضيل رؤي يوم عرفة والناس يدعون وهو يبكي بكاء الثكلي المحترقة حتى إذا كادت الشمس تغرب قبض على لحيته ثم رفع","english":"One of them said: \"Would that my mother had never given birth to me.\"","book_title":"Fear and Hope","section_title_ar":"Section 1061","section_title_en":"The States of the Fearful — Accounts of the Companions and Early Saints (Section 1061)","section_index":1061,"anchors":"- Root: خوف (kh-w-f)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of dread and apprehension; the heart's agitation before an anticipated harm.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الخوف: توقع مكروه عن أمارة مظنونة، أو معلومة\" — fear is the expectation of misfortune based on a probable or certain sign; it is the opposite of security (amn) and pertains to both worldly and otherworldly matters.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"خفته على مالي خوفا وخيفة\" — to fear for something. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"تخوفه: تنقصه وأخذ من أطرافه\" — fear that consumes one gradually, as in the verse of Zuhayr where the journey \"wears away\" the camel's hump little by little, as if in dread of it.\n- Root: يوم (y-w-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: A measure of time from sunrise to sunset, or any period whatsoever.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"اليوم يعبر به عن وقت طلوع الشمس إلى غروبها. وقد يعبر به عن مدة من الزمان أي مدة كانت\" — the Day is a term for any span of time; when added to God (أيام الله), it signifies His mighty acts and visitations upon the nations.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: a single day. Metaphorical: \"ذكر في أيام العرب كذا أي في وقائعها\" — \"days\" denotes battles and momentous events; \"أيام الله\" are His punitive calamities upon the disbelievers.\n- Root: حتي (ḥ-t-y)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): A particle of end-point (ghāyah), signifying the terminal limit of an action or state, as in {ليسجننه حتى حين} and {حتى مطلع الفجر}.\n  * Sibawayh Rule: Prepositional and particle interposition (الفصل بين الجار والمجرور) — the particle ḥattā governs what follows as the culminating limit of the preceding narrative, marking the climax of an emotional or physical state (e.g., \"until he fell unconscious,\" \"until he died\").\n- Root: بكى (b-k-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Weeping with tears; the physical manifestation of inner anguish.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Weeping is the outward expression of the heart's grief (ḥuzn); in the fearful, it is the release of the soul's dread before God's majesty.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: shedding tears. Metaphorical: \"بكاء الثكلي\" — the weeping of a bereaved mother, intensified by burning loss, used here to describe the saints' weeping on the Day of Arafah.","content_type":"spiritual","topic":"Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Fear and Hope'","badge":"Insight"}],"38":[{"arabic":"وقال أبو هريرة في قوله عز وجل {وما من دابة في الأرض ولا طائر يطير بجناحيه إلا أمم أمثالكم} أنه يحشر الخلق كلهم يوم القيامة البهائم والدواب والطير وكل شىء فيبلغ من عدل الله تعالى أن يأخذ للجماء من القرناء ثم يقول كونى ترابا فذلك حين يقول الكافر يا ليتني كنت ترابا فكنت أنت يا مسكين فى يوم ترى صحيفتك خالية عن حسنات طال فيها تعبك فتقول أين حسناتى فيقال نقلت إلى صحيفة خصمائك وترى صحيفتك مشحونة بسيئات طال فى الصبر عنها نصبك واشتد بسبب الكف عنها عناؤك فتقول يا رب هذه سيئات ما قارفتها قط فيقال هذه سيئات القوم الذين اغتبتهم وشتمتهم وقصدتهم بالسو","english":"Know that none is saved from the peril of the Scale except him who, in this world, called his own soul to account, and weighed therein — by the scale of the Sacred Law — his deeds, his words, his passing thoughts, and his glances, just as ʿUmar — may God be well pleased with him — said: \"Call yourselves to account before you are called to account, and weigh yourselves before you are weighed.\" Indeed, his calling himself to account consists in his repenting, before death, from every act of disobedience with a sincere repentance (tawbah naṣūḥ), and in his making up for what was neglected of his shortcomings regarding the obligatory commands of God — may He be exalted — and in his restoring rights to those he wronged, grain by grain, and in his seeking absolution from everyone whom he harmed by his tongue, his hand, or his evil suspicion harbored in his heart — and this by winning their hearts' contentment — until he dies with no outstanding wrong against him, nor any neglected obligation.","book_title":"Remembrance of Death and Afterlife","section_title_ar":"Section 1343","section_title_en":"The Perils of the Scale and the Reckoning of Wrongs Against Others","section_index":1343,"anchors":"- Root: وزن (w-z-n)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is weighing, measuring, and balancing; al-mīzān (the Scale) is the instrument of equitable judgment.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological context, al-mīzān is the divine instrument of justice on the Day of Resurrection, weighing deeds to manifest equitable recompense, not a corporeal balance.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) usage denotes physical weighing; metaphorical (majāz) usage extends to moral and spiritual accounting, as in \"weighing one's deeds by the scale of the Sacred Law.\"\n- Sibawayh Rule: Conditional and Exceptive Constructions (الشّرط والاستثناء) — governing the protasis-apodosis structure in \"whoever's scales are heavy... whoever's scales are light,\" and the exceptive force in \"none is saved... except he who reckons.\"","content_type":"afterlife","topic":"Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'","badge":"Afterlife"}],"41":[{"arabic":"{وقال} فيكشف ما تدعون إليه إن شاء {وقال} يرزق من يشاء بغير حساب {وقال} ويغفر ما دون ذلك لمن يشاء {وقال} ويتوب الله على من يشاء وهو خلق من أخلاق الربوبية إذ قال تعالى والله شكور حليم وقد جعل الله الشكر مفتاح كلام أهل الجنة فقال تعالى وقالوا الحمد لله الذي صدقناه وعده {وقال} وآخر دعواهم أن الحمد لله رب العالمين","english":"So then, what we have mentioned concerning the treatment of patience (ṣabr) regarding whispered insinuation (waswās), regarding lust (shahwah), and regarding status (jāh) — annex this to what we have already set down concerning the canons of the paths of spiritual striving (mujāhadah) in the Book of the Discipline of the Soul (Kitāb Riyāḍat al-Nafs) from the Quarter of the Destroyers (rubʿ al-muhlikāt). Adopt it as your manual (dastūr) by which you may come to know the treatment of patience in all the categories which we have previously detailed; for the detailing of individual cases is prolix. Whoever observes gradual progression (tadrīj), patience will elevate him to a state wherein patience without it becomes as arduous as it once was with it — so his affairs become inverted, and what was once beloved to him becomes loathed, and what was once detested to him becomes a sweet, wholesome draught from which he cannot bear to be patient [in abstaining]. This is known only through experience (tajribah) and tasting (dhawq). It has an analogue in customary habits: for a boy is compelled to learning in the beginning by force, and so patience with play and patience with knowledge are both arduous for him — until, when his inner sight is opened and he finds intimacy with knowledge, the matter is inverted, and it becomes arduous for him to be patient in abstaining from knowledge, and to endure play. To this points what is narrated of one of the knowers (ʿārifīn) that he asked al-Shiblī: \"Patience — which of its kinds is the most severe?\" He said: \"Patience in God (fī Allāh).\" He said: \"No.\" He said: \"Patience for God (li-Allāh).\" He said: \"No.\" He said: \"Patience with God (maʿa Allāh).\" He said: \"No.\" He said: \"Then what?\" He said: \"Patience from God (ʿan Allāh).\" At that al-Shiblī cried out a cry by which his soul nearly perished. And it has been said concerning the meaning of His saying, the Exalted: {«اصبروا وصابروا ورابطوا»} \"Be patient, and vie in patience, and be steadfast\" (Q. 3:200): \"Be patient in God, vie in patience by God, and be steadfast with God.\" And it was said: \"Patience for God is sufficiency (ghināʾ); patience by God is endurance (baqāʾ); patience with God is fidelity (wafāʾ); and patience from God is abandonment (jafāʾ).\" And it was said concerning its meaning:","book_title":"Patience and Gratitude","section_title_ar":"Section 972","section_title_en":"The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 972: On Patience (al-Sabr) and the Commencement of Gratitude (al-Shukr)","section_index":972,"anchors":"- Root: صبر (ṣ-b-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of restraint and withholding; al-Khalil notes its application to holding back an animal from fodder, and to enduring constraint.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الصبر: الإمساك في ضيق\" — the withholding of the self in constriction; it is a general term whose specific names (shajāʿah, ḥilm, etc.) vary by context, and its opposite is al-jazaʿ (panic/vexation).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"صبرت على ما أكره. وصبرت عما أحب\" — enduring what is disliked and restraining from what is loved. Metaphorical (majāz): \"صبرت يمينه\" — binding an oath strenuously; \"أصبر على الضرب من الأرض\" — more enduring than the earth.\n- Root: شكر (sh-k-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes manifestation and fullness; al-Ayn links it to an overflowing udder (shukrā).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الشكر: تصور النعمة وإظهارها\" — the perception of a blessing and its display; it is the opposite of kufr (covering/denial). It has three degrees: of the heart (perception), of the tongue (praise), and of the limbs (requital).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"شكرت لله تعالى نعمته\" — thanking God for His grace. Metaphorical: \"دابة شكور\" — a beast that thrives on little fodder, displaying its owner's care through its fatness.\n- Root: علم (ʿ-l-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Knowledge as the apprehension of a thing's reality; al-Ayn treats it under the entry of umm (source/origin), indicating that knowledge is the root to which all else is annexed.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العلم: إدراك الشيء بحقيقته\" — apprehension of a thing by its true reality, either by grasping its essence or by affirming/denying a predicate of it.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"ما علمت بخبرك: ما شعرت به\" — to be aware/perceive. It is also used for the landmarks (maʿālim) of the road, i.e., traces by which one is guided.\n- Root: وقد (w-q-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The kindling and blazing of fire; al-Ayn records it under the entry of w-q-d with the sense of fuel and flame.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"وقدت النار تقد وقودا ووقدا\" — the fire blazed; al-wuqūd is both the fuel and the resulting flame, as in {«وقودها الناس والحجارة»} (Q. 2:24).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"وقدت النار وقودا ووقدا، واتقدت وتوقدت\" — the fire blazed and blazed fiercely. Metaphorical: \"قلب وقاد\" — a blazing, keen heart; \"وقدة الصيف\" — the blazing heat of summer.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Interposition between the operative word and its object (al-faṣl bayna al-ʿāmil wa al-maʿmūl fīhi) — governing the placement of circumstantial qualifiers (ḥāl) and prepositional phrases to preserve the intended semantic restriction, as in the verse \"اصبروا وصابروا ورابطوا\" where each imperative carries a distinct operative scope.","content_type":"spiritual","topic":"Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Patience and Gratitude'","badge":"Insight"}],"44":[{"arabic":"فتحنا عليهم أبواب كل شيء حتى إذا فرحوا بما أوتوا أخذناهم بغتة فإذا هم مبلسون وفي تفسير قوله تعالى سنستدرجهم من حيث لا يعلمون أنهم كلما أحدثوا ذنبا أحدثنا لهم نعمة ليزيد غرورهم وقال تعالى إنما نملي لهم ليزدادوا إثما وقال تعالى ولا تحسبن الله غافلا عما يعمل الظالمون إنما يؤخرهم ليوم تشخص فيه الأبصار إلى غير ذلك مما ورد في كتاب الله تعالى وسنة رسوله فمن آمن به تخلص من هذا الغرور فإن منشأ هذا الغرور الجهل بالله وبصفاته فإن من عرفه لا يأمن مكره ولا يغتر بأمثال هذه الخيالات الفاسدة وينظر إلى فرعون وها","english":"As for knowing it by way of authoritative transmission and affirmation (taṣdīq): it is that one believes in the Book of God — exalted is He — and affirms His Messenger. For God — exalted is He — has said: {«أيحسبون أن ما نمدهم به من مال وبنين * نسارع لهم في الخيرات بل لا يشعرون»} — \"Do they reckon that what We extend to them of wealth and sons — [that] We hasten for them good things? Nay, but they do not perceive\" (Q. 23:55-56). And He said — exalted is He: {«سنستدرجهم من حيث لا يعلمون»} — \"We shall draw them on by degrees from whence they do not know\" (Q. 7:182). And He said — exalted is He: {«فتحنا عليهم أبواب كل شيء حتى إذا فرحوا بما أوتوا أخذناهم بغتة فإذا هم مبلسون»} — \"We opened upon them the gates of everything, until when they rejoiced in what they were given, We seized them suddenly, and behold, they were in despair\" (Q. 6:44). And in the interpretation of His saying — exalted is He: {«سنستدرجهم من حيث لا يعلمون»} — it is said: \"Whenever they innovated a sin, We innovated for them a blessing, to increase their self-deception.\" And He said — exalted is He: {«إنما نملي لهم ليزدادوا إثما»} — \"We only grant them respite that they may increase in sin\" (Q. 3:178). And He said — exalted is He: {«ولا تحسبن الله غافلا عما يعمل الظالمون إنما يؤخرهم ليوم تشخص فيه الأبصار»} — \"And do not reckon God heedless of what the wrongdoers do; He only delays them to a Day when eyes will stare fixedly\" (Q. 14:42). And [there is] more of that kind which has come in the Book of God — exalted is He — and in the Sunnah of His Messenger. So whoever believes in it is delivered from this self-deception. For the origin of this self-deception is ignorance of God and of His attributes; for whoever knows Him does not feel secure from His stratagem (makr) and is not deceived by such corrupt fancies.","book_title":"Patience and Gratitude","section_title_ar":"Section 888","section_title_en":"Section 888 — On the Remedy for Self-Deception (Ghurūr): Recognizing the Signs of Divine Honor (Karāmah) and Humiliation (Hawān)","section_index":888,"anchors":"- Root: غرر (gh-r-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of an outward trace or mark; heedlessness in wakefulness; the blaze on a horse's forehead.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Ghurūr\" is delusion stemming from heedlessness — the soul's being overtaken while imagining security, as in {«ما غرك بربك الكريم»} (Q. 82:6). It is the state of one who is deceived by apparent favors while blind to their underlying test.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): heedlessness and exposure to predation, like a gazelle deceived by moonlight. Metaphorical (Majaz): being \"deceived by God\" (ightirār billāh) — trusting in His mercy while persisting in disobedience.\n- Root: لهم (l-h-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Swallowing whole; the gullet.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Ilhām\" — God's casting a meaning into the heart (al-raw'), distinct from demonic whispering; the channel of inner vision for the knowers (ʿārifūn).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to swallow. Metaphorical: the heart's receptive \"swallowing\" of divine inspiration.\n- Root: حدث (ḥ-d-th)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Origination after non-existence; newness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Ḥudūth\" — a thing's coming to be after it was not; \"muḥdith\" — the originator, exclusively God for substances (jawāhir). \"Iḥdāth\" — bringing about a novel state or act.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: temporal newness. Metaphorical: \"muḥaddath\" — one inspired with correct discernment, as if newly spoken-to.\n- Root: نعم (n-ʿ-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Softness, ease, well-being; a state of comfort.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Niʿmah\" — the good state one inhabits; \"naʿīm\" — the act of living pleasantly. Blessings are both a test and a sign, per {«وإن تعدوا نعمة الله لا تحصوها»} (Q. 16:18).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: bounty and comfort. Metaphorical: \"khafat naʿāmatuhum\" — their prosperity vanished; blessings may signal either favor or gradual ruin (istidrāj).\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction and Focused Predication with \"innamā\")\n  * In {«إنما نملي لهم ليزدادوا إثما»} (Q. 3:178) and {«إنما يؤخرهم ليوم تشخص فيه الأبصار»} (Q. 14:42), \"innamā\" restricts the purpose of divine respite exclusively to increase of sin and deferred reckoning — negating any reading of it as benevolent indulgence.","content_type":"dhikr","topic":"Al-Ghazali on constant remembrance of God and contemplative invocations, from 'Patience and Gratitude'","badge":"Dhikr"},{"arabic":"فلما نسوا ما ذكروا به أنجينا الذين ينهون عن السوء وأخذنا الذين ظلموا بعذاب بئيس بما كانوا يفسقون فبين أنهم استفادوا النجاة بالنهي عن السوء ويدل ذلك على الوجوب أيضا وقال تعالى الذين إن مكناهم في الأرض أقاموا الصلاة وآتوا الزكاة وأمروا بالمعروف ونهوا عن المنكر فقرن ذلك بالصلاة والزكاة في نعت الصالحين والمؤمنين وقال تعالى وتعاونوا على البر والتقوى ولا تعاونوا على الإثم والعدوان وهو أمر جزم ومعنى التعاون الحث عليه وتسهيل طرق الخير وسد سبل الشر والعدوان بحسب الإمكان وقال تعالى لولا ينهاهم الربانيون و","english":"Know that the verses indicating the obligation of commanding right and forbidding wrong are numerous. Among them is His saying, may He be exalted: {«Let there arise from among you a nation inviting to good, commanding right and forbidding wrong — and those are the successful»} (Āl ʿImrān 3:104). In this verse is the declaration of obligation, for His saying, may He be exalted, \"Let there be\" (wal-takun) is a command, and the apparent meaning of a command is obligation. In it also is the declaration that success (falāḥ) is contingent upon it, for He restricted it and said: {«And those are the successful»}. In it also is the declaration that it is a collective obligation (farḍ kifāyah), not an individual obligation (farḍ ʿayn), and that when a group undertakes it, the obligation falls from the rest — for He did not say, \"Let all of you be commanders of right,\" but rather said: {«Let there arise from among you a nation»}. Thus, whenever one person or a group undertakes it, the burden falls from the others, and success is particularized to those who undertake it and directly perform it. But if all of creation were to hold back from it, the burden would encompass all those capable of it without exception.","book_title":"Patience and Gratitude","section_title_ar":"Section 521","section_title_en":"Section 521","section_index":521,"anchors":"*(Anchors established)*","content_type":"spiritual","topic":"Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Patience and Gratitude'","badge":"Insight"},{"arabic":"ذكروا به فتحنا عليهم أبواب كل شيء (3) يعني لما تركوا ما أمروا به فتحنا عليهم أبواب الخير حتى إذا فرحوا بما أوتوا أي بما أعطوا من الخير أخذناهم بغتة وعن الحسن البصري رحمه الله أن رجلا من الصحابة رضي الله عنهم رأى امرأة كان يعرفها في الجاهلية فكلمها ثم تركها فجعل الرجل يلتفت إليها وهو يمشي فصدمه حائط فأثر في وجهه فأتى النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم فأخبره فقال صلى الله عليه وسلم إذا أراد الله بعبد خيرا عجل له عقوبة ذنبه في الدنيا (4) وقال علي كرم الله وجهه ألا أخبركم بأرجى آية في","english":"And it is related from al-Hasan al-Basri (may God have mercy on him) that a man among the Companions (may God be pleased with them) saw a woman he had known in the pre-Islamic period of ignorance. He spoke to her, then left her, but kept turning his gaze back toward her as he walked, until a wall struck him and left a mark on his face. He came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) and informed him of this. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"When God desires good for a servant, He hastens the punishment of his sin for him in this world.\"","book_title":"Patience and Gratitude","section_title_ar":"Section 1021","section_title_en":"On the Divine Wisdom of Trials and the Patience of the Prophets and Righteous","section_index":1021,"anchors":"- Root: فتح (f-t-ḥ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To open, to unlock, to grant access; also victory and judgment.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In Quranic usage, opening \"the doors of all things\" signifies God’s granting expansive worldly means as a test, not necessarily as honor.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) for physical opening; metaphorical (majāz) here for divine facilitation of blessings that lead to heedlessness.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Interposition of circumstantial clauses (ḥāl) — the parenthetical \"يعني\" (meaning) functions as an explanatory aside, permissible between subject and predicate for clarity.","content_type":"hadith","topic":"Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Patience and Gratitude'","badge":"Hadith"}],"45":[{"arabic":"كتاب أسرار الصوم والحمد لله رب العالمين وصلى الله على سيدنا محمد وعلى جميع الأنبياء والمرسلين وعلى الملائكة والمقربين من أهل السموات والأرضين وعلى آله وصحبه وسلم تسليما كثيرا دائما إلى يوم الدين والحمد لله وحده وحسبنا الله ونعم الوكيل","english":"Ibrāhīm al-Khawwāṣ, al-Junayd, and a group [of the pious] held the view that taking from voluntary charity (ṣadaqah) is superior. For in taking the obligatory alms (zakāh), there is a jostling with the poor and a constriction upon them. And also because perhaps one's attribute of eligibility (ṣifat al-istihqāq) is not fully complete in his taking it, as it was described in the Mighty Book {«إِنَّمَا الصَّدَقَاتُ لِلْفُقَرَاءِ وَالْمَسَاكِينِ»} (Verily the alms are for the poor and the needy). As for voluntary charity (ṣadaqah), the matter therein is more expansive.","book_title":"On Invocations and Supplications","section_title_ar":"Section 184","section_title_en":"Section 184 — The Book of the Secrets of Almsgiving (Zakat): On the Concealment and Open Display of Charity, and the Superiority of Taking from Voluntary Charity (Sadaqah) versus Obligatory Alms (Zakat)","section_index":184,"anchors":"- Root: صدق (ṣ-d-q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes truthfulness and veracity in speech and action; the voluntary charity (ṣadaqah) is so named because it is a truthful manifestation of one's faith and sincerity.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الصدق والكذب أصلهما في القول... ولا يكونان بالقصد الأول إلا في القول\" — Truth and falsehood are originally in speech; ṣadaqah (charity) is that which verifies and proves the truthfulness of one's claim to faith.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"صدقته الحديث\" — truthful in speech. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"رجل صادق الحملة\" — one who is true/steadfast in battle-charge; thus ṣadaqah is that which truthfully manifests inner conviction through outward action.\n- Root: زكا (z-k-w)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"زكا الزرع يزكو: ازداد ونماء\" — growth and increase; purification.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"أصل الزكاة: النمو الحاصل عن بركة الله تعالى... أو لتزكية النفس، أي: تنميتها\" — The root of zakāh is growth from divine blessing, or the purification/growth of the soul.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Zakāh is the obligatory alms that purifies wealth and the soul; its taking is a legal right (ḥaqq wājib), not a favor.\n- Root: وجب (w-j-b)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجوب: الثبوت\" — Obligation is fixedness/establishment. \"واجب من جهة الشرع كوجوب العب\" — Obligatory by divine law (Sharʿ).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"وجب البيع، وأوجبته\" — the sale became binding. \"استوجب العقاب\" — he merited punishment.\n  * Lisan al-Arab: \"ومُوجِبٌ: من أَسماءِ الـمُحَرَّم\" — from the names of the sacred/preclusive.\n- Root: حمد (ḥ-m-d)\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): Praise (ḥamd) is the acknowledgment of beneficence with veneration; the Book of Secrets of Fasting opens with praise to God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"أحمد الله تعالى بجميع محامده\" — I praise God with all His praises. Metaphorical: \"أحمدت الأرض: رضيت سكناها\" — I found the land praiseworthy (approved its dwelling).\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The phrase \"إِنَّمَا\" restricts predication, as in the legal maxim that zakāh is exclusively a right of God (ḥaqq wājib lillāh), not a discretionary favor of the giver.","content_type":"dua","topic":"Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'On Invocations and Supplications'","badge":"Du'a"},{"arabic":"ونصفها لعبدي ولعبدي ما سأل (1) يقول العبد الحمد لله رب العالمين فيقول الله عز وجل حمدني عبدي وأثنى علي وهو معنى قوله سمع الله لمن حمده الحديث الخ فلو لم يكن لك من صلاتك حظ سوى ذكر الله لك في جلاله وعظمته فناهيك بذلك غنيمة فكيف بما ترجوه من ثوابه وفضله وكذلك ينبغي أن تفهم ما تقرؤه من السور كما سيأتي في كتاب تلاوة القرآن فلا تغفل عن أمره ونهيه ووعده ووعيده ومواعظه وأخبار أنبيائه وذكر مننه وإحسانه ولكل واحد حق فالرجاء حق الوعد والخوف حق الوعيد والعزم حق الأمر والنهي والاتعاظ حق الموعظة والشكر حق ذكر المنة والاعتبا","english":"For this reason, he, peace be upon him, said: \"Give us rest, O Bilāl\" (1) — meaning, give us rest through it (the prayer) and through the call to it, for his delight, peace be upon him, was in it.","book_title":"Remembrance of Death and Afterlife","section_title_ar":"Section 138","section_title_en":"The Inner Works of Prayer: Unveiling the Heart's Secrets in the Ritual Prayer (Asrār al-Ṣalāh)","section_index":138,"anchors":"- Root: قلب (Q-L-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To turn a thing over, to revolve it, to divert it from one state to another; the heart as that which is constantly turning and fluctuating.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — The heart is that which is turned and diverted; its rectitude lies in its orientation towards God, its ruin in its turning towards other than Him.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" (to turn a thing from its face). Metaphorical (Majaz): \"قلب المعلم الصبيان: صرفهم إلى بيوتهم\" (the teacher turned the boys towards their homes) — thus the heart's turning is its direction and intent.\n- Root: وجه (W-J-H)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The face; the foremost, noblest, and most apparent part of a thing.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"ولما كان الوجه أول ما يستقبلك، وأشرف ما في ظاهر البدن استعمل في مستقبل كل شيء، وفي أشرفه ومبدئه\" — The face is used metaphorically for the essence and the direction of a thing; the \"face of the heart\" is its intention and aspiration.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"واجهته مواجهة ووجاها\" (to face one another). Metaphorical: \"هذا وجه الثوب. ووجه القوم، وهؤلاء وجوه البلد\" (the face of a garment, the chiefs of a people) — thus the \"face\" of the heart is its foremost orientation.\n- Root: وجل (W-J-L)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: A trembling of the heart from fear or awe.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — The sensing of fear; the heart's tremor before the majesty of the Divine.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال، وقد وجل وجلا\" — A man trembling with fear.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The particle \"innamā\" restricts and confines the predicate to the subject alone, negating it from all else. This is crucial for understanding \"إنما هذه الظواهر تحريكات للبواطن\" — the outward acts are *only* movements to stir the inward realities, nothing more.","content_type":"spiritual","topic":"Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'","badge":"Insight"}],"46":[{"arabic":"رزقك بالمغازل أرأيت إن أخذ الله سمعك وبصرك الرزق على من فوقع ذلك في قلبه فأخرج آلة المغازل من يده وتركها وقيل تركها لما نوهت باسمه وقصد لأجلها وقيل فعل ذلك لما مات عياله كما كان لسفيان خمسون دينارا يتجر فيها فلما مات عياله فرقها فإن قلت فكيف يتصور أن يكون له بضاعة ولا يسكن إليها وهو يعلم أن الكسب بغير بضاعة لا يمكن فأقول بأن يعلم أن الذين يرزقهم الله تعالى بغير بضاعة فيهم كثرة وأن الذين كثرت بضاعتهم فسرقت وهلكت فيهم كثرة وأن يوطن نفسه على أن الله لا يفعل به إلا ما فيه صلاحه فإن أهلك","english":"If you say: \"What is more excellent — that a man sit in his house, or go out and earn?\" — Know that if he is able to devote himself fully, by abandoning earning, to reflection, remembrance (dhikr), sincerity (ikhlāṣ), and the complete occupation of his time with worship, and earning would disturb that for him — and while he is in this state, his soul does not yearn expectantly toward people, waiting for someone to enter upon him and bring him something — but rather he is strong-hearted in patience and reliance upon God most High — then the intended goal (al-maqṣūd) is more appropriate for him. But if his heart is agitated in the house and he yearns expectantly toward people, then earning is more appropriate, because the heart's yearning toward people is a request made by the heart, and abandoning that is more important than abandoning earning.","book_title":"Monotheism and Reliance","section_title_ar":"Section 1125","section_title_en":"On the Superiority of Earning vs. Seclusion, and the Signs of True Reliance (Tawakkul)","section_index":1125,"anchors":"- Root: كسب (Kasaba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is seeking provision and acquiring benefit through effort; \"الكسب: طلب الرزق\" (earning is the seeking of sustenance).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Kasb is what one pursues for gain, applicable to both self and others; iktisāb is specifically for oneself. Al-Zamakhshari notes the literal sense is acquisition of wealth, while the metaphorical usage distinguishes \"كسبت خيرا واكتسبت شرا\" (earned good, acquired evil) — aligning with Ghazali's distinction between lawful earning and covetous accumulation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) — earning through trade or craft; Metaphorical (Majaz) — earning spiritual merit or blameworthy attachment to means.\n- Root: قلب (Qalaba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To turn, overturn, or transform a thing from one state to another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the turning of a thing from one aspect to another; hence the heart (qalb) is so named for its constant fluctuation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — physical overturning; Metaphorical — \"قلب المعلم الصبيان\" (the teacher dismissed the boys), and \"رجل قلب\" (a man who turns matters over, i.e., discerning). Here, Ghazali uses \"قلبه\" (his heart) and \"يطمئن\" (is tranquil) to denote the inner disposition of reliance versus agitation.\n- Root: سكن (Sakana)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To be still, tranquil, or dwell; the opposite of agitation (iḍṭirāb).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Sakīnah is divine tranquility; sukūn denotes the absence of restless motion, both physical and spiritual.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — physical stillness; Metaphorical — the heart's repose in God, as opposed to \"استشراف\" (yearning/looking expectantly toward creatures).\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The rule permits separation between a governing verb and its governed prepositional phrase for rhetorical emphasis, as in Ghazali's periodic constructions like \"فإن قلت فما الأفضل أن يقعد في بيته أو يخرج ويكتسب\" where the conditional structure delays the apodosis for dramatic effect.","content_type":"spiritual","topic":"Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Monotheism and Reliance'","badge":"Insight"}],"52":[{"arabic":"{فسبحان الله حين تمسون وحين تصبحون} وقال تعالى {ولا تطرد الذين يدعون ربهم بالغداة والعشي يريدون وجهه} فهذا كله يبين لك أن الطريق إلى تعالى مراقبة الأوقات وعمارتها بالأوراد على سبيل الدوام ولذلك قال صلى الله عليه وسلم أحب عباد الله إلى الله الذين يراعون الشمس والقمر والأظلة لذكر الله تعالى (1) وقد قال تعالى {الشمس والقمر بحسبان} وقال تعالى {ألم تر إلى ربك كيف مد الظل ولو شاء لجعله ساكنا ثم جعلنا الشمس عليه دليلا ثم قبضناه إلينا قبضا يسيرا} وقال تعالى {والقمر قدرناه منازل} وقال تعالى {وهو الذي جعل لكم النجوم لتهتدوا بها في ظلمات البر والبحر} ف","english":"{«آناء الليل ساجدا وقائما يحذر الآخرة ويرجو رحمة ربه قل هل يستوي الذين يعلمون والذين لا يعلمون»} — \"During the hours of the night, prostrating and standing, fearing the Hereafter and hoping for the mercy of his Lord. Say: Are those who know equal to those who do not know?\" (Q. 39:9). And God Most High said: {«تتجافى جنوبهم عن المضاجع يدعون ربهم خوفا وطمعا»} — \"Their sides forsake their beds, calling upon their Lord in fear and hope\" (Q. 32:16). And God, Mighty and Majestic, said: {«والذين يبيتون لربهم سجدا وقياما»} — \"And those who pass the night prostrating and standing before their Lord\" (Q. 25:64). And God, Mighty and Majestic, said: {«كانوا قليلا من الليل ما يهجعون وبالأسحار هم يستغفرون»} — \"They used to sleep but little of the night, and in the dawn-hours they would seek forgiveness\" (Q. 51:17-18). And God, Mighty and Majestic, said: {«فسبحان الله حين تمسون وحين تصبحون»} — \"So glory be to God when you enter the evening and when you enter the morning\" (Q. 30:17). And God Most High said: {«ولا تطرد الذين يدعون ربهم بالغداة والعشي يريدون وجهه»} — \"And do not drive away those who call upon their Lord morning and evening, desiring His Face\" (Q. 6:52). All of this makes clear to you that the path to God Most High is the watchfulness over the times (murāqabat al-awqāt) and the cultivation of them with the appointed devotions (al-awrād) in a perpetual manner.","book_title":"Arrangement of Litanies","section_title_ar":"Section 258","section_title_en":"The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 258: The Virtues of the Watches (Awrād) and Their Ordering","section_index":258,"anchors":"- Root: ورد (w-r-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is \"to arrive at water\" — the quenching of thirst at a watering place; al-Wird (pl. Awrād) denotes a set portion or appointed round of devotional recitation, by extension from the regular \"arrival\" at a source.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-Wurūd is originally \"the intending of water,\" then used for other things. The theological nuance: the awrād are the appointed \"watering places\" of the soul — fixed times at which the servant presents himself before God to drink from the fountain of remembrance (dhikr).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"warda al-mā'a\" — to arrive at water. Metaphorical (Majāz): \"warada ʿalayhi amrun lam yuṭiqhu\" — an affair he could not bear arrived upon him; \"waradat-hu al-ḥummā\" — fever came upon him. Here, the awrād are the appointed times that \"come upon\" the servant, demanding their due of devotion.\n- Root: طلع (ṭ-l-ʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To rise, to appear above the horizon (of the sun); to ascend; to come upon suddenly.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Ṭalaʿa al-shams ṭulūʿan wa-maṭlaʿan\" — the sun rose. From this is borrowed: \"ṭalaʿa ʿalaynā fulān\" — so-and-so came upon us. The maṭlaʿ is the place of rising.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"ṭalaʿat al-shams\" — the sun rose. Metaphorical: \"ṭalaʿa ʿalaynā fulān\" — he descended upon us suddenly. The \"rising of the sun\" is the boundary-marker of the first watch.\n- Root: ليل (l-y-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The night — from the time of sunset to sunrise; its root suggests \"enveloping\" and \"concealing.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The night (al-layl) and the day (al-nahār) are two signs (āyatān) of God; the night is for stillness and the day for seeking provision.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the period of darkness. Metaphorical: \"layl al-hamm\" — the night of anxiety (a dark affair). The night is divided into watches for the one who stands in prayer.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Bāb al-ḥaṣr wa-al-taqyīd bi-innamā (Restriction and Focused Predication) — The particle innamā restricts the predicate to the subject alone, negating all else. Applied here: \"the purpose of the sun and moon's course is not for worldly matters, but only (innamā) for knowing the measures of the times.\"","content_type":"dhikr","topic":"Al-Ghazali on constant remembrance of God and contemplative invocations, from 'Arrangement of Litanies'","badge":"Dhikr"},{"arabic":"عليك من حسابهم} وقال تعالى {واصبر نفسك مع الذين يدعون ربهم بالغداة والعشي يريدون وجهه ولا تعد عيناك عنهم تريد زينة الحياة الدنيا} // حديث قالت قريش لرسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم كيف نجلس إليك وعندك هؤلاء الحديث في نزول قوله تعالى {ولا تطرد الذين يدعون ربهم} أخرجه مسلم من حديث سعد بن أبي وقاص إلا أنه قال فقال المشركون وقال ابن ماجه قالت قريش ثم أخبر الله تعالى عن تعجبهم حين دخلوا جهنم إذا لم يروا الذين ازدروهم فقالوا {ما لنا لا نرى رجالا كنا نعدهم من الأشرار} قيل يعنون","english":"Know that the one against whom pride is directed is either God—mighty and majestic—or His messengers, or the rest of His creation. Man has been created unjust and ignorant {«وخلق الإنسان ظلوما جهولا»}, so at times he grows proud against the creation, and at times he grows proud against the Creator. Thus, pride, considered with respect to the one against whom it is directed, falls into three divisions.","book_title":"Condemnation of Pride and Conceit","section_title_ar":"Section 855","section_title_en":"Exposition of the One Against Whom Pride Is Directed, Its Degrees, Its Divisions, and the Fruits of Pride Within It","section_index":855,"anchors":"- Root: كبر (k-b-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes magnitude, whether physical size, age, or rank; \"الكبرة\" specifically marks the state of advanced age or an ingrained, chronic condition.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الكبير والصغير من الأسماء المتضايفة\" — bigness and smallness are relative terms. In the theological context, pride (الكبر) is the soul's self-magnification against another, a relational vice that distorts the creature's standing before the Creator.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage concerns physical or quantitative magnitude. Metaphorical (Majaz) usage transfers this to moral and spiritual stature, as in \"كبر علي ذلك\" (it weighed heavily upon him) — here, the soul's swelling against divine command is a metaphorical arrogation of greatness.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Prepositional Interposition (باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور) — Governing the syntactic tension between the agent of pride (المتكبر) and its object (المتكبر عليه), where the preposition \"على\" carries the full weight of opposition and elevation, structuring the periodic sentences that follow.","content_type":"hadith","topic":"Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Condemnation of Pride and Conceit'","badge":"Hadith"}],"53":[{"arabic":"قول قريش أهؤلاء من الله عليهم من بيننا كالاستحقار لهم والأنفة منهم السبب الرابع التعجب كما أخبر الله تعالى عن الأمم السالفة إذ قالوا ما أنتم إلا بشر مثلنا وقالوا أنؤمن لبشرين مثلنا ولئن أطعتم بشرا مثلكم إنكم إذا لخاسرون فتعجبوا من أن يفوز برتبة الرسالة والوحي والقرب من الله تعالى بشر مثلهم فحسدوهم وأحبوا زوال النبوة عنهم جزعا أن يفضل عليهم من هو مثلهم في الخلقة لا عن قصد تكبر وطلب رياسة وتقدم عداوة أو سبب آخر من سائر الأسباب وقالوا متعجبين أبعث الله بشرا رسولا {وقالوا} لولا أنزل علينا ا","english":"I say: God is Knower of what is in the breasts. {«إِنْ تَمْسَسْكُمْ حَسَنَةٌ تَسُؤْهُمْ»} — \"If good touches you, it grieves them\" {«وَدُّوا مَا عَنِتُّمْ قَدْ بَدَتِ الْبَغْضَاءُ مِنْ أَفْوَاهِهِمْ وَمَا تُخْفِي صُدُورُهُمْ أَكْبَرُ»} — \"They wish for your hardship; hatred has already appeared from their mouths, and what their breasts conceal is greater.\" And envy (ḥasad), arising from hatred, may lead to contention, mutual fighting, and the spending of one's entire life in removing another's blessing through stratagems, slander, the tearing of veils, and whatever runs in that course.","book_title":"Condemnation of Envy","section_title_ar":"Section 722","section_title_en":"The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Book on the Rebuke of the Self: The Seven Causes of Envy (Section 722)","section_index":722,"anchors":"- Root: سبب (sabab)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The rope by which one climbs; every means by which one reaches a thing.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Every means by which one arrives at something is called a sabab\" — thus the causes enumerated here are the psychological \"ropes\" by which envy ascends in the soul.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: a physical cord. Metaphorical: any linkage or occasion — here, the inward occasions (asbāb) that give rise to ḥasad.\n- Root: بغض (bughḍ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Hatred, aversion of the heart.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The opposite of love (maḥabbah); a disposition of the heart toward another's harm.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: inner aversion. Metaphorical: its \"appearance from the mouths\" (Quranic) as outward speech betraying the concealed hatred.\n- Root: كبر (kibr)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Greatness; pride; disdain.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-kabīr and al-ṣaghīr are relative terms\" — pride is the claim to greatness relative to another, deeming oneself above him.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: bigness in stature or age. Metaphorical: \"takabbara 'alayhi\" — to deem oneself greater than another and refuse submission to him.\n- Root: قصد (qaṣd)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Straightness of path; intending a goal.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-qaṣd: istiqāmat al-ṭarīq\" — the straight road; also moderation between extremes.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to head toward a place. Metaphorical: \"maqāṣid\" — the aims and objectives toward which one directs oneself; here, the worldly goals whose pursuit breeds rivalry and envy.\n- Root: نزل (nazal)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To descend from a height; to alight.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-nuzūl fī al-aṣl huwa inḥiṭāṭ min 'uluww\" — descent from above; the sending down of revelation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: descending from a high place. Metaphorical: \"anzala Allāhu al-kitāb\" — God's sending down of the Quran; and \"mā unzila\" — what was revealed, as in the Quranic citation regarding the Qurashites' objection to revelation upon a mere human.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Causal Coordination (al-'aṭf al-sababī) — The enumeration of the seven causes proceeds by coordinated clauses (fa-... thumma ...), each introduced as an independent proposition linked by causation (sabab), requiring the translator to preserve the logical sequence of psychological motivations without collapsing them into one another.","content_type":"ethics","topic":"Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Condemnation of Envy'","badge":"Ethics"},{"arabic":"تعالى {ليقولوا أهؤلاء من الله عليهم من بيننا} أي استحقارا لهم واستبعادا لتقدمهم وقالت قريش لرسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم كيف نجلس إليك وعندك هؤلاء وأشاروا إلى فقراء المسلمين فازدروهم بأعينهم لفقرهم وتكبروا عن مجالستهم فأنزل الله تعالى {ولا تطرد الذين يدعون ربهم بالغداة والعشي} إلى قوله {ما عليك من حسابهم} وقال تعالى {واصبر نفسك مع الذين يدعون ربهم بالغداة والعشي يريدون وجهه ولا تعد عيناك عنهم تريد زينة الحياة الدنيا} // حديث قالت قريش لرسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم كيف نجلس إليك وعندك هؤلاء الحد","english":"Know that the one against whom pride is directed is either God—mighty and majestic—or His messengers, or the rest of His creation. Man has been created unjust and ignorant {«وخلق الإنسان ظلوما جهولا»}, so at times he grows proud against the creation, and at times he grows proud against the Creator. Thus, pride, considered with respect to the one against whom it is directed, falls into three divisions.","book_title":"Condemnation of Pride and Conceit","section_title_ar":"Section 855","section_title_en":"Exposition of the One Against Whom Pride Is Directed, Its Degrees, Its Divisions, and the Fruits of Pride Within It","section_index":855,"anchors":"- Root: كبر (k-b-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes magnitude, whether physical size, age, or rank; \"الكبرة\" specifically marks the state of advanced age or an ingrained, chronic condition.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الكبير والصغير من الأسماء المتضايفة\" — bigness and smallness are relative terms. In the theological context, pride (الكبر) is the soul's self-magnification against another, a relational vice that distorts the creature's standing before the Creator.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage concerns physical or quantitative magnitude. Metaphorical (Majaz) usage transfers this to moral and spiritual stature, as in \"كبر علي ذلك\" (it weighed heavily upon him) — here, the soul's swelling against divine command is a metaphorical arrogation of greatness.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Prepositional Interposition (باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور) — Governing the syntactic tension between the agent of pride (المتكبر) and its object (المتكبر عليه), where the preposition \"على\" carries the full weight of opposition and elevation, structuring the periodic sentences that follow.","content_type":"ethics","topic":"Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Condemnation of Pride and Conceit'","badge":"Ethics"}],"54":[{"arabic":"أمره بالقعود مع طائفة فقال عز وجل وإذا جاءك الذين يؤمنون بآياتنا فقل سلام عليكم وأمره بالإعراض عن غيرهم فقال وإذا رأيت الذين يخوضون في آياتنا فأعرض عنهم حتى قال فلا تقعد بعد الذكرى مع القوم الظالمبن وقال تعالى واصبر نفسك مع الذين يدعون ربهم بالغداة والعشي فكذلك الانبساط والإدلال يحتمل من بعض العباد دون بعض فمن انبساط الأنس قول موسى عليه السلام إن هي إلا فتنتك تضل بها من تشاء وتهدي من تشاء وقوله في التعليل والاعتذار لما قيل له اذهب إلى فرعون {فقال} ولهم على ذنب وقوله إني أخاف أن يكذبون ويضيق صدري ولا ينطلق لساني وقوله إننا نخا","english":"As for Adam (peace be upon him), it was said concerning him: {«وعصى آدم ربه فغوى»} \"And Adam disobeyed his Lord, so he went astray\" (Q. 20:121), then {«اجتباه ربه فتاب عليه وهدى»} \"his Lord chose him, and turned to him in forgiveness, and guided him\" (Q. 20:122).","book_title":"Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment","section_title_ar":"Section 1187","section_title_en":"On the Varieties of Divine Proximity (Al-Inbisāṭ) and Awe (Al-Haybah) in the States of the Prophets and Saints","section_index":1187,"anchors":"- Root: بسط (B-S-T)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is \"to spread out, extend, expand\" — both physically (basting a garment) and metaphorically.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: \"بسط الشيء: نشره وتوسيعه\" — used for expansion of mercy, favor, and the soul's comfort. In Kalam, it contrasts with *qabḍ* (contraction/withholding).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"بسط الثوب والفراش إذا نشره.\" Metaphorical (Majaz): \"بسطني ما بسطك\" (what pleases you pleases me) — hence *inbisāṭ* here denotes the soul's expansive intimacy with God, a spiritual state, not a physical act.\n- Root: أبلس (B-L-S)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"أبلس: انقطعت حجته ويئس\" — to be reduced to silence, to despair.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الإبلاس: الحزن المعترض من شدة اليأس\" — the despair of Iblis is a theological state of being cut off from mercy.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: despair/silence. Metaphorical: Iblis (the proper name) is derived from this root — he is the one \"despairing\" of divine mercy.\n- Root: عله (not used; avoided per instruction) — *Note: The text's key term \"الإدلال\" (al-idlāl) is from root دلو (D-L-W).*\n- Root: دلو (D-L-W)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"دلا الدلو: أخرجها من البئر\" — to draw water from a well; hence *iddalā'a* (إدلال) means to advance boldly, relying on a familiar bond.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Not a primary Quranic term, but lexically implies confident approach based on prior intimacy.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: lowering the bucket. Metaphorical: \"أدلّ عليه\" — to act with bold familiarity toward someone who loves you, trusting in their affection.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Applied in the phrase \"فإنما هي عند ذوي الاعتبار من الأسماء\" (they are but names to those who reflect), restricting the ontological status of stories to mere signifiers for the discerning.","content_type":"spiritual","topic":"Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment'","badge":"Insight"}],"59":[{"arabic":"لا نهاية له وإنما لكل عبد بقدر رزقه فلا رطب ولا يابس إلا في كتاب مبين قل لو كان البحر مدادا لكلمات ربي لنفد البحر قبل أن تنفد كلمات ربي ولو جئنا بمثله مددا ولذلك قال علي رضي الله عنه لو شئت لأوقرت سبعين بعيرا من تفسير فاتحة الكتاب فالغرض مما ذكرناه التنبيه على طريق التفهيم لينفتح بابه فأما الاستقصاء فلا مطمع فيه ومن لم يكن له فهم ما في القرآن ولو في أدنى الدرجات دخل في قوله تعالى ومنهم من يستمع إليك حتى إذا خرجوا من عندك قالوا للذين أوتوا العلم ماذا قال آنفا أولئك الذين طبع الله على قلوبهم والطابع هي الموان","english":"I say: These meanings are buried treasures that are not unveiled except to the divinely aided ones. To this 'Ali — may God be pleased with him — alluded by his saying: \"The Messenger of God — may God's peace and blessings be upon him — did not confide to me anything that he concealed from the people, except that God — mighty and majestic — grants to a servant understanding (fahm) of His Book. So let one be eager in seeking that understanding.\" (1) And Ibn Mas'ud — may God be pleased with him — said: \"Whoever desires the knowledge of the first and the last, let him stir up the Qur'an.\" And the greatest of the sciences of the Qur'an lie beneath the names of God — mighty and majestic — and His attributes, for most of creation have grasped from them only matters befitting their understandings and have not stumbled upon their depths.","book_title":"Etiquette of Quran Recitation","section_title_ar":"Section 228","section_title_en":"The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 228: On the Inner Meanings of the Qur'an and the Removal of Obstacles to Understanding","section_index":228,"anchors":"- Root: فهم (f-h-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Comprehension, discernment, the faculty by which one grasps meanings\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الفهم: هيئة للإنسان بها يتحقق معاني ما يحسن\" — a disposition by which one verifies the realities of what is perceived; distinct from mere hearing or transmission\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: swift apprehension; Metaphorical: the divine gift of insight into hidden meanings, as in the verse {ففهمناها سليمان}\n- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Turning, overturning, transformation from one state to another\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart as that which is perpetually turned and overturned between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical turning; Metaphorical: the heart as the seat of understanding that is veiled or illuminated, rusted or polished\n- Root: سمع (s-m-')\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Hearing, the auditory faculty\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"السمع: قوة في الأذن به يدرك الأصوات\" — extends metaphorically to comprehension and obedience; \"أو ألقى السمع وهو شهيد\" — one who casts his hearing while present\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: auditory perception; Metaphorical: attentive obedience and receptive understanding\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Specification) — governing the precise delimitation of predicates such as \"إنما يتذكر أولوا الألباب\" where restriction confines the action to its proper subject","content_type":"spiritual","topic":"Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Etiquette of Quran Recitation'","badge":"Insight"}],"60":[{"arabic":"نجره إلى مسلم فإنك قلت {وهو الذي يتوفاكم بالليل ويعلم ما جرحتم بالنهار ثم يبعثكم فيه ليقضى أجل مسمى} (11) اللهم فالق الإصباح وجاعل الليل سكنا والشمس والقمر حسبانا أسألك خير هذا اليوم وخير ما فيه وأعوذ بك من شره وشر ما فيه (12) بسم الله ما شاء الله لا قوة إلا بالله ما شاء الله","english":"When you rise in the morning and hear the call to prayer (adhān), it is recommended for you to respond to the caller of prayer (mu'adhdhin) — and we have already mentioned this, along with the supplications for entering and leaving the privy, and the supplications of ablution (wuḍū'), in the Book of Purification. When you set out toward the mosque, say: \"O Allah, place light (nūr) in my heart, and light in my tongue, and place light in my hearing, and place light in my sight, and place light behind me, and light before me, and place light above me. O Allah, grant me light.\" And also say: \"O Allah, I ask You by the right of those who ask of You, and by the right of this my walking unto You — for I have not gone out in insolence, nor in vanity, nor for show, nor for reputation. I have gone out seeking protection from Your wrath and seeking Your good pleasure. So I ask You to deliver me from the Fire and to forgive me my sins — verily, none forgives sins but You.\"","book_title":"On Invocations and Supplications","section_title_ar":"Section 254","section_title_en":"Section 254 — Supplications for the Hours of Day and Night: From Dawn to Sleep","section_index":254,"anchors":"- Root: ن و ر (n-w-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: النور: الضوء المنتشر الذي يُبصر به، وهو ضد الظلمة\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): النور في القرآن يقع على ضروب: منها المعقول كالإيمان والحكمة، قال تعالى: {قد جاءكم من الله نور وكتاب مبين}، ومنها المحسوس كضوء الشمس والقمر\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): النور حقيقة في الضوء المحسوس، ومجاز في الهدى والإيمان والعلم — فدعاء النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم بطلب النور يشمل المعنيين جميعاً\n- Root: س ج د (s-j-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: السجود: وضع الجبهة على الأرض تعظيماً، وأصله التطامن والانخفاض\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): السجود ضربان: سجود عبادة وهو وضع الجبهة على الأرض تقرباً، وسجود طاعة وهو الانقياد والخضوع — قال تعالى: {ولله يسجد من في السموات والأرض طوعاً وكرهاً}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): سجد حقيقة في وضع الجبهة، ومجاز في الخضوع والانقياد — وقوله \"سجد لك سوادي وخيالي\" من المجاز المرسل\n- Root: ذ ك ر (dh-k-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: الذكر: نقيض النسيان، والذكر: الصوت الذي يُذكر به الشيء، والذكر: الشرف والصيت\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): الذكر ضربان: ذكر بالقلب وهو التفكر في عظمة الله، وذكر باللسان وهو التلفظ بأسمائه — وأعلى مراتبه ما اجتمع فيه القلب واللسان\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): الذكر حقيقة في استحضار الشيء بالقلب أو اللسان، ومجاز في الصلاة والدعاء والثناء\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — جواز تقديم المعمول وتأخيره مع بقاء العامل عاملاً في المعنى، كقوله \"لك الحمد\" حيث تقدم الجار والمجرور على المبتدأ للاختصاص والاهتمام","content_type":"dua","topic":"Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'On Invocations and Supplications'","badge":"Du'a"}],"68":[{"arabic":"قال الله تعالى لنبيه {فاعرض عنهم حتى يخوضوا في حديث غيره} ومهما كان كلامه مائلا إلى الإرجاء وتجرئة الناس على المعاصي وكان الناس يزدادون بكلامه جراءة وبعفو الله وبرحمته وثوقا يزيد بسببه رجاؤهم على خوفهم فهو منكر ويجب منعه عنه لأن فساد ذلك عظيم بل لو رجح خوفهم على رجائهم فذلك أليق وأقرب بطباع الخلق فإنهم إلى الخوف أحوج وإنما العدل تعديل الخوف والرجاء كما قال عمر رضي الله عنه لو نادى مناد يوم القيامة ليدخل النار كل الناس إلا رجلا واحدا لرجوت أن أكون أنا ذلك الرجل ولو نادى مناد ليدخل الجنة كل الناس إلا رج","english":"God the Exalted said to His Prophet: {«So turn away from them until they engage in a discourse other than that»} (6:68). And whenever his speech inclines toward irjāʾ (deferring judgment, i.e., the doctrine that sins do not harm faith) and emboldening people toward acts of disobedience, and the people increase through his speech in audacity and in trust in God's pardon and His mercy, such that their hope (rajāʾ) is increased by it over their fear (khawf) — then this is a reprehensible evil (munkar), and he must be prevented from it, for the corruption of that is immense. Rather, even if their fear outweighs their hope, that is more fitting and nearer to the natural constitution of creatures, for they are in greater need of fear. Justice is only the balancing of fear and hope, as ʿUmar (may God be pleased with him) said: \"If a crier were to call out on the Day of Resurrection, 'Let every person enter the Fire except one man,' I would hope that I were that man. And if a crier were to call out, 'Let every person enter Paradise except one man,' I would fear that I were that man.\"","book_title":"Enjoining Good and Forbidding Evil","section_title_ar":"Section 545","section_title_en":"The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 545: On the Preacher, the Storyteller, and the Reciter; and the Regulations of the Mosque","section_index":545,"anchors":"- Root: قصص (qaṣaṣa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes the tracing of a matter, following its traces and narrating its account; al-qaṣaṣ is the narration of reports and tales.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-qaṣaṣ is the seeking of a matter's trace and its exposition; the qāṣṣ (storyteller) is one who narrates accounts to admonish and remind.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: qaṣaṣa atharah — he followed his trace. Metaphorical: qaṣṣa ʿalayhi al-khabar — he narrated the report to him, as if tracing its unfolding.\n- Root: وعظ (waʿaẓa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Al-waʿẓ is admonition and exhortation, combining reminder of good with the softening of the heart through warning and counsel.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-waʿẓ is the reminder of hearts through speech that softens, whether by way of enticement (targhīb) or intimidation (tarhīb).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: waʿaẓahu — he admonished him. Metaphorical: it is the speech that penetrates the heart and leaves its effect upon it, as if branding it with counsel.\n- Root: بدع (badaʿa)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-bidʿah is a novel matter (muḥdath) introduced into religion after the prophetic era, not sanctioned by the Book, the Sunnah, or consensus.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: badaʿa al-shayʾ — he originated it without precedent. Metaphorical: the innovator (mubtadiʿ) is one who introduces into the religion what is not from it.\n- Root: خوف (khāfa)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-khawf is the anticipation of something disliked based on a probable or certain indication; it is opposed by amn (security). The balanced servant alternates between khawf and rajāʾ (hope), for each alone is blameworthy.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: khiftuhu — I feared him. Metaphorical: takhawwufahu — to diminish it gradually, as if taking from its edges out of caution.\n- Root: حضر (ḥaḍara)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-ḥaḍar is the opposite of badw (nomadism); then it was made a term for the presence of a place, a person, or an affair.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: ḥaḍartu al-majlis — I attended the session. Metaphorical: aḥḍir dhihnaka — be attentive and present of mind.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Bāb al-ḥaṣr wa-al-taqyīd bi-innamā (Restriction and Specification by innamā) — When the predicate is restricted to the subject by innamā, it negates the attribute from all others and affirms it solely for the restricted one, as in \"innamā al-ʿadl taʿdīl al-khawf wa-al-rajāʾ\" (justice is only the balancing of fear and hope).","content_type":"afterlife","topic":"Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Enjoining Good and Forbidding Evil'","badge":"Afterlife"},{"arabic":"سلام عليكم وأمره بالإعراض عن غيرهم فقال وإذا رأيت الذين يخوضون في آياتنا فأعرض عنهم حتى قال فلا تقعد بعد الذكرى مع القوم الظالمبن وقال تعالى واصبر نفسك مع الذين يدعون ربهم بالغداة والعشي فكذلك الانبساط والإدلال يحتمل من بعض العباد دون بعض فمن انبساط الأنس قول موسى عليه السلام إن هي إلا فتنتك تضل بها من تشاء وتهدي من تشاء وقوله في التعليل والاعتذار لما قيل له اذهب إلى فرعون {فقال} ولهم على ذنب وقوله إني أخاف أن يكذبون ويضيق صدري ولا ينطلق لساني وقوله إننا نخاف أن يفرط علينا أو أن يطغى وهذا من غير موسى عليه السلام من سوء الأدب لأن الذ","english":"As for Adam (peace be upon him), it was said concerning him: {«وعصى آدم ربه فغوى»} \"And Adam disobeyed his Lord, so he went astray\" (Q. 20:121), then {«اجتباه ربه فتاب عليه وهدى»} \"his Lord chose him, and turned to him in forgiveness, and guided him\" (Q. 20:122).","book_title":"Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment","section_title_ar":"Section 1187","section_title_en":"On the Varieties of Divine Proximity (Al-Inbisāṭ) and Awe (Al-Haybah) in the States of the Prophets and Saints","section_index":1187,"anchors":"- Root: بسط (B-S-T)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is \"to spread out, extend, expand\" — both physically (basting a garment) and metaphorically.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: \"بسط الشيء: نشره وتوسيعه\" — used for expansion of mercy, favor, and the soul's comfort. In Kalam, it contrasts with *qabḍ* (contraction/withholding).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"بسط الثوب والفراش إذا نشره.\" Metaphorical (Majaz): \"بسطني ما بسطك\" (what pleases you pleases me) — hence *inbisāṭ* here denotes the soul's expansive intimacy with God, a spiritual state, not a physical act.\n- Root: أبلس (B-L-S)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"أبلس: انقطعت حجته ويئس\" — to be reduced to silence, to despair.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الإبلاس: الحزن المعترض من شدة اليأس\" — the despair of Iblis is a theological state of being cut off from mercy.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: despair/silence. Metaphorical: Iblis (the proper name) is derived from this root — he is the one \"despairing\" of divine mercy.\n- Root: عله (not used; avoided per instruction) — *Note: The text's key term \"الإدلال\" (al-idlāl) is from root دلو (D-L-W).*\n- Root: دلو (D-L-W)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"دلا الدلو: أخرجها من البئر\" — to draw water from a well; hence *iddalā'a* (إدلال) means to advance boldly, relying on a familiar bond.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Not a primary Quranic term, but lexically implies confident approach based on prior intimacy.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: lowering the bucket. Metaphorical: \"أدلّ عليه\" — to act with bold familiarity toward someone who loves you, trusting in their affection.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Applied in the phrase \"فإنما هي عند ذوي الاعتبار من الأسماء\" (they are but names to those who reflect), restricting the ontological status of stories to mere signifiers for the discerning.","content_type":"spiritual","topic":"Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment'","badge":"Insight"},{"arabic":"فأعرض عنهم حتى قال فلا تقعد بعد الذكرى مع القوم الظالمبن وقال تعالى واصبر نفسك مع الذين يدعون ربهم بالغداة والعشي فكذلك الانبساط والإدلال يحتمل من بعض العباد دون بعض فمن انبساط الأنس قول موسى عليه السلام إن هي إلا فتنتك تضل بها من تشاء وتهدي من تشاء وقوله في التعليل والاعتذار لما قيل له اذهب إلى فرعون {فقال} ولهم على ذنب وقوله إني أخاف أن يكذبون ويضيق صدري ولا ينطلق لساني وقوله إننا نخاف أن يفرط علينا أو أن يطغى وهذا من غير موسى عليه السلام من سوء الأدب لأن الذي أقيم مقام الأنس يلاطف ويحتمل ولم يحتمل ل","english":"As for Adam (peace be upon him), it was said concerning him: {«وعصى آدم ربه فغوى»} \"And Adam disobeyed his Lord, so he went astray\" (Q. 20:121), then {«اجتباه ربه فتاب عليه وهدى»} \"his Lord chose him, and turned to him in forgiveness, and guided him\" (Q. 20:122).","book_title":"Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment","section_title_ar":"Section 1187","section_title_en":"On the Varieties of Divine Proximity (Al-Inbisāṭ) and Awe (Al-Haybah) in the States of the Prophets and Saints","section_index":1187,"anchors":"- Root: بسط (B-S-T)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is \"to spread out, extend, expand\" — both physically (basting a garment) and metaphorically.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: \"بسط الشيء: نشره وتوسيعه\" — used for expansion of mercy, favor, and the soul's comfort. In Kalam, it contrasts with *qabḍ* (contraction/withholding).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"بسط الثوب والفراش إذا نشره.\" Metaphorical (Majaz): \"بسطني ما بسطك\" (what pleases you pleases me) — hence *inbisāṭ* here denotes the soul's expansive intimacy with God, a spiritual state, not a physical act.\n- Root: أبلس (B-L-S)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"أبلس: انقطعت حجته ويئس\" — to be reduced to silence, to despair.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الإبلاس: الحزن المعترض من شدة اليأس\" — the despair of Iblis is a theological state of being cut off from mercy.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: despair/silence. Metaphorical: Iblis (the proper name) is derived from this root — he is the one \"despairing\" of divine mercy.\n- Root: عله (not used; avoided per instruction) — *Note: The text's key term \"الإدلال\" (al-idlāl) is from root دلو (D-L-W).*\n- Root: دلو (D-L-W)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"دلا الدلو: أخرجها من البئر\" — to draw water from a well; hence *iddalā'a* (إدلال) means to advance boldly, relying on a familiar bond.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Not a primary Quranic term, but lexically implies confident approach based on prior intimacy.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: lowering the bucket. Metaphorical: \"أدلّ عليه\" — to act with bold familiarity toward someone who loves you, trusting in their affection.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Applied in the phrase \"فإنما هي عند ذوي الاعتبار من الأسماء\" (they are but names to those who reflect), restricting the ontological status of stories to mere signifiers for the discerning.","content_type":"spiritual","topic":"Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment'","badge":"Insight"}],"70":[{"arabic":"قلوب أهلها قال الله تعالى {وذر الذين اتخذوا دينهم لعبا ولهوا} وقال تعالى {أفمن زين له سوء عمله فرآه حسنا} فكل ما أحدث بعد الصحابة رضي الله عنهم مما جاوز قدر الضرورة والحاجة فهو من اللعب واللهو وحكي عن إبليس لعنه الله أنه بث جنوده في وقت الصحابة رضي الله عنهم فرجعوا إليه محسورين فقال ما شأنكم قالوا ما رأينا مثل هؤلاء ما نصيب منهم شيئا وقد أتعبونا فقال إنكم لا تقدرون عليهم قد صحبوا نبيهم وشهدوا تنزيل ربهم ولكن سيأتي بعدهم قوم تنالون منهم حاجتكم فلما جاء التابعون بث جنوده فرجعوا إليه منكسين فقالوا ما رأينا","english":"For this reason ʿAlī, may God be pleased with him, said — when it was said to him, \"You have opposed so-and-so\" — \"The best of us is the most following of us of this religion.\" So one ought not to be perturbed by opposing the people of one's own age when one is thereby conforming to the people of the age of the Messenger of God ﷺ. For the people have adopted an opinion (raʾy) concerning their present situation, due to the inclination of their natures toward it, and their souls have not permitted them to acknowledge that this is a cause of deprivation from Paradise — so they have claimed that there is no path to Paradise other than it.","book_title":"Book of Knowledge","section_title_ar":"Section 64","section_title_en":"** Section 64 — On the Sciences of the Inner Life: The Obligation to Follow the Salaf and the Condemnation of Innovation (Bidʿah)","section_index":64,"anchors":"**\n- Root: علم (ʿalima)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning — perception of a thing by its true nature; knowledge as the apprehension of reality.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العلم: إدراك الشيء بحقيقته\" — knowledge is the apprehension of a thing in its true reality, divided into apprehension of the essence and judgment upon the thing.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal usage — \"ما علمت بخبرك: ما شعرت به\" (I did not know of your news: I had no awareness of it). The scholar (ʿālim) is one whose knowledge is manifest in action.\n- Root: بدع (badaʿa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning — to originate, to create something new without prior example.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الإبداع: إنشاء صنعة بلا احتذاء واقتداء\" — origination of a thing without model or precedent. When used of God, it is bringing into existence without instrument, matter, time, or place.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — \"أبدع الشيء وابتدعه: اخترعه\" (he originated it and innovated it: he invented it). Metaphorical — \"أبدعت حجتك إذا ضعفت\" (your argument became weak/innovative in the bad sense).\n- Root: حدث (ḥadatha)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحدوث: كون الشيء بعد أن لم يكن\" — coming-to-be of a thing after it was not. \"المحدث: ما أوجد بعد أن لم يكن\" — the originated thing.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — \"هو حدث من الأحداث\" (he is a youth among youths). Metaphorical — \"صاروا أحاديث\" (they became [mere] tales). In religious terminology: muḥdathāt = newly-invented matters in religion.\n- Root: هدى (hadā)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Guidance — directing to the path with gentleness; al-hudā is the guidance of the Prophet ﷺ in matters of religion.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"حسن الهدى\" — excellent guidance, the prophetic way (sunnah) as opposed to innovation.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — \"إنما هما اثنتان: الكلام والهدى\" restricts the two matters exclusively to speech and guidance, excluding all else.\n\n---\n\n#","content_type":"spiritual","topic":"Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Book of Knowledge'","badge":"Insight"}],"71":[{"arabic":"تعالى بتخصيص الإضافة إليه وإن كان الكل من جهته تعالى فقال تعالى {قل إن هدى الله هو الهدى} وهو المسمى حياة في قوله تعالى {أو من كان ميتا فأحييناه وجعلنا له نورا يمشي به في الناس} والمعنى بقوله تعالى {أفمن شرح الله صدره للإسلام فهو على نور من ربه} وأما الرشد فنعني به العناية الإلهية التي تعين الإنسان عند توجهه إلى مقاصده فتقوبه على ما فيه صلاحه وتفتره عما فيه فساده ويكون ذلك من الباطن كما قال تعالى {ولقد آتينا إبراهيم رشده من قبل وكنا به عالمين} فالرشد عبارة عن هداية باعثة إلى جهة السعادة محركة إليها فالصبي إذا بلغ خبيرا بحفظ الم","english":"PageV04P107\nBut if one does not know what constitutes the welfare of his hereafter—so that he even supposes corruption to be welfare—then from where would mere volition benefit him? There is no benefit in volition, power, and causes except after guidance (hidāyah). For this reason, God—exalted is He—said: {«ربنا الذي أعطى كل شيء خلقه ثم هدى»} \"Our Lord who gave everything its creation, then guided.\" And He said—exalted is He—: {«ولولا فضل الله عليكم ورحمته ما زكى منكم أحد أبدا ولكن الله يزكي من يشاء»} \"And were it not for God's bounty upon you and His mercy, not one of you would ever be purified; but God purifies whom He wills.\" And the Prophet—peace be upon him—said: \"No one enters Paradise except by the mercy of God—exalted is He—,\" that is, by His guidance. So it was said: \"Not even you, O Messenger of God?\" He said: \"Not even I\" (1). And guidance (hidāyah) has three stations (manāzil):","book_title":"Patience and Gratitude","section_title_ar":"Section 999","section_title_en":"The Degrees of Divine Guidance (Hidāyah), Righteous Discernment (Rushd), Straightening (Tasdīd), and Divine Support (Ta'yīd)","section_index":999,"anchors":"- Root: هدي (H-D-Y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of gentle, gracious direction (dalālah bi-luṭf); the root also yields \"gift\" (hadiyyah) and the leading beasts of prey (hawādī) that guide the herd.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Guidance is \"directional indication with gentleness\" (dalālah bi-luṭf), distinct from mere intellectual proof; it is a divine act that may be general (to all) or specific (to the elect), and its withholding is a form of abandonment.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"He guided him to the path\" (hadāhu lil-sabīl). Metaphorical (majāz): \"He guided him\" meaning \"he preceded him\" (taqaddamahu), as a leader precedes the caravan; hence guidance is not merely pointing but active leading.\n- Root: رشد (R-SH-D)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The state of being rightly directed in one's affairs; integrity of judgment; names Rāshid, Rushayd derive from it.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Rushd is the opposite of ghayy (error/ruin); it is used like hidāyah but with a nuance of practical, executive discernment—knowing the good AND being moved to pursue it. Al-Raghib explicitly distinguishes the rushd of Abraham (given before, as a special gift) from the mere legal competence (rushd) of an orphan.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"A man is rāshid or rashīd\" (possessing rightness in conduct). Metaphorical: \"He is of sound lineage\" (huwa li-rushdah), i.e., legitimate—showing the root's extension to ontological soundness.\n- Root: سدد (S-D-D)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To aim correctly, to hit the mark; the arrow that flies straight (sadīd); the opposite of deviation (ighrāḍ).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): (Not in the retrieved scholia, but by extension from the root's usage in the Qur'an, e.g., {wa-saddidū}, \"and be straight/aim rightly\")—it denotes the divine facilitation of the creature's movements so they align with the intended good.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"He aimed the arrow and it went straight.\" Metaphorical: \"He was granted success in speech and action\" (sudda li-l-ṣawāb).\n- Root: أيد (A-Y-D)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Strength, support, reinforcement; the \"aid\" (ayd) of God is His empowering of His servant.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): (By extension from {إذ أيدتك بروح القدس})—divine reinforcement through a holy spirit, i.e., an immaterial power that fortifies both inner perception (baṣīrah) and outer action.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"He strengthened him\" (ayyadahu). Metaphorical: Used for the conferral of spiritual power that makes the servant invincible against error.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Gradation and Specification (Tartīb wa-Takhṣīṣ) — The author structures a sequence of terms (hidāyah → rushd → tasdīd → ta'yīd) where each subsequent term is syntactically and semantically superordinate, building a hierarchy of divine acts. The particle \"wa-ammā\" (وأما) marks a new topic with contrastive focus, and the anaphoric references (هو، وهي) bind the exposition into a single periodic argument.","content_type":"hadith","topic":"Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Patience and Gratitude'","badge":"Hadith"}],"75":[{"arabic":"قال الله تعالى ما كذب الفؤاد ما رأى وقال تعالى وكذلك نرى إبراهيم ملكوت السموات والأرض الآية وسمي ضده عمى فقال تعالى فإنها لا تعمى الأبصار ولكن تعمي القلوب التي في الصدور وقال تعالى ومن كان في هذه أعمى فهو في الآخرة أعمى وأضل سبيلا وهذه الأمور التي كشفت للأنبياء بعضها كان بالبصر وبعضها كان بالبصيرة وسمي الكل رؤية وبالجملة من لم تكن بصيرته الباطنة ثاقبة لم يعلق به من الدين إلا قشوره وأمثلته دون لبابه وحقائقه فهذه أقسام ما ينطلق اسم العقل عليها","english":"I say: Know that the washing of the eye is forbidden — that is, the eye of insight (ʿayn al-baṣīrah) is not to be washed with the water of blind imitation and mere hearsay, for it is the organ by which the realities of the intellect are disclosed. And the divisions of what the name \"intellect\" (al-ʿaql) is applied to have become clear: it is said of the inborn faculty (al-gharīzah), and it is said of the knowledge that is acquired through that faculty, and it is said of the state of that knowledge when it governs the soul, and it is said of the very actions that proceed from that governance.","book_title":"Contemplation","section_title_ar":"Section 68","section_title_en":"The Washing of the Eye is Forbidden — On the True Meaning of Intellect (ʿAql) and Its Divisions","section_index":68,"anchors":"- Root: عقل (ʿAql)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes binding, restraining, and holding back — the intellect is that which restrains the soul from base desires and prevents it from straying.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العقل يقال للقوة المتهيئة لقبول العلم، ويقال للعلم الذي يستفيده الإنسان بتلك القوة عقل\" — al-ʿaql is said of the faculty prepared to receive knowledge, and it is also said of the knowledge acquired by that faculty. It is the instrument of divine discernment and the locus of taklīf (moral responsibility).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): the inborn faculty of discernment. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"نخلة لا تعقل الإبار إذا لم تقبله\" — a palm tree that does not accept pollination is said to be \"not to ʿaqil\" the pollen, extending the root to the sense of \"accepting/receiving.\" Thus, knowledge is called ʿaql metaphorically as its fruit.\n- Root: بصر (Baṣar)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Sight, perception — both of the eye and of the heart.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"البصر يقال للجارحة الناظرة... ويقال لقوة القلب المدركة: بصيرة وبصر\" — baṣar is said of the seeing organ, and of the perceiving power of the heart: baṣīrah and baṣar. The inner sight (baṣīrah) perceives immaterial realities.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: ocular vision. Metaphorical: \"هذه آية مبصرة\" — this is an illuminating sign; \"واجعلني بصيرة عليهم أي رقيبا وشاهدا\" — make me a watcher over them. The inner eye perceives what the outer eye cannot.\n- Root: فطر (Faṭara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To split, cleave, originate, create — the primordial act of divine creation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"أصل الفطر: الشق طولا... وفطر الله الخلق\" — the root is splitting lengthwise; God's \"fatr\" of creation is His originating of things. \"فطرة الله التي فطر الناس عليها\" — the primordial disposition upon which God originated mankind.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"فطر الله الخلق، وهو فاطر السموات: مبتدعها\" — God originated creation; He is the Originator of the heavens. \"وكل مولود يولد على الفطرة\" — every newborn is born upon the fiṭrah, i.e., the innate disposition receptive to the religion of truth.\n- Root: نفس (Nafs)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Self, soul, spirit; the innermost reality of a thing.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النفس: الروح... وقوله: تعلم ما في نفسي ولا أعلم ما في نفسك\" — nafs is the spirit/soul; \"You know what is in my nafs and I know not what is in Your nafs\" — the nafs is the seat of inner assent and recognition.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the self. Metaphorical: \"دفق نفسه أي دمه\" — he poured out his nafs, i.e., his blood. The nafs is the locus of the primordial covenant (mīthāq).\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The construction \"إنما العاقل من آمن بالله\" restricts the predicate: the intellect is not merely a faculty of cleverness but is defined by its fruit — faith and God-consciousness (taqwā).","content_type":"spiritual","topic":"Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Contemplation'","badge":"Insight"}],"76":[{"arabic":"أول بعض الصوفية درجات ما كان يظهر لإبراهيم الخليل صلى الله عليه وسلم في ترقيه وقال {فلما جن عليه الليل} أي أظلم عليه الأمر {رأى كوكبا} أي وصل إلى حجاب من حجب النور فعبر عنه بالكوكب وما أريد به هذه الأجسام المضيئة فإن آحاد العوام لا يخفى عليهم أن الربوبية لا تليق بالأجسام بل يدركون ذلك بأوائل نظرهم فما لا يضلل العوام لا يضلل الخليل عليه السلام والحجب المسماة أنوارا ما أريد بها الضوء المحسوس بالبصر بل أريد بها ما أريد بقوله تعالى الله نور السموات والأرض مثل نوره كمشكاة فيها مصباح الآية ولنتجاوز هذه المعاني فإنها خارجة عن علم المعاملة ولا يو","english":"Now, the first watch (al-wird al-awwal) is that which extends from the dawn prayer (ṣalāt al-ṣubḥ) until sunrise (ṭulūʿ al-shams). When the servant has completed his obligatory prayer, he should remain seated in his place of prayer, occupying himself with the remembrance of God most High, until the sun has risen. For it has been reported that the Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, said: \"That I should sit in my assembly, remembering God most High therein, from the morning prayer until sunrise, is more beloved to me than freeing four slaves.\" {«لأن أقعد في مجلسي أذكر الله تعالى فيه من صلاة الغداة إلى طلوع الشمس أحب إلي من أن أعتق أربع رقاب»} And it is related that he, peace and blessings be upon him, when he had prayed the morning prayer, would sit in his place of prayer until the sun rose; and in some versions [it is added] that he would pray two rakʿahs—that is, after sunrise. Concerning the virtue of this [practice], an incalculable number of reports have come down. Al-Ḥasan related that the Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, used to mention, among what he recounted of his Lord's mercy, that He said: \"O son of Adam, remember Me for an hour after the dawn prayer and for an hour after the afternoon prayer, and I shall suffice you for what lies between them.\" {«يا ابن آدم اذكرني بعد صلاة الفجر ساعة وبعد صلاة العصر ساعة أكفك ما بينهما»}","book_title":"Arrangement of Litanies","section_title_ar":"Section 260","section_title_en":"The Daily Liturgy of the Devout Servant: The First Watch (After Dawn Prayer until Sunrise)","section_index":260,"anchors":"- Root: ذكر (dhakara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Remembrance, invocation, mention; the opposite of forgetfulness (nisyān).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Remembrance of God (dhikr) is the heart's presence with the Divine, ranging from the tongue's utterance to the heart's contemplation; it is the noblest act when accompanied by presence of heart.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): verbal mention. Metaphorical (majāz): the heart's steadfastness in knowledge of God, which is the reality of dhikr.\n- Root: صلا (ṣallā)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Prayer (ṣalāh); root meaning of supplication and blessing; also \"to kindle a fire\" (as in ṣalā al-nār).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Ṣalāh is the servant's standing before God in humility and devotion, comprising praise, recitation, bowing, and prostration; its perfection lies in the heart's presence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the ritual prayer with its pillars. Metaphorical: supplication (duʿā') and blessing (taṣliyah) upon the Prophet.\n- Root: ورد (warada)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To arrive at water; a portion or share of something that comes regularly.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The daily portion (wird) of devotion—a set liturgy of prayers, recitations, and remembrances that the servant regularly performs at appointed times.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: arriving at a watering place. Metaphorical: the soul's regular approach to the fountain of divine grace through prescribed acts of worship.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Restriction and Specification (al-ḥaṣr wa al-taqyīd) — governing the precise enumeration of the four functions (adʿiyah, adhkār, qirā'ah, fikr) after the dawn prayer, each specified by its own governing verb and object.","content_type":"dua","topic":"Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'Arrangement of Litanies'","badge":"Du'a"},{"arabic":"إخبارا عنه فلما جن عليه الليل رأى كوكبا قال هذا ربي وليس المعنى به هذه الأجسام المضيئة فإنه كان يراها في الصغر ويعلم أنها ليست آلهة وهي كثيرة وليست واحدا والجهال يعلمون أن الكوكب ليس بإله فمثل إبراهيم عليه السلام لا يغره الكوكب الذي لا يغر السوادية ولكن المراد به أنه نور من الأنوار التي هي من حجب الله عز وجل وهي على طريق السالكين ولا يتصور الوصول إلى الله تعالى إلا بالوصول إلى هذه الحجب وهي حجب من نور بعضها أكبر من بعض وأصغر النيرات الكوكب فاستعير له لفظه وأعظمها الشمس وبينهما رتبة القمر فلم يزل إب","english":"...as an informing account of him. When the night darkened over him, he saw a star and said: \"This is my Lord\" {«هذا ربي»}. The meaning is not these luminous bodies [themselves], for he had seen them in his childhood and knew that they were not gods — and they are many, not one. Even the ignorant know that a star is not a god. So how could the like of Abraham, peace be upon him, be deluded by a star that would not delude even a common Bedouin?","book_title":"Condemnation of Delusion","section_title_ar":"Section 908","section_title_en":"Section 908 — On the Veils of Light and the Delusions of the Wayfarers; The Fourth Class: Those Deluded by Wealth","section_index":908,"anchors":"- Root: نور (n-w-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of radiant light, illumination, and luminosity; the source of visibility.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Light is of two kinds: sensory (perceived by the eye) and intelligible/spiritual (perceived by the inner eye of insight). Divine light is that which emanates from divine matters, such as the light of the intellect and the light of the Quran. The Prophet Abraham's vision pertains to this spiritual light, not mere celestial bodies.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage denotes physical illumination; metaphorical (Majaz) usage denotes clarity, manifestation, and guidance — \"نور الأمر: بينه\" (he illuminated the matter: he made it clear).\n- Root: حجب (ḥ-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Literal meaning of screening, veiling, and preventing access.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحجب والحجاب: المنع من الوصول\" — the veil is that which prevents reaching. In the divine context, veils are not physical barriers but ontological screens separating the creature from the Creator, each veil being a luminous reality.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal usage: \"ضرب الحجاب على النساء\" (the veil was drawn over women). Metaphorical usage: \"له دعوات تخرق الحجب أي تبلغ العرش\" (his supplications pierce the veils, i.e., reach the Throne) — indicating that veils between God and servant are traversed by spiritual ascent.\n- Root: وصل (w-ṣ-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Union, connection, reaching, and attainment.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الاتصال: اتحاد الأشياء بعضها ببعض\" — connection is the unification of things with one another. In the spiritual path, \"الوصول إلى الله\" (attainment to God) is not physical approach but the removal of veils and the realization of divine truths.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: joining or connecting physical objects. Metaphorical: \"وصل إلى بني فلان واتصل: انتمى\" (he connected to such a tribe: he became affiliated) — indicating that spiritual connection is one of ontological relation, not spatial proximity.\n- Root: غرر (gh-r-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Deception, delusion, and being beguiled by appearances.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Delusion (ghurūr) is the state of being deceived by that which appears beneficial but is in reality harmful or deficient — the root of all spiritual pride and self-satisfaction.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: being tricked or deceived. Metaphorical: the state of one who stops at a partial manifestation of truth, mistaking it for the whole.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The rule governing the separation of the prepositional phrase from its governed noun, allowing for parenthetical clarifications that do not disrupt the syntactic unity of the sentence, as in the layered structure of \"فلم يزل إبراهيم عليه السلام لما رأى ملكوت السموات... يصل إلى نور بعد نور.\"","content_type":"ethics","topic":"Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Condemnation of Delusion'","badge":"Ethics"},{"arabic":"عظمه غير خال عن الهوى في حضيض النقص والانحطاط عن ذروة الكمال قال لا أحب الآفلين إلى أن قال إني وجهت وجهي للذي فطر السموات والأرض وسالك هذه الطريق قد يغتر في الوقوف على بعض هذه الحجب وقد يغتر بالحجاب الأول وأول الحجب بين الله وبين العبد هو نفسه فإنه أيضا أمر رباني وهو نور من أنوار الله تعالى أعني سر القلب الذي تتجلى فيه حقيقة الحق كله حتى إنه ليتسع لجملة العالم ويحيط به وتتجلى فيه صورة الكل وعند ذلك يشرق نوره إشراقا عظيما إذ يظهر فيه الوجود كله على ما هو عليه وهو في أول الأمر محجوب بمشكاة هي كالساتر له فإذا تجلى نوره وانكشف جمال","english":"...as an informing account of him. When the night darkened over him, he saw a star and said: \"This is my Lord\" {«هذا ربي»}. The meaning is not these luminous bodies [themselves], for he had seen them in his childhood and knew that they were not gods — and they are many, not one. Even the ignorant know that a star is not a god. So how could the like of Abraham, peace be upon him, be deluded by a star that would not delude even a common Bedouin?","book_title":"Condemnation of Delusion","section_title_ar":"Section 908","section_title_en":"Section 908 — On the Veils of Light and the Delusions of the Wayfarers; The Fourth Class: Those Deluded by Wealth","section_index":908,"anchors":"- Root: نور (n-w-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of radiant light, illumination, and luminosity; the source of visibility.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Light is of two kinds: sensory (perceived by the eye) and intelligible/spiritual (perceived by the inner eye of insight). Divine light is that which emanates from divine matters, such as the light of the intellect and the light of the Quran. The Prophet Abraham's vision pertains to this spiritual light, not mere celestial bodies.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage denotes physical illumination; metaphorical (Majaz) usage denotes clarity, manifestation, and guidance — \"نور الأمر: بينه\" (he illuminated the matter: he made it clear).\n- Root: حجب (ḥ-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Literal meaning of screening, veiling, and preventing access.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحجب والحجاب: المنع من الوصول\" — the veil is that which prevents reaching. In the divine context, veils are not physical barriers but ontological screens separating the creature from the Creator, each veil being a luminous reality.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal usage: \"ضرب الحجاب على النساء\" (the veil was drawn over women). Metaphorical usage: \"له دعوات تخرق الحجب أي تبلغ العرش\" (his supplications pierce the veils, i.e., reach the Throne) — indicating that veils between God and servant are traversed by spiritual ascent.\n- Root: وصل (w-ṣ-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Union, connection, reaching, and attainment.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الاتصال: اتحاد الأشياء بعضها ببعض\" — connection is the unification of things with one another. In the spiritual path, \"الوصول إلى الله\" (attainment to God) is not physical approach but the removal of veils and the realization of divine truths.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: joining or connecting physical objects. Metaphorical: \"وصل إلى بني فلان واتصل: انتمى\" (he connected to such a tribe: he became affiliated) — indicating that spiritual connection is one of ontological relation, not spatial proximity.\n- Root: غرر (gh-r-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Deception, delusion, and being beguiled by appearances.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Delusion (ghurūr) is the state of being deceived by that which appears beneficial but is in reality harmful or deficient — the root of all spiritual pride and self-satisfaction.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: being tricked or deceived. Metaphorical: the state of one who stops at a partial manifestation of truth, mistaking it for the whole.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The rule governing the separation of the prepositional phrase from its governed noun, allowing for parenthetical clarifications that do not disrupt the syntactic unity of the sentence, as in the layered structure of \"فلم يزل إبراهيم عليه السلام لما رأى ملكوت السموات... يصل إلى نور بعد نور.\"","content_type":"spiritual","topic":"Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Condemnation of Delusion'","badge":"Insight"}],"79":[{"arabic":"الحقيقي وذلك كإفلاس من يصبح بكرة ويتوجه إلى القبلة ويقول وجهت وجهي للذي فطر السموات والأرض حنيفا وهو أول كذب يفاتح الله به كل يوم إن لم يكن وجه قلبه متوجها إلى الله تعالى على الخصوص فإنه إن أراد بالوجه وجه الظاهر فما وجهه إلا إلى الكعبة وما صرفه إلا عن سائر الجهات والكعبة ليست جهة للذي فطر السموات والأرض حتى يكون المتوجه إليها متوجها إليه تعالى عن أن تحده الجهات والأقطار وإن أراد به وجه القلب وهو المطلوب المتعبد به فكيف يصدق في قوله وقلبه متردد في أوطاره وحاجاته الدنيوية ومتصرف في طلب الحيل في جمع الأموال والجاه واستكثار الأسباب ومتوجه بالكل","english":"This [bankruptcy] is like the bankruptcy of one who rises at dawn, turns toward the qiblah, and says: \"I have turned my face toward Him Who originated the heavens and the earth, as a ḥanīf\" {«وجهت وجهي للذي فطر السموات والأرض حنيفا»} — and this is the very first lie with which he opens his address to God each day, unless the face of his heart is turned toward God — Exalted is He — exclusively and specifically. For if by \"face\" he means the outward, physical face, then his face is turned only toward the Kaʿbah, and he has merely diverted it from the other cardinal directions. But the Kaʿbah is not a direction (jihah) for the One Who originated the heavens and the earth, such that one who turns toward it would thereby be turning toward Him — far exalted is He above being bounded by directions and regions (aqṭār).","book_title":"Book of Knowledge","section_title_ar":"Section 29","section_title_en":"The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 29: On True Monotheism (Tawḥīd) and the Remembrance of God (Dhikr)","section_index":29,"anchors":"- Root: هوي (h-w-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الهوى: سقوط الشيء من علو إلى أسفل؛ ثم استعمل في ميل النفس إلى ما تلتذ به من غير داعية العقل\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الهوى: ميل النفس إلى ما تميل إليه من الشهوات، وقد ذم الله اتباعه في غير موضع من كتابه\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"هويته: أحببته\" — Metaphorical: \"فلان يهوي في الدين: يذهب فيه بغير بصيرة\"\n- Root: وجه (w-j-h)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الوجه: ما يستقبل به الشيء، وأول ما يبدو من الإنسان\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"ولما كان الوجه أول ما يستقبلك وأشرف ما في ظاهر البدن استعمل في مستقبل كل شيء، وعبر عن الذات بالوجه في قوله: {ويبقى وجه ربك ذو الجلال والإكرام}\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"وجه الثوب: ما استقبلك منه\" — Metaphorical: \"وجه القلب: توجهه وإخلاصه لله\"\n- Root: ذكر (dh-k-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الذكر: نقيض النسيان، والذكر: الثناء والقول المتردد على اللسان\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"الذكر: حضور المعنى في النفس، ويستعمل في القول الدال على ذلك، وفي الحفظ\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"ذكرت الشيء: استحضرته\" — Metaphorical: \"مجالس الذكر: المجالس التي يذكر فيها الله تعالى\"\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — \"إنما\" restricts the predicate to the subject alone, negating all else; applied here to confine true tawḥīd to the heart's orientation, not the tongue's utterance.","content_type":"spiritual","topic":"Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Book of Knowledge'","badge":"Insight"},{"arabic":"الآفلين إلى أن قال إني وجهت وجهي للذي فطر السموات والأرض وسالك هذه الطريق قد يغتر في الوقوف على بعض هذه الحجب وقد يغتر بالحجاب الأول وأول الحجب بين الله وبين العبد هو نفسه فإنه أيضا أمر رباني وهو نور من أنوار الله تعالى أعني سر القلب الذي تتجلى فيه حقيقة الحق كله حتى إنه ليتسع لجملة العالم ويحيط به وتتجلى فيه صورة الكل وعند ذلك يشرق نوره إشراقا عظيما إذ يظهر فيه الوجود كله على ما هو عليه وهو في أول الأمر محجوب بمشكاة هي كالساتر له فإذا تجلى نوره وانكشف جمال القلب بعد إشراق نور الله عليه ربما التفت صاحب القل","english":"...as an informing account of him. When the night darkened over him, he saw a star and said: \"This is my Lord\" {«هذا ربي»}. The meaning is not these luminous bodies [themselves], for he had seen them in his childhood and knew that they were not gods — and they are many, not one. Even the ignorant know that a star is not a god. So how could the like of Abraham, peace be upon him, be deluded by a star that would not delude even a common Bedouin?","book_title":"Condemnation of Delusion","section_title_ar":"Section 908","section_title_en":"Section 908 — On the Veils of Light and the Delusions of the Wayfarers; The Fourth Class: Those Deluded by Wealth","section_index":908,"anchors":"- Root: نور (n-w-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of radiant light, illumination, and luminosity; the source of visibility.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Light is of two kinds: sensory (perceived by the eye) and intelligible/spiritual (perceived by the inner eye of insight). Divine light is that which emanates from divine matters, such as the light of the intellect and the light of the Quran. The Prophet Abraham's vision pertains to this spiritual light, not mere celestial bodies.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage denotes physical illumination; metaphorical (Majaz) usage denotes clarity, manifestation, and guidance — \"نور الأمر: بينه\" (he illuminated the matter: he made it clear).\n- Root: حجب (ḥ-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Literal meaning of screening, veiling, and preventing access.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحجب والحجاب: المنع من الوصول\" — the veil is that which prevents reaching. In the divine context, veils are not physical barriers but ontological screens separating the creature from the Creator, each veil being a luminous reality.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal usage: \"ضرب الحجاب على النساء\" (the veil was drawn over women). Metaphorical usage: \"له دعوات تخرق الحجب أي تبلغ العرش\" (his supplications pierce the veils, i.e., reach the Throne) — indicating that veils between God and servant are traversed by spiritual ascent.\n- Root: وصل (w-ṣ-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Union, connection, reaching, and attainment.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الاتصال: اتحاد الأشياء بعضها ببعض\" — connection is the unification of things with one another. In the spiritual path, \"الوصول إلى الله\" (attainment to God) is not physical approach but the removal of veils and the realization of divine truths.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: joining or connecting physical objects. Metaphorical: \"وصل إلى بني فلان واتصل: انتمى\" (he connected to such a tribe: he became affiliated) — indicating that spiritual connection is one of ontological relation, not spatial proximity.\n- Root: غرر (gh-r-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Deception, delusion, and being beguiled by appearances.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Delusion (ghurūr) is the state of being deceived by that which appears beneficial but is in reality harmful or deficient — the root of all spiritual pride and self-satisfaction.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: being tricked or deceived. Metaphorical: the state of one who stops at a partial manifestation of truth, mistaking it for the whole.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The rule governing the separation of the prepositional phrase from its governed noun, allowing for parenthetical clarifications that do not disrupt the syntactic unity of the sentence, as in the layered structure of \"فلم يزل إبراهيم عليه السلام لما رأى ملكوت السموات... يصل إلى نور بعد نور.\"","content_type":"spiritual","topic":"Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Condemnation of Delusion'","badge":"Insight"},{"arabic":"وإذا قلت وما أنا من المشركين فأخطر ببالك الشرك الخفي فإن قوله تعالى فمن كان يرجو لقاء ربه فليعمل عملا صالحا ولا يشرك بعبادة ربه أحدا نزل فيمن يقصد بعبادته وجه الله وحمد الناس وكن حذرا مشفقا من هذا الشرك واستشعر الخجلة في قلبك إن وصفت نفسك بأنك لست من المشركين من غير براءة عن هذا الشرك فإن اسم الشرك يقع على القليل والكثير منه وإذا قلت محياي ومماتي لله فاعلم أن هذا حال عبد مفقود لنفسه موجود لسيده وأنه إن صدر ممن رضاه وغضبه وقيامه وقعوده ورغبته في الحياة ورهبته من الموت لأمور ا","english":"For this reason, he, peace be upon him, said: \"Give us rest, O Bilāl\" (1) — meaning, give us rest through it (the prayer) and through the call to it, for his delight, peace be upon him, was in it.","book_title":"Mysteries of Prayer","section_title_ar":"Section 138","section_title_en":"The Inner Works of Prayer: Unveiling the Heart's Secrets in the Ritual Prayer (Asrār al-Ṣalāh)","section_index":138,"anchors":"- Root: قلب (Q-L-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To turn a thing over, to revolve it, to divert it from one state to another; the heart as that which is constantly turning and fluctuating.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — The heart is that which is turned and diverted; its rectitude lies in its orientation towards God, its ruin in its turning towards other than Him.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" (to turn a thing from its face). Metaphorical (Majaz): \"قلب المعلم الصبيان: صرفهم إلى بيوتهم\" (the teacher turned the boys towards their homes) — thus the heart's turning is its direction and intent.\n- Root: وجه (W-J-H)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The face; the foremost, noblest, and most apparent part of a thing.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"ولما كان الوجه أول ما يستقبلك، وأشرف ما في ظاهر البدن استعمل في مستقبل كل شيء، وفي أشرفه ومبدئه\" — The face is used metaphorically for the essence and the direction of a thing; the \"face of the heart\" is its intention and aspiration.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"واجهته مواجهة ووجاها\" (to face one another). Metaphorical: \"هذا وجه الثوب. ووجه القوم، وهؤلاء وجوه البلد\" (the face of a garment, the chiefs of a people) — thus the \"face\" of the heart is its foremost orientation.\n- Root: وجل (W-J-L)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: A trembling of the heart from fear or awe.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — The sensing of fear; the heart's tremor before the majesty of the Divine.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال، وقد وجل وجلا\" — A man trembling with fear.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The particle \"innamā\" restricts and confines the predicate to the subject alone, negating it from all else. This is crucial for understanding \"إنما هذه الظواهر تحريكات للبواطن\" — the outward acts are *only* movements to stir the inward realities, nothing more.","content_type":"spiritual","topic":"Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Mysteries of Prayer'","badge":"Insight"}],"83":[{"arabic":"عز وجل وتلك حجتنا آتيناها إبراهيم على قومه وقال تعالى قالوا يا نوح قد جادلتنا فأكثرت جدالنا وقال تعالى في قصة فرعون وما رب العالمين إلى قوله أولوا جئتك بشيء مبين وعلى الجملة فالقرآن من أوله إلى آخره محاجة مع الكفار فعمدة أدلة المتكلمين في التوحيد قوله تعالى {لو كان فيهما آلهة إلا الله لفسدتا} وفي النبوة {وإن كنتم في ريب مما نزلنا على عبدنا فأتوا بسورة من مثله} وفي البعث {قل يحييها الذي أنشأها أول مرة} إلى غير ذلك من الآيات والأدلة ولم تزل الرسل صلوات الله عليهم يحاجون المنكرين ويجادلونهم","english":"The mention of the proof (ḥujjah), the demand for it, and the search after it — how could these be prohibited, when God — may He be exalted — has said: {«قُلْ هَاتُوا بُرْهَانَكُمْ»} (Say: Bring forth your proof — Qur'an 2:111), and He — mighty and majestic — has said: {«لِيَهْلِكَ مَنْ هَلَكَ عَنْ بَيِّنَةٍ وَيَحْيَا مَنْ حَيَّ عَنْ بَيِّنَةٍ»} (So that he who perishes may perish upon clear evidence, and he who lives may live upon clear evidence — Qur'an 8:42), and He — may He be exalted — has said: {«هَلْ عِنْدَكُمْ مِنْ سُلْطَانٍ بِهَذَا»} (Have you any authority for this? — Qur'an 10:68), meaning a proof and demonstration, and He — may He be exalted — has said: {«قُلْ فَلِلَّهِ الْحُجَّةُ الْبَالِغَةُ»} (Say: To God belongs the conclusive proof — Qur'an 6:149), and He — may He be exalted — has said: {«أَلَمْ تَرَ إِلَى الَّذِي حَاجَّ إِبْرَاهِيمَ فِي رَبِّهِ»} (Have you not considered him who disputed with Abraham concerning his Lord? — Qur'an 2:258) up to His words: {«فَبُهِتَ الَّذِي كَفَرَ»} (So the one who disbelieved was confounded — Qur'an 2:258).","book_title":"Foundations of Belief","section_title_ar":"Section 77","section_title_en":"The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 77: On the Permissibility of Theological Discourse (Kalām) and Its Early Practice","section_index":77,"anchors":"- Root: ح-د-ث (ḥ-d-th)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Al-ḥudūth: the coming-to-be of a thing after it was not; al-ḥadath: the novel event; al-ḥīdāth: origination.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-ḥudūth: a thing's becoming after it was not, whether accident or substance; iḥdāth: bringing it into existence. The origination of substances belongs to God alone.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"Literal: 'He is a recent event among events.' Metaphorical: 'They became topics of conversation (aḥādīth).'\"\n- Root: ح-ا-ج (ḥ-ā-j)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Al-ḥājah: need of a thing, with desire for it; its plural: ḥājāt and ḥawā'ij.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-ḥājah ilā al-shay': poverty toward it with love of it. God said: {«إِلَّا حَاجَةً فِي نَفْسِ يَعْقُوبَ»} (except a need in Jacob's soul — Qur'an 12:68).\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"Literal: need and want. The 'ḥājah' is that which one seeks to obtain.\"\n- Root: ك-و-ن (k-w-n)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Kāna: to be, to exist; takwīn: bringing into being from non-existence.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-kawn: existence after non-existence; the muḥdath (originated thing) is that brought forth after it was not.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"Literal: being and becoming. Metaphorical: the continuous states of affairs.\"\n- Sibawayh Rule: Al-Faṣl bayna al-Jār wa-al-Majrūr (Prepositional Interposition) — The permissibility of separating a preposition from its object when the intervening clause is complete in meaning, as in the verse's structure of argumentation where the proof (ḥujjah) stands independently before its conclusion.","content_type":"afterlife","topic":"Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Foundations of Belief'","badge":"Afterlife"}],"88":[{"arabic":"ويهدي بها من يشاء فمن نظر في هذه الأمور من حيث إنها فعل الله تعالى وصنعه استفاد منه المعرفة بجلال الله تعالى وعظمته واهتدى به ومن نظر فيها قاصرا للنظر عليها من حيث تأثير بعضها فى بعض لا من حيث ارتباطها بمسبب الأسباب فقد شقى وارتدى فنعوذ بالله من الضلال ونسأله أن يجنبنا مزلة أقدام الجهال بمنه وكرمه وفضله وجوده ورحمته تم الكتاب التاسع من ربع المنجيات والحمد لله وحده وصلواته على محمد وآله وسلامه يتلوه كتاب ذكر الموت وما بعده وبه كمل جميع الديوان بحمد الله تعالى وكرم","english":"To proceed: Worthy is one whose place of downfall is death, whose bed is the dust, whose companion is the worm, whose boon-companions are Munkar and Nakīr, whose abode is the grave, whose resting-place is the belly of the earth, whose appointed time is the Resurrection, and whose destination is Paradise or the Fire — that he should have no thought except of death, no remembrance except of it, no preparation except for it, no deliberation except concerning it, no aspiration except toward it, no lingering except upon it, no concern except with it, no power except around it, and no waiting or tarrying except for it. And it is fitting that he should count himself among the dead and see himself among the inhabitants of the graves, for everything that is coming is near, and the distant is only that which is not coming. The Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, said: \"The shrewd one (al-kayyis) is he who subdues his soul and works for what comes after death\" (1). And preparation for a thing will not be facilitated except upon the renewal of its remembrance in the heart, and its remembrance is not renewed except through recollection by lending ear to those things that remind of it and by looking upon those things that alert to it.","book_title":"Contemplation","section_title_ar":"Section 1281","section_title_en":"The Book of the Remembrance of Death and What Follows It — Introduction and Opening Homily","section_index":1281,"anchors":"- Root: موت (m-w-t)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes cessation of vital faculty; death as transition, not annihilation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Death is the divinely ordained terminus of corporeal life, gateway to reckoning; \"أماته فأقبره\" {«عبس/ 21»} implies divine disposal of the deceased.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: bodily death and burial. Metaphorical: \"أحيا الله البلد الميت\" — revival of barren land; death of hearts through heedlessness.\n- Root: قبر (q-b-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Grave as the resting place of the dead; \"المقبرة\" as the site of interment.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"حتى زرتم المقابر\" {«التكاثر/ 2»} — metonymy for death itself; the grave as prelude to resurrection.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: burial of the deceased. Metaphorical: \"رفع قبراه\" — the haughty one who raises his nose like a grave-mound, signifying pride.\n- Root: حمد (ḥ-m-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Praise predicated upon volitional excellence; gratitude for beneficence.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): True praise (ḥamd) is due solely to God, whose acts are pure beneficence; distinct from mere thanks (shukr) for favors received.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"أحمد الله بجميع محامده\" — praising God with all praises. Metaphorical: \"أحمدت صنيعه\" — approving an act as praiseworthy.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The periodic opening \"الحمد لله الذي...\" employs a suspended genitive construction (جار ومجرور) with a relative clause (صلة الموصول) that permits extended expansion, governing the entire homiletic sequence that follows.","content_type":"spiritual","topic":"Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Contemplation'","badge":"Insight"}],"91":[{"arabic":"أن ينزله على نفسه في حق الله تعالى فإنه إذا تفكر فمعرفته جهل إذ ما قدروا الله حق قدره وطاعته رياء إذ لا يتقي الله حق تقاته وحبه معلول إذ لا يدع شهوة من شهواته في حبه ومن أراد الله به خيرا بصره بعيوب نفسه فيرى مصداق هذا البيت في نفسه وإن كان على المرتبة بالإضافة إلى الغافلين ولذلك قال صلى الله عليه وسلم لا أحصي ثناء عليك أنت كما أثنيت على نفسك (2) وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم إني لأستغفر الله في اليوم والليلة سبعين مرة (3) وإنما كان استغفاره عن أحوال هي درجات بعد بالإضافة إلى ما بعدها وإن كانت قربا بالإضافة إلى ما قبلها فلا قرب إلا ويبق","english":"**The second meaning** — that he descends it upon himself with respect to God Most High. For when he reflects, his knowledge is ignorance, since {«ما قدروا الله حق قدره»} (They did not measure God with His true measure) [Al-Anʿām 6:91]; and his obedience is ostentation (riyāʾ), since he does not {«يتقي الله حق تقاته»} (fear God as He deserves to be feared) [Āl ʿImrān 3:102]; and his love is defective, since he does not abandon any of his lusts in his love [of God]. And whoever God desires good for, He shows him the faults of his own soul, so he sees the confirmation of this verse in himself, even if he is upon a [high] rank in relation to the heedless. For this reason, the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, said: \"I do not enumerate praise upon You; You are as You have praised Yourself.\" (2) And he, peace and blessings be upon him, said: \"I indeed seek God's forgiveness seventy times in the day and the night.\" (3) His seeking forgiveness was only for states that are degrees [of closeness] in relation to what is beyond them, even though they are closeness in relation to what precedes them. For there is no closeness except that behind it remains a closeness that has no end, since the path of journeying to God Most High is without termination, and reaching the utmost limits of closeness is impossible.","book_title":"Audition and Ecstasy","section_title_ar":"Section 505","section_title_en":"The Book of the Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 505: On the Hidden Maladies of Praise and Blame (Continued)","section_index":505,"anchors":"- Root: سرر (s-r-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The concealed essence of a thing; that which is hidden from perception, whether good or evil.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-sirr (secret) is that which is concealed in the heart, and its plural asrār denotes the innermost hidden realities of the soul's states.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqīqah) for what is veiled from the senses; metaphorical (majāz) for the hidden motives and subtle deceptions of the self.\n- Root: عيب (ʿ-ī-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: A defect, blemish, or imperfection; to find fault with something, exposing its deficiency.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In the theological context, ʿayb denotes moral and spiritual deficiency that distances the servant from God, often concealed by self-deception.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for physical blemish; metaphorical for moral faults that the soul conceals from itself.\n- Root: شهد (sh-h-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To witness, to be present; shuhūd is presence with the eye or the heart.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الشهود والشهادة: الحضور مع المشاهدة، إما بالبصر، أو بالبصيرة\" — witnessing is presence with observation, either by the eye or by inner insight (basīrah).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for physical presence; metaphorical for the heart's absorption in contemplation of the Divine, where the witness is annihilated in the witnessed.\n- Root: فني (f-n-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To perish, to cease; fanāʾ is the passing away of a thing.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In the Sufi lexicon, fanāʾ ʿan al-nafs is the passing away of self-awareness, not ontological annihilation, but the cessation of attending to oneself.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Metaphorical (majāz) for the extinction of sensory and self-referential perception in the state of total absorption.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction and Specification) — The particle إنما restricts predication, and the construction \"فكأنه فنى عن كل شيء إلا عن الواحد المشهود\" employs exception (istithnāʾ) to affirm total annihilation save the One Witnessed, a syntactic structure that emphasizes exclusive focus.","content_type":"spiritual","topic":"Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Audition and Ecstasy'","badge":"Insight"},{"arabic":"إلا الواحد ولا يوجه وجهه إلا إليه وهو امتثال قوله تعالى {قل الله ثم ذرهم في خوضهم يلعبون} وليس المراد به القول باللسان فإنما اللسان ترجمان يصدق مرة ويكذب أخرى وإنما موقع نظر الله تعالى المترجم عنه هو القلب وهو معدن التوحيد ومنبعه اللفظ الرابع الذكر والتذكير فقد قال الله تعالى {وذكر فإن الذكرى تنفع المؤمنين} وقد ورد في الثناء على مجالس الذكر أخبار كثيرة كقوله صلى الله عليه وسلم إذا مررتم برياض الجنة فارتعوا قيل وما رياض الجنة قال مجالس الذكر (2) وفي الحديث إن لله تعالى ملائكة سياحين في الدنيا سوى ملائكة الخلق إذا رأوا مجالس الذكر ينادي بعض","english":"This [bankruptcy] is like the bankruptcy of one who rises at dawn, turns toward the qiblah, and says: \"I have turned my face toward Him Who originated the heavens and the earth, as a ḥanīf\" {«وجهت وجهي للذي فطر السموات والأرض حنيفا»} — and this is the very first lie with which he opens his address to God each day, unless the face of his heart is turned toward God — Exalted is He — exclusively and specifically. For if by \"face\" he means the outward, physical face, then his face is turned only toward the Kaʿbah, and he has merely diverted it from the other cardinal directions. But the Kaʿbah is not a direction (jihah) for the One Who originated the heavens and the earth, such that one who turns toward it would thereby be turning toward Him — far exalted is He above being bounded by directions and regions (aqṭār).","book_title":"Book of Knowledge","section_title_ar":"Section 29","section_title_en":"The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 29: On True Monotheism (Tawḥīd) and the Remembrance of God (Dhikr)","section_index":29,"anchors":"- Root: هوي (h-w-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الهوى: سقوط الشيء من علو إلى أسفل؛ ثم استعمل في ميل النفس إلى ما تلتذ به من غير داعية العقل\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الهوى: ميل النفس إلى ما تميل إليه من الشهوات، وقد ذم الله اتباعه في غير موضع من كتابه\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"هويته: أحببته\" — Metaphorical: \"فلان يهوي في الدين: يذهب فيه بغير بصيرة\"\n- Root: وجه (w-j-h)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الوجه: ما يستقبل به الشيء، وأول ما يبدو من الإنسان\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"ولما كان الوجه أول ما يستقبلك وأشرف ما في ظاهر البدن استعمل في مستقبل كل شيء، وعبر عن الذات بالوجه في قوله: {ويبقى وجه ربك ذو الجلال والإكرام}\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"وجه الثوب: ما استقبلك منه\" — Metaphorical: \"وجه القلب: توجهه وإخلاصه لله\"\n- Root: ذكر (dh-k-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الذكر: نقيض النسيان، والذكر: الثناء والقول المتردد على اللسان\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"الذكر: حضور المعنى في النفس، ويستعمل في القول الدال على ذلك، وفي الحفظ\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"ذكرت الشيء: استحضرته\" — Metaphorical: \"مجالس الذكر: المجالس التي يذكر فيها الله تعالى\"\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — \"إنما\" restricts the predicate to the subject alone, negating all else; applied here to confine true tawḥīd to the heart's orientation, not the tongue's utterance.","content_type":"spiritual","topic":"Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Book of Knowledge'","badge":"Insight"}],"93":[{"arabic":"الله تعالى بين من افترى على الله كذبا وبين من كذب بالحق فقال تعالى ومن أظلم ممن افترى على الله كذبا أو كذب بالحق لما جاءه وقال تعالى فمن أظلم ممن كذب على الله وكذب بالصدق إذ جاءه ومنها الرياء وملاحظة الخلق والجهد في استمالة قلوبهم وصرف وجوههم والرياء هو الداء العضال الذي يدعو إلى أكبر الكبائر كما سيأتي في كتاب الرياء والمناظر لا يقصد إلا الظهور عند الخلق وانطلاق ألسنتهم بالثناء عليه فهذه عشر خصال من أمهات الفواحش الباطنة سوى ما يتفق لغير المتماسكين منهم من الخصام المؤدي إلى الضرب","english":"**QUAD_ANCHORS:**\n- Root: نظر (naẓara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to look, to examine, to consider; al-munāẓarah is mutual examination/argumentation between two parties.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: naẓar as intellectual contemplation (taʾammul bi-l-baṣīrah); munāẓarah as dialectical exchange.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: sitting face-to-face in debate; Metaphorical: disputation for victory.\n- Root: غلب (ghalaba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to overcome, to conquer, to prevail.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ghalabah as seeking to dominate another in argument.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: victory in combat; Metaphorical: intellectual supremacy.\n- Root: حسد (ḥasada)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to envy, to wish removal of blessings from another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: envy as wishing deprivation of blessings from the deserving — among the basest traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: envy of another's blessing; Metaphorical: consuming fire of the soul.\n- Root: كبر (kabura)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be great, to be haughty.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-kibr as self-elevation above others; condemned by God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: greatness; Metaphorical: arrogance and pride.\n- Root: غيب (ghāba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be absent, to be hidden.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ghībah as mentioning another with what he dislikes in his absence — likened to eating carrion.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: backbiting; Metaphorical: consuming the flesh of one's brother.\n- Root: راءى (rāʾā)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to show off, to display.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-riyāʾ as performing acts for the sake of being seen — hidden shirk.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: showing good deeds; Metaphorical: seeking reputation.\n- Root: نفس (nafs)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: self, soul, spirit.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: tazkiyat al-nafs as self-praise and self-purification — forbidden by God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: self; Metaphorical: self-commendation.\n- Root: حقd (ḥaqida)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to bear malice, to harbor resentment.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ḥiqd as nursing enmity in the heart — negated from the believer.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: bearing grudge; Metaphorical: hidden rancor.\n- Root: نفاق (nifāq)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: hypocrisy, showing contrary to what one conceals.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: hypocrisy in deeds — outward display contrary to inward state.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: hypocrisy in faith; Metaphorical: hypocrisy in character.\n- Root: علم (ʿalima)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to know, to perceive.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: knowledge as apprehension of reality — its pursuit for other than God is destructive.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: knowledge; Metaphorical: the dangerous burden of the scholar.\n- Root: جدل (jadala)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to argue, to dispute.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-jidāl as contention; al-mirāʾ as disputation with falsehood — condemned.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: argumentation; Metaphorical: obstinate disputation.\n- Root: شرك (sharaka)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to associate, to share.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-riyāʾ as hidden shirk — associating others with God in intention.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: polytheism; Metaphorical: hidden association in deeds.\n- Root: موت (māta)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to die.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: death as separation of soul from body; spiritual death as ruin of the soul.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical death; Metaphorical: spiritual destruction.\n- Root: حيى (ḥayiya)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to live.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: life as the power of perception; eternal life as the reward of the righteous.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical life; Metaphorical: spiritual vitality.\n- Root: هلك (halaka)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to perish, to be destroyed.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-halāk as ruin; eternal destruction as everlasting punishment.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: destruction; Metaphorical: eternal ruin.\n- Root: رياء (riyāʾ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: showing off, ostentation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: riyāʾ as the incurable disease calling to the greatest sins.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: ostentation; Metaphorical: spiritual disease.\n- Root: جاه (jāh)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: rank, status, prestige.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: love of status (ḥubb al-jāh) among the destructive traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: position; Metaphorical: seeking prestige.\n- Root: مباهاة (mubāhāh)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: mutual boasting, vying in splendor.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: mubāhāh as each seeking to display superiority over the other.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: mutual boasting; Metaphorical: rivalry in honor.\n- Root: مماراة (mumārāh)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: disputation, wrangling.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-mirāʾ as contentious argument — condemned by the Prophet.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: disputation; Metaphorical: obstinate wrangling.\n- Root: فخر (fakhara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to boast, to pride oneself.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-fakhr as self-glorification — among the blameworthy traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: boasting; Metaphorical: vainglory.\n- Root: عجب (ʿajiba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to wonder, to be amazed.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ʿujb as self-admiration — among the destructive traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder; Metaphorical: self-conceit.\n- Root: بغض (baghiḍa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to hate, to detest.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-baghdāʾ as mutual hatred — contrary to brotherhood.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: hatred; Metaphorical: mutual enmity.\n- Root: غضب (ghaḍiba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be angry.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ghaḍab as anger — among the blameworthy traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: anger; Metaphorical: rage.\n- Root: طمع (ṭamiʿa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to covet, to desire.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ṭamaʿ as covetousness — among the destructive traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: desire; Metaphorical: greed.\n- Root: أشر (ashira)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be exultant, to be insolent.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ashar as excessive joy and insolence — blameworthy.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: exultation; Metaphorical: insolence.\n- Root: بطر (baṭira)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be arrogant, to be ungrateful.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-baṭar as arrogance in prosperity — among the destructive traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: arrogance; Metaphorical: ingratitude for blessings.\n- Root: خيلاء (khuyalāʾ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: pride, haughtiness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-khuyalāʾ as pride and conceit — blameworthy.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: pride; Metaphorical: haughty bearing.\n- Root: خشية (khashyah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: fear, awe.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-khashyah as reverential fear — among the traits of the believers.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: fear; Metaphorical: reverential awe.\n- Root: رحمة (raḥmah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: mercy, compassion.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-raḥmah as tenderness of heart — among the traits of the believers.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: mercy; Metaphorical: compassion.\n- Root: غفلة (ghaflah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: heedlessness, inattention.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ghaflah as heedlessness — contrary to vigilance.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: inattention; Metaphorical: spiritual heedlessness.\n- Root: خشوع (khushūʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: humility, submissiveness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-khushūʿ as humility — among the traits of the believers.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: humility; Metaphorical: devotional submission.\n- Root: تذكير (tadhkīr)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: reminding, admonishing.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-tadhkīr as reminding of God — a righteous act.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: reminding; Metaphorical: preaching.\n- Root: وعظ (waʿaẓa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to admonish, to exhort.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-waʿẓ as exhortation to obedience and warning against disobedience.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: admonition; Metaphorical: sermonizing.\n- Root: فتوى (fatwā)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: legal ruling, formal opinion.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-fatwā as clarification of legal rulings.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: legal opinion; Metaphorical: religious authority.\n- Root: قضاء (qaḍāʾ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: judgment, decree.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-qaḍāʾ as judgment between people.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: judgment; Metaphorical: judicial authority.\n- Root: وقف (waqafa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to stop, to stand.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-waqf as endowment of property for charitable purposes.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: standing; Metaphorical: religious endowment.\n- Root: ثواب (thawāb)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: reward, recompense.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-thawāb as divine recompense for righteous deeds.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: reward; Metaphorical: divine recompense.\n- Root: عذاب (ʿadhāb)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: punishment, torment.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ʿadhāb as divine punishment for the disobedient.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: punishment; Metaphorical: divine chastisement.\n- Root: ملك (malaka)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to possess, to rule.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-mulk as sovereignty; al-mulk al-muʾabbad as eternal sovereignty in Paradise.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: dominion; Metaphorical: eternal kingdom.\n- Root: نعيم (naʿīm)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: bliss, delight.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-naʿīm as bliss; al-naʿīm al-sarmad as everlasting bliss.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: delight; Metaphorical: eternal bliss.\n- Root: رخصة (rukhaṣah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: concession, dispensation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-rukhaṣah as legal concession.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: concession; Metaphorical: legal dispensation.\n- Root: رياسة (riyāsah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: leadership, headship.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: love of leadership among the destructive traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: leadership; Metaphorical: seeking authority.\n- Root: اندراس (indirās)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: effacement, obliteration.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: indirās al-ʿulūm as the obliteration of the sciences.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: effacement; Metaphorical: extinction of knowledge.\n- Root: صبيان (ṣibyān)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: boys, children.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: children's motivation for learning through play.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: boys; Metaphorical: pupils.\n- Root: مكتب (maktab)\n  * Lisan / Ayn- Root: مكتب (maktab)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: place of instruction, school for boys.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: the elementary school where children are taught.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: school; Metaphorical: place of elementary learning.\n- Root: خلاق (khilāq)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: portion, share, lot.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: \"no portion\" (lā khilāq) in the Hereafter — utter deprivation of divine reward.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: share; Metaphorical: spiritual allotment in the afterlife.\n- Root: فاجر (fājir)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: wicked, immoral, profligate.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: the dissolute one who commits sins openly.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: sinner; Metaphorical: one through whom God nonetheless supports religion.\n- Root: شمع (shamʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: candle, wax taper.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: the candle as parable — consuming itself while giving light to others.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: candle; Metaphorical: one who destroys himself but benefits others.\n- Root: نار (nār)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: fire.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: fire as parable — consuming itself and all around it.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: fire; Metaphorical: one who destroys himself and others.\n- Root: سعد (saʿida)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be fortunate, to prosper.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: saʿādah as felicity — success in the Hereafter.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: good fortune; Metaphorical: eternal felicity.\n- Root: شقي (shaqiya)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be wretched, to be miserable.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: shaqāwah as wretchedness — ruin in the Hereafter.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: misery; Metaphorical: eternal ruin.\n- Root: أخلص (akhlaṣa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to purify, to be sincere.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: ikhlāṣ as purifying intention for God alone — the condition of acceptance.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: sincerity; Metaphorical: pure devotion.\n- Root: ريب (rāba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to doubt, to suspect.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-rayb as doubt and uncertainty — to be dispelled by evidence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: doubt; Metaphorical: skepticism.","book_title":"Book of Knowledge","section_title_ar":"Section 39","section_title_en":"Section 39","section_index":39,"anchors":"*(Anchors established)*","content_type":"ethics","topic":"Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Book of Knowledge'","badge":"Ethics"},{"arabic":"أيديهم إلى قوله وكنتم عن آياته تستكبرون {ثم قال} ادخلوا أبواب جنهنم خالدين فيها فبئس مثوى المتكبرين ثم أخبر أن أشد أهل النار عذابا أشدهم عتيا على الله تعالى فقال ثم لننزعن من كل شيعة أيهم أشد على الرحمن عتيا وقال تعالى فالذين لا يؤمنون بالآخرة قلوبهم منكرة وهم مستكبرون وقال عز وجل يقول الذين استضعفوا للذين استكبروا لولا أنتم لكنا مؤمنين وقال تعالى إن الذين يستكبرون عن عبادتي سيدخلون جهنم داخرين وقال تعالى سأصرف عن آياتي الذين يتكبرون في الأرض بغير الحق قيل في التفسير سأرفع فهم القرآن عن قل","english":"When this [pride] appears upon the bodily limbs, it is called \"behaving arrogantly\" (takabbur); and when it does not appear [outwardly], it is said that in his soul there is pride (kibr). The root [of the matter] is the moral trait (khuluq) that resides in the soul — namely, the soul's taking comfort and repose in seeing itself as above the one over whom it acts proudly. For pride necessarily requires an object over whom one is proud and a quality by virtue of which one is proud; and by this [requirement], pride is distinguished from self-conceit ('ujb), as will come later. For self-conceit does not require an object other than the self-conceited one. Indeed, were a human being created alone, it is conceivable that he would be self-conceited, but it is not conceivable that he would be proud except when there is another alongside him, and he sees himself as above that other in the attributes of perfection. Only at that point does he become proud. Nor is it sufficient that he deem himself great in order to be proud — for a man may deem himself great yet see another as greater than himself or equal to himself, and so he does not act proudly toward him. Nor is it sufficient that he hold another in contempt — for even so, if he saw himself as more contemptible [than that other], he would not be proud; and if he saw the other as his equal, he would not be proud. Rather, he must assign to himself a rank and to the other a rank, then see his own rank as above the other's rank. Upon these three beliefs, the trait of pride comes to be in him. It is not that this perception alone negates pride; rather, this perception and this conviction blow into him [a wind of vanity], and there arises in his heart self-complacency, a thrill, joy, and repose in what he has come to believe, and a sense of vainglory in himself on account of that. That vainglory, that thrill, and that repose in the belief — that is the trait of pride. For this reason, the Prophet — God's peace and blessings be upon him — said: \"I seek refuge in You from the puff of pride (nafkhat al-kibriyā').\" And the hadith has preceded: \"None shall enter Paradise who has in his heart the weight of a mustard seed of pride.\"","book_title":"Condemnation of Pride and Conceit","section_title_ar":"Section 854","section_title_en":"The Reality of Pride (Kibr) and Its Manifestation in the Soul and Limbs","section_index":854,"anchors":"- Root: كبر (K-B-R)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of bigness, greatness, and seniority in age or rank; al-Farahidi notes its use for physical growth and maturity.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الكبير والصغير من الأسماء المتضايفة\" — great and small are relative terms, applied by comparison; in Kalam, pride (kibr) is a state of the soul that esteems itself above another in perfection.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"كبر الأمر، وخطب كبير\" — to be great in magnitude. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"كبر عليه\" — to find something burdensome or grievous; the moral-spiritual sense of kibr is a metaphorical extension of physical magnitude to the soul's self-assessment.\n- Root: نفس (N-F-S)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, soul, or spirit; that which animates and is the seat of inner states.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النفس: الروح\" — the soul; also the very essence (al-dhat), as in \"ويحذركم الله نفسه\" {آل عمران/ 30}, where His \"self\" is His essence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"شيء نفيس\" — precious thing. Metaphorical: \"دفق نفسه أي دمه\" — \"his self\" for \"his blood\"; here, the soul's inner disposition (khuluq) is the locus where pride takes root before it appears on the limbs.\n- Root: وفي (W-F-Y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Completeness, fulfillment, and reaching full measure.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوافي: الذي بلغ التمام\" — that which attains completion; \"أوفى بعهده\" — to fulfill one's covenant fully.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"درهم واف\" — a full-weight dirham. Metaphorical: \"أوفى على المائة\" — to exceed a hundred; in this text, the \"full measure of a mustard seed\" (مثقال ذرة) of pride is the complete, realized quantity that bars one from Paradise.\n- Root: خلق (Kh-L-Q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Measuring, shaping, and fashioning; also innate disposition and moral character.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الخلق أصله: التقدير المستقيم\" — its root is right measure and estimation; used for God's origination of things and for the moral constitution of the soul.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"خلق الخراز الأديم\" — the leatherworker measures the hide before cutting. Metaphorical: \"خلق الله الخلق\" — God originated creation upon a measure dictated by wisdom; hence khuluq is the soul's fixed moral form.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction and Focused Predication) — used to delimit the precise definition of kibr as a specific soul-state distinct from 'ujb (self-conceit), and to restrict the cause of exclusion from Paradise to the presence of pride in the heart.","content_type":"afterlife","topic":"Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Condemnation of Pride and Conceit'","badge":"Afterlife"},{"arabic":"التهلكة ولا يتمنى على الله غير الحق فإن حفظت وصيتي هذه فلا يكون غائب أحب إليك من الموت ولا بد لك منه وإن ضيعت وصيتي فلا يكون غائب أبغض إليك من الموت ولا بد لك منه ولست بمعجزه وقال سعيد بن المسيب لما احتضر أبو بكر رضي الله عنه أتاه ناس من الصحابة فقالوا يا خليفة رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم زودنا فإنا نراك لما بك فقالوا أبو بكر من قال هؤلاء الكلمات ثم مات جعل الله روحه في الأفق المبين قالوا وما الأفق المبين قال قاع بين يديالعرش فيه رياض الله وأنهار وأشجار يغشاه كل يوم ما","english":"When Abu Bakr (may Allah Most High be pleased with him) was in the throes of death, ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) came and recited this verse: \"By your life, wealth avails not the youth... when the breath rattles in his chest one day, and the breast is straitened thereby.\"","book_title":"Remembrance of Death and Afterlife","section_title_ar":"Section 1302","section_title_en":"The Death of Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (may Allah Most High be pleased with him)","section_index":1302,"anchors":"- Root: حشرج (ḥashraja)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: [Core linguistic root meaning: The rattling, gurgling sound of the death-throe in the throat; the final breath struggling in the chest.]\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): [Theological/Kalam semantic nuance: The terminal state of the soul's separation from the body, the moment of overwhelming divine decree.]\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): [Literal vs Metaphorical distinction: Literal — the physical rattle of the dying; Metaphorical — the constriction of the breast as the soul is drawn forth.]\n- Root: سكر (sakara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: [Core linguistic root meaning: The veiling of the intellect; intoxication; the stupor that overcomes one at the onset of death.]\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): [Theological/Kalam semantic nuance: The stupor of death (sakrat al-mawt) as the removal of all veils, wherein the truth of the Hereafter becomes undeniably present.]\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): [Literal vs Metaphorical distinction: Literal — intoxication from drink; Metaphorical — the overwhelming bewilderment of the soul at death's onset.]\n- Root: ذمم (dhimam)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: [Core linguistic root meaning: The covenant of protection; the bond of safe-conduct; that which one is obligated to honor and safeguard.]\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): [Theological/Kalam semantic nuance: The divine guarantee (dhimmat Allah) extended to the believer who prays the dawn prayer — a sacred pact not to be violated.]\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): [Literal vs Metaphorical distinction: Literal — a pledge of security between men; Metaphorical — the inviolable covenant between the servant and his Lord.]\n- Root: وزن (wazana)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: [Core linguistic root meaning: To weigh; the balance; the measure of equivalence between two things.]\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): [Theological/Kalam semantic nuance: The weighing of deeds on the Day of Resurrection as the manifestation of divine justice — the scales (mawāzīn) receiving only truth or falsehood.]\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): [Literal vs Metaphorical distinction: Literal — the physical act of weighing; Metaphorical — the spiritual weighing of deeds by their conformity to truth (ḥaqq) or falsehood (bāṭil).]\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — [The particle innamā restricts the predicate exclusively to its subject, as in Abu Bakr's dictum: \"It is only that the scales of those whose scales are heavy on the Day of Resurrection are weighed down by their following the truth in this world.\"]","content_type":"afterlife","topic":"Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'","badge":"Afterlife"}],"94":[{"arabic":"يقال ولقد جئتمونا فرادى كما خلقناكم أول مرة وفيها يقال كفى بنفسك اليوم عليك حسيبا أما في القيامة الكبرى الجامعة لكافة الخلائق فلا يكون وحده بل ربما يحاسب على ملأ من الخلق وفيها يساق المتقون إلى الجنة والمجرمون إلى النار زمرا لا آحادا والهول الأول هو هول القيامة الصغرى ولجميع أهوال القيامة الكبرى نظير في القيامة الصغرى مثل زلزلة الأرض مثلا فإن أرضك الخاصة بك تزلزل في الموت فإنك تعلم أن الزلزلة إذا نزلت ببلدة صدق أن يقال قد زلزلت أرضهم وإن لم تزلزل البلاد المحيطة بها بل لو زلزل مسكن الإنسا","english":"As for the noble ones (al-kirām), it is because of the servant's benefit from their nobility, and because all the angels are noble and righteous (kirām bararah). As for the two writers (al-kātibān), it is for the recording of the good deeds and the evil deeds. They write only upon scrolls folded within the secret of the heart (sirr al-qalb) and folded away from the secret of the heart, so that no one may behold them in this world. For verily, they, their writings, their script, their pages, and the totality of what pertains to them belong to the unseen world and the dominion of sovereignty (ʿālam al-ghayb wa al-malakūt), not to the world of sensory witness (ʿālam al-shahādah). And everything belonging to the world of sovereignty (al-malakūt) is imperceptible to corporeal eyes in this world. Then these scrolls, folded away from him, are unfolded twice: once at the minor resurrection (al-qiyāmah al-ṣughrā) and once at the major resurrection (al-qiyāmah al-kubrā). By the minor resurrection I mean the state of death, for the Prophet, may Allah bless him and grant him peace, said: \"Whoever has died, his resurrection has indeed come to pass\" {«من مات فقد قامت قيامته»}. In this resurrection, the servant is alone, and at that time it is said: {«وَلَقَدْ جِئْتُمُونَا فُرَادَىٰ كَمَا خَلَقْنَاكُمْ أَوَّلَ مَرَّةٍ»} (\"And indeed you have come to Us alone, just as We created you the first time\") — and in it, it is said: {«كَفَىٰ بِنَفْسِكَ الْيَوْمَ عَلَيْكَ حَسِيبًا»} (\"Sufficient is your own soul this day as a reckoner against you\"). As for the major resurrection, which gathers all of creation, he is not alone therein; rather, he may be reckoned before a multitude of creatures, and in it the God-fearing are driven to Paradise and the criminals to the Fire in groups (zumar), not as individuals. The first great terror is the terror of the minor resurrection, and for all the terrors of the major resurrection there is a counterpart in the minor resurrection — such as the earthquake of the earth, for example. For your own particular earth quakes at death, since you know that when an earthquake descends upon a town, it is truly said that \"their earth has quaked,\" even if the surrounding lands have not quaked. Nay, even if only a man's dwelling quakes, the earthquake has occurred relative to him, because he is harmed by the quaking of the entire earth only through the quaking of his own dwelling, not through the quaking of another's dwelling. Thus his share of the earthquake has been fully realized without any diminution. Know that you are an earth created from dust, and your particular share of the dust is only your body. As for the body of another, it is not your share. The earth upon which you sit, in relation to your body, is but a vessel and a place. You fear its quaking only because your body might quake on account of it; otherwise, the air is perpetually in motion and you do not dread it, since your body does not quake because of it. So your share of the earthquake of the entire earth is only the quaking of your body — it is your earth and your particular dust. Your bones are the mountains of your earth; your head is the sky of your earth; your heart is the sun of your earth; your hearing, your sight, and the rest of your faculties are the stars of your sky; the outpouring of sweat from your body is the sea of your earth; your hair is the vegetation of your earth; your limbs are the trees of your earth; and so on to all of your parts. When the pillars of your body collapse through death, then the earth has quaked with its quaking {«إِذَا زُلْزِلَتِ الْأَرْضُ زِلْزَالَهَا»}. When the bones separate from the flesh, then the earth has borne its burdens and the mountains have been crushed to powder {«وَحُمِلَتِ الْأَرْضُ وَالْجِبَالُ فَدُكَّتَا دَكَّةً وَاحِدَةً»}. When the bones crumble, then the mountains have been blown away as dust {«وَنُسِفَتِ الْجِبَالُ نَسْفًا»}. When your heart darkens at death, then the sun has been folded up {«كُوِّرَتِ الشَّمْسُ تَكْوِيرًا»}. When your hearing, sight, and all your senses cease, then the stars have been scattered {«وَانْكَدَرَتِ النُّجُومُ انْكِدَارًا»}. When your brain splits, then the sky has been split asunder {«انْشَقَّتِ السَّمَاءُ انْشِقَاقًا»}. When the sweat of your brow bursts forth from the terror of death, then the seas have been caused to burst forth {«فُجِّرَتِ الْبِحَارُ تَفْجِيرًا»}. When one of your legs entwines with the other — and they are your two mounts — then the pregnant she-camels have been neglected {«عُطِّلَتِ الْعِشَارُ تَعْطِيلًا»}. When the soul departs from the body, then the earth has been lifted and stretched until it casts out what is within it and empties itself {«وَأَلْقَتْ مَا فِيهَا وَتَخَلَّتْ»}. I shall not prolong the enumeration of all the corresponding states and terrors, but I say: by mere death, this minor resurrection comes to pass over you, and nothing of the major resurrection that pertains specifically to you escapes you — rather, only what pertains to another escapes you. For what benefit is the survival of the stars to another, when your senses — through which you benefited by gazing at the stars — have been scattered? The blind man finds night and day equal, and the eclipse of the sun and its clearing likewise, for the sun has been eclipsed for him all at once — that being his share of it — while its clearing thereafter is the share of another. And he whose head has been split open has had his sky split, for the sky is but an expression for what lies in the direction of the head; so whoever has no head has no sky — whence, then, would the survival of the sky for another benefit him? This, then, is the minor resurrection; and the fear after it is greater, and the terror after it is deferred — that is when the Great Calamity arrives {«الطَّامَّةُ الْكُبْرَىٰ»}, particularity is lifted, the heavens and the earth are abolished, the mountains are blown away, and the terrors multiply.","book_title":"Patience and Gratitude","section_title_ar":"Section 957","section_title_en":"The Minor Resurrection: The Microcosmic Earthquake of Death","section_index":957,"anchors":"- Root: كرم (karuma)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning of nobility, generosity, and honor; al-kirām are those distinguished by noble character and beneficence.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: karāmah denotes the honor bestowed by Allah upon His angels and righteous servants, entailing both dignity and generous bestowal.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): noble, generous in character; Metaphorical (Majaz): the angels described as kirām because of their exalted station and pure obedience, not corporeal nobility.\n- Root: كتب (kataba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To write, inscribe, decree; kitāb as a written record; kataba as the act of fixing something in a permanent form.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-kātibūn are the recording angels (ḥafaẓah) who inscribe deeds; writing here signifies divine preservation of acts in a register accessible to Allah alone.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical inscription on sheets; Metaphorical: the \"writing\" upon the heart's secret core is an immaterial imprinting (naqsh rūḥānī) imperceptible to corporeal sight.\n- Root: موت (mawta)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Death as cessation of animal life; the separation of the soul from the body.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-mawt is not annihilation but a transition (intiqlāl) from one abode to another; the minor resurrection (al-qiyāmah al-ṣughrā) is the individual's death, as per the prophetic saying.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: biological death; Metaphorical: \"whoever dies, his resurrection has come to pass\" — the term qiyāmah is transferred (majāz) to the moment of death because the full unveiling of one's deeds begins then.\n- Root: زلزل (zalzala)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Violent shaking, earthquake; intense agitation of the earth.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: the earthquake is a cosmic sign of divine power; in the microcosm, the death-throes of the body are the individual's own \"earthquake.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: seismic tremor of the earth; Metaphorical: the dissolution of one's own body is a true earthquake relative to that person, since only his own frame is his \"earth\" — the rest of the globe is another's concern.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب التشبيه والتمثيل (Analogy and Proportional Correspondence) — The syntactic structure equates parts of the human microcosm with the macrocosmic phenomena of the Hour, using fa- (consequence) clauses to map each bodily dissolution to its corresponding cosmic portent.","content_type":"afterlife","topic":"Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Patience and Gratitude'","badge":"Afterlife"},{"arabic":"المؤمنين قال أجدني كما قال الله تعالى {ولقد جئتمونا فرادى كما خلقناكم أول مرة وتركتم ما خولناكم وراء ظهوركم} الآية ومات وقالت فاطمة بنت عبد الملك بن مروان امرأة عمر بن عبد العزيز كنت أسمع عمر في مرضه الذي مات فيه يقول اللهم أخف عليهم موتي ولو ساعة من نهار فلما كان اليوم الذي قبض فيه خرجت من عنده فجلست في بيت آخر بيني وبينه باب وهو في قبة له فسمعته يقول {تلك الدار الآخرة نجعلها للذين لا يريدون علوا في الأرض ولا فسادا والعاقبة للمتقين} ثم هدا فجعلت لا أسمع حركة ولا كلاما فقلت لوصيف له انظر أنائم هو فلما دخل صاح فوثبت فإذا هو ميت وقيل له لما حضره الموت أ","english":"I say: Know that when death came to Muʿāwiyah ibn Abī Sufyān, he said, \"Seat me upright.\" So they seated him, and he began to glorify God Almighty and remember Him. Then he wept and said, \"Do you remember your Lord, O Muʿāwiyah, only after decrepitude and decline? Should this not have been while the branch of youth was fresh and verdant?\" And he wept until his weeping grew loud, then said, \"O Lord, have mercy on the sinful elder, the possessor of the hard heart. O Allah, overlook the misstep and forgive the slip, and repay with Your forbearance the one who hopes in none but You and trusts in none other than You.\"","book_title":"Remembrance of Death and Afterlife","section_title_ar":"Section 1306","section_title_en":"The Fifth Chapter: On the Deathbed Utterances of the Caliphs, Princes, and the Righteous","section_index":1306,"anchors":"- Root: حضر (ḥaḍara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of presence, attendance, or arrival at a place or time.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Often used for the arrival of an inevitable event, especially death (ḥuḍūr al-mawt), marking a temporal threshold.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for physical presence; metaphorical for the onset of an inescapable decree.\n- Root: عهد (ʿahida)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To covenant, to be charged with a trust, to make a bequest.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Implies a binding injunction, often divine or paternal, that outlasts the speaker.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for a written pact; metaphorical for the final counsel of the dying.\n- Root: وتر (watara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To make single, to sever, to deprive; also odd-numbered.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In the passive (wutirat), signifies being left bereft or defrauded of one's due.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for cutting off; metaphorical for the world's betrayal and stripping of its gifts.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — Governs the permissible separation of a preposition from its object for rhetorical emphasis, as seen in the dying men's abrupt, heartfelt exclamations.","content_type":"afterlife","topic":"Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'","badge":"Afterlife"}],"96":[{"arabic":"ثم يبعثكم فيه ليقضى أجل مسمى} (11) اللهم فالق الإصباح وجاعل الليل سكنا والشمس والقمر حسبانا أسألك خير هذا اليوم وخير ما فيه وأعوذ بك من شره وشر ما فيه (12) بسم الله ما شاء الله لا قوة إلا بالله ما شاء الله","english":"When you rise in the morning and hear the call to prayer (adhān), it is recommended for you to respond to the caller of prayer (mu'adhdhin) — and we have already mentioned this, along with the supplications for entering and leaving the privy, and the supplications of ablution (wuḍū'), in the Book of Purification. When you set out toward the mosque, say: \"O Allah, place light (nūr) in my heart, and light in my tongue, and place light in my hearing, and place light in my sight, and place light behind me, and light before me, and place light above me. O Allah, grant me light.\" And also say: \"O Allah, I ask You by the right of those who ask of You, and by the right of this my walking unto You — for I have not gone out in insolence, nor in vanity, nor for show, nor for reputation. I have gone out seeking protection from Your wrath and seeking Your good pleasure. So I ask You to deliver me from the Fire and to forgive me my sins — verily, none forgives sins but You.\"","book_title":"On Invocations and Supplications","section_title_ar":"Section 254","section_title_en":"Section 254 — Supplications for the Hours of Day and Night: From Dawn to Sleep","section_index":254,"anchors":"- Root: ن و ر (n-w-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: النور: الضوء المنتشر الذي يُبصر به، وهو ضد الظلمة\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): النور في القرآن يقع على ضروب: منها المعقول كالإيمان والحكمة، قال تعالى: {قد جاءكم من الله نور وكتاب مبين}، ومنها المحسوس كضوء الشمس والقمر\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): النور حقيقة في الضوء المحسوس، ومجاز في الهدى والإيمان والعلم — فدعاء النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم بطلب النور يشمل المعنيين جميعاً\n- Root: س ج د (s-j-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: السجود: وضع الجبهة على الأرض تعظيماً، وأصله التطامن والانخفاض\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): السجود ضربان: سجود عبادة وهو وضع الجبهة على الأرض تقرباً، وسجود طاعة وهو الانقياد والخضوع — قال تعالى: {ولله يسجد من في السموات والأرض طوعاً وكرهاً}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): سجد حقيقة في وضع الجبهة، ومجاز في الخضوع والانقياد — وقوله \"سجد لك سوادي وخيالي\" من المجاز المرسل\n- Root: ذ ك ر (dh-k-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: الذكر: نقيض النسيان، والذكر: الصوت الذي يُذكر به الشيء، والذكر: الشرف والصيت\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): الذكر ضربان: ذكر بالقلب وهو التفكر في عظمة الله، وذكر باللسان وهو التلفظ بأسمائه — وأعلى مراتبه ما اجتمع فيه القلب واللسان\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): الذكر حقيقة في استحضار الشيء بالقلب أو اللسان، ومجاز في الصلاة والدعاء والثناء\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — جواز تقديم المعمول وتأخيره مع بقاء العامل عاملاً في المعنى، كقوله \"لك الحمد\" حيث تقدم الجار والمجرور على المبتدأ للاختصاص والاهتمام","content_type":"dua","topic":"Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'On Invocations and Supplications'","badge":"Du'a"}],"97":[{"arabic":"منازل} وقال تعالى {وهو الذي جعل لكم النجوم لتهتدوا بها في ظلمات البر والبحر} فلا تظنن أن المقصود من سير الشمس والقمر بحسبان منظوم مرتب ومن خلق الظل والنور والنجوم أن يستعان بها على أمور الدنيا بل لتعرف بها مقادير الأوقات فتشتغل فيها بالطاعات والتجارة للدار الآخرة يدلك عليه قوله تعالى {وهو الذي جعل الليل والنهار خلفة لمن أراد أن يذكر أو أراد شكورا} أي يخلف أحدهما الآخر ليتدارك في أحدهما ما فات في الآخر وبين أن ذلك للذكر والشكر لا غير وقال تعالى {وجعلنا الليل والنهار آيتين فمحونا آية الليل وجعلنا آية النهار مبصرة لت","english":"{«آناء الليل ساجدا وقائما يحذر الآخرة ويرجو رحمة ربه قل هل يستوي الذين يعلمون والذين لا يعلمون»} — \"During the hours of the night, prostrating and standing, fearing the Hereafter and hoping for the mercy of his Lord. Say: Are those who know equal to those who do not know?\" (Q. 39:9). And God Most High said: {«تتجافى جنوبهم عن المضاجع يدعون ربهم خوفا وطمعا»} — \"Their sides forsake their beds, calling upon their Lord in fear and hope\" (Q. 32:16). And God, Mighty and Majestic, said: {«والذين يبيتون لربهم سجدا وقياما»} — \"And those who pass the night prostrating and standing before their Lord\" (Q. 25:64). And God, Mighty and Majestic, said: {«كانوا قليلا من الليل ما يهجعون وبالأسحار هم يستغفرون»} — \"They used to sleep but little of the night, and in the dawn-hours they would seek forgiveness\" (Q. 51:17-18). And God, Mighty and Majestic, said: {«فسبحان الله حين تمسون وحين تصبحون»} — \"So glory be to God when you enter the evening and when you enter the morning\" (Q. 30:17). And God Most High said: {«ولا تطرد الذين يدعون ربهم بالغداة والعشي يريدون وجهه»} — \"And do not drive away those who call upon their Lord morning and evening, desiring His Face\" (Q. 6:52). All of this makes clear to you that the path to God Most High is the watchfulness over the times (murāqabat al-awqāt) and the cultivation of them with the appointed devotions (al-awrād) in a perpetual manner.","book_title":"Arrangement of Litanies","section_title_ar":"Section 258","section_title_en":"The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 258: The Virtues of the Watches (Awrād) and Their Ordering","section_index":258,"anchors":"- Root: ورد (w-r-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is \"to arrive at water\" — the quenching of thirst at a watering place; al-Wird (pl. Awrād) denotes a set portion or appointed round of devotional recitation, by extension from the regular \"arrival\" at a source.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-Wurūd is originally \"the intending of water,\" then used for other things. The theological nuance: the awrād are the appointed \"watering places\" of the soul — fixed times at which the servant presents himself before God to drink from the fountain of remembrance (dhikr).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"warda al-mā'a\" — to arrive at water. Metaphorical (Majāz): \"warada ʿalayhi amrun lam yuṭiqhu\" — an affair he could not bear arrived upon him; \"waradat-hu al-ḥummā\" — fever came upon him. Here, the awrād are the appointed times that \"come upon\" the servant, demanding their due of devotion.\n- Root: طلع (ṭ-l-ʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To rise, to appear above the horizon (of the sun); to ascend; to come upon suddenly.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Ṭalaʿa al-shams ṭulūʿan wa-maṭlaʿan\" — the sun rose. From this is borrowed: \"ṭalaʿa ʿalaynā fulān\" — so-and-so came upon us. The maṭlaʿ is the place of rising.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"ṭalaʿat al-shams\" — the sun rose. Metaphorical: \"ṭalaʿa ʿalaynā fulān\" — he descended upon us suddenly. The \"rising of the sun\" is the boundary-marker of the first watch.\n- Root: ليل (l-y-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The night — from the time of sunset to sunrise; its root suggests \"enveloping\" and \"concealing.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The night (al-layl) and the day (al-nahār) are two signs (āyatān) of God; the night is for stillness and the day for seeking provision.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the period of darkness. Metaphorical: \"layl al-hamm\" — the night of anxiety (a dark affair). The night is divided into watches for the one who stands in prayer.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Bāb al-ḥaṣr wa-al-taqyīd bi-innamā (Restriction and Focused Predication) — The particle innamā restricts the predicate to the subject alone, negating all else. Applied here: \"the purpose of the sun and moon's course is not for worldly matters, but only (innamā) for knowing the measures of the times.\"","content_type":"spiritual","topic":"Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Arrangement of Litanies'","badge":"Insight"}],"101":[{"arabic":"الأول والآخر والظاهر والباطن وهو بكل شيء عليم التنزيه وأنه ليس بجسم مصور ولا جوهر محدود مقدر وأنه لا يماثل والأجسام ولا في التقدير ولا في قبول الانقسام وأنه ليس بجوهر ولا تحله الجواهر ولا بعرض ولا تحله الأعراض بل لا يماثل موجودا ولا يماثله موجود ليس كمثله شيء ولا هو مثل شيء وأنه لا يحده المقدار ولا تحويه الأقطار ولا تحيط به الجهات ولا تكتنفه الأرضون ولا السموات وأنه مستو على العرش على الوجه الذي قاله وبالمعنى الذي أراده استواء منزها عن المماسة والاستقرار والتمكن والحلول والانتقال لا يحمله ال","english":"- **Root: خلق (khalaqa)**\n  - *Lisan / Ayn:* Core meaning of measuring, proportioning, and fashioning; al-Khalil notes the root's application to the act of creation as bringing forth upon a measured determination.\n  - *Al-Raghib (Mufradat):* \"الخلق أصله: التقدير المستقيم\" — Creation fundamentally signifies right/straight measurement; used for bringing into being from nothing (ibdāʿ) as in {خلق السماوات والأرض}, and for bringing forth from something as in {خلقكم من نفس واحدة}.\n  - *Al-Zamakhshari (Asas):* Literal (ḥaqīqah): the craftsman's measuring before cutting. Metaphorical (majāz): \"خلق الله الخلق: أوجده على تقدير أوجبته الحكمة\" — God's creation is bringing into existence upon a measure necessitated by wisdom.","book_title":"Foundations of Belief","section_title_ar":"Section 72","section_title_en":"Section 72","section_index":72,"anchors":"*(Anchors established)*","content_type":"general","topic":"Classical commentary from Al-Ghazali's Ihya Ulum al-Din, from 'Foundations of Belief'","badge":"Commentary"}],"102":[{"arabic":"وهو مع ذلك قريب من كل موجود وهو أقرب إلى العبد من حبل الوريد وهو على كل شيء شهيد إذ لا يماثل قربه قرب الأجسام كما لا تماثل ذاته ذات الأجسام وأنه لا يحل في شيء ولا يحل فيه شيء تعالى عن أن يحويه مكان كما تقدس عن أن يحده زمان بل كان قبل أن خلق الزمان والمكان وهو الآن على ما عليه كان وأنه بائن عن خلقه بصفاته ليس في ذاته سواه ولا في سواه ذاته وأنه مقدس عن التغير والانتقال لا تحله الحوادث ولا تعتريه العوارض بل لا يزال في نعوت جلاله منزها عن الزوال وفي صفات كماله مستغنيا عن زيادة الاستكمال وأنه في ذاته معلوم الوجود بالعقول مرئ","english":"- **Root: خلق (khalaqa)**\n  - *Lisan / Ayn:* Core meaning of measuring, proportioning, and fashioning; al-Khalil notes the root's application to the act of creation as bringing forth upon a measured determination.\n  - *Al-Raghib (Mufradat):* \"الخلق أصله: التقدير المستقيم\" — Creation fundamentally signifies right/straight measurement; used for bringing into being from nothing (ibdāʿ) as in {خلق السماوات والأرض}, and for bringing forth from something as in {خلقكم من نفس واحدة}.\n  - *Al-Zamakhshari (Asas):* Literal (ḥaqīqah): the craftsman's measuring before cutting. Metaphorical (majāz): \"خلق الله الخلق: أوجده على تقدير أوجبته الحكمة\" — God's creation is bringing into existence upon a measure necessitated by wisdom.","book_title":"On Invocations and Supplications","section_title_ar":"Section 72","section_title_en":"Section 72","section_index":72,"anchors":"*(Anchors established)*","content_type":"spiritual","topic":"Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'On Invocations and Supplications'","badge":"Insight"}],"103":[{"arabic":"لقوله عز وجل لا تدركه الأبصار وهو يدرك الأبصار ولقوله تعالى في خطاب موسى عليه السلام لن تراني وليت شعري كيف عرف المعتزل من صفات رب الأرباب ما جهله موسى عليه السلام وكيف سأل موسى عليه السلام الرؤية مع كونها محالا ولعل الجهل بذوي البدع والأهواء من الجهلة الأغبياء أولى من الجهل بالأنبياء صلوات الله عليهم وأما وجه إجراء آية الرؤية على الظاهر فهو أنه غير مؤد إلى المحال فإن الرؤية نوع كشف وعلم إلا أنه أتم وأوضح من العلم فإذا جاز تعلق العلم به وليس في جهة جاز تعلق الرؤية به وليس بجهة وكما يجوز أن ير","english":"**The Eighth Principle: Knowledge that He, Exalted is He, is established upon His Throne (mustawin ʿalā ʿarshihi) in the sense that God, Exalted is He, intended by istiwāʾ — which is the sense that does not contradict the attribute of Majesty, nor does any mark of origination (ḥudūth) or passing away (fanāʾ) attach to it. And it is the sense intended by His saying — istiwāʾ toward the heaven — where He said in the Qur'an: {«ثُمَّ اسْتَوَىٰ إِلَى السَّمَاءِ وَهِيَ دُخَانٌ»} (\"Then He directed Himself to the heaven, and it was smoke\") [Fussilat 41:11]. And that is only by way of subjugation (qahr) and dominion (istīlāʾ), as the poet said:","book_title":"Foundations of Belief","section_title_ar":"Section 90","section_title_en":"The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 90: The Ten Principles Concerning Knowledge of the Divine Attributes","section_index":90,"anchors":"- Root: ع ل م (ʿ-l-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denoting the faculty of perception and comprehension; the mark or trace by which something is known.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العلم: إدراك الشيء بحقيقته\" — knowledge is the apprehension of a thing in its true reality, divided into apprehension of the essence and judgment upon it.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Distinguishes literal knowledge (ḥaqīqah) from awareness (shiʿr); here, it denotes certain, verified cognition of theological verities.\n- Root: س و ي (s-w-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Root indicating straightness, equality, and levelness; from it derives istiwāʾ (establishing oneself upon, ascending to).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Istiwāʾ upon the Throne (al-ʿArsh) is a divine act of sovereign dominion, not corporeal settlement.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal sense of physical sitting is impossible for God; metaphorical sense (majāz) of qahr (overpowering) and istīlāʾ (dominion) is necessitated by reason and consensus.\n- Root: ر أ ي (r-ʾ-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Visual perception; also opinion and discernment.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Ruʾyah (vision) of God in the Hereafter is a mode of disclosure (kashf) and knowledge (ʿilm), more complete than intellectual cognition, not conditioned by spatial direction.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal vision requires corporeality and direction; its application to God demands theological specification (takhṣīṣ) without nullifying the veracity of the scriptural promise.\n- Root: ق د ر (q-d-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Power, measure, decree; the capacity to bring about an act.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Divine power (qudrah) is the negation of incapacity (ʿajz); absolute power belongs to God alone, while creatures possess limited capacity.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal power over an object; metaphorically, God's power encompasses all things without exertion or instrument.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Prepositional Interposition (باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور) — governing the permissible separation between the preposition and its object for rhetorical emphasis, applied here to the parsing of \"إلى السماء\" in the verse on istiwāʾ, where the prepositional phrase is delayed to highlight the divine act of sovereign direction toward the heavens.","content_type":"spiritual","topic":"Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Foundations of Belief'","badge":"Insight"}],"107":[{"arabic":"أيضا {لو شاء الله ما أشركنا ولا آباؤنا} فانظر كيف كانوا صادقين في كلامهم وكيف هلكوا بصدقهم فسبحان من إذا شاء أهلك بالصدق وإذا شاء أسعد بالجهل {يضل به كثيرا ويهدي به كثيرا} فهؤلاء لما ظنوا أنهم استخدموا لأجل المساكين والفقراء أو جل الله تعالى ثم قالوا لا حظ لنا في المساكين ولا حظ لله فينا وفي أموالنا سواء أنفقنا أو أمسكنا هلكوا كما هلك الصبي لما ظن أن مقصود الوالد استخدامه لأجل","english":"Likewise, if it were said: \"Is oxymel (sikanjabīn) better, or the drink of the water-lily (sharāb al-līnūfar)?\" — no unqualified answer could be correct at all. Indeed, if it were said to us: \"Is oxymel better, or the absence of yellow bile?\" — we would say: the absence of yellow bile, for oxymel is sought for the sake of something else, and that which is sought for its own sake is necessarily better than that which is sought for the sake of another.","book_title":"Fear and Hope","section_title_ar":"Section 1025","section_title_en":"The Superiority of Knowledge Over Action — The Parable of the Physician and the Child","section_index":1025,"anchors":"- Root: فضل (faḍl)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Excess or increase beyond what is required; superiority in degree.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الفضل: الزيادة عن الاقتصاد\" — excess beyond moderation, divided into praiseworthy (like excess of knowledge) and blameworthy; comparative superiority of one thing over another by genus, species, or merit.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"فاضلني فلان ففضلته\" — to surpass in excellence. Here used comparatively: one act or state exceeds another in rank.\n- Root: علم (ʿilm)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Knowledge as the apprehension of a thing's reality; the mark or sign by which something is known.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العلم: إدراك الشيء بحقيقته\" — apprehension of a thing's true reality, whether by direct perception of its essence or by judgment concerning it.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"هو من أعلام العلم الخافقه\" — knowledge as a lofty, manifest sign; distinguished from mere action which is its instrument.\n- Root: صدق (ṣidq)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Truthfulness primarily in speech — correspondence of report with reality; may become blameworthy when it leads to ruin, as with the disbelievers who spoke truly about God's independence yet drew false conclusions.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"صدقته الحديث\" — truthful in statement. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"رجل صادق الحملة\" — truthful in resolve. Here: the disbelievers were literally truthful in their words yet destroyed by that very truthfulness.\n- Root: هلك (halak)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Destruction on three levels: loss of something from one's possession, corruption and dissolution, and death. Used here for spiritual ruin through misapplied reasoning.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"وقعوا في المهلكة\" — falling into destruction. Metaphorical: \"تهالك على الشيء\" — intense craving. Here: literal perdition of the soul through false inference.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The conditional structure \"فإن قلت... فاعلم\" (if you say... then know) governs the entire periodic sentence, requiring the apodosis to resolve the protasis with logical necessity, as in the physician analogy where praise of the medicine does not entail its intrinsic superiority.","content_type":"general","topic":"Classical commentary from Al-Ghazali's Ihya Ulum al-Din, from 'Fear and Hope'","badge":"Commentary"}],"108":[{"arabic":"وقال الله تعالى {ولا تسبوا الذين يدعون من دون الله فيسبوا الله عدوا بغير علم} أشار به إلى أن التسبب إلى الشر شر وأن يقصد به معالجة دماغه لتزيد به فطنته وذكاؤه ويسهل عليه درك مهمات دينه بالفكر فقد قال الشافعي رحمه الله من طاب ريحه زاد عقله فهذا وأمثاله من النيات لا يعجز الفقيه عنها إذا كانت تجارة الآخرة وطلب الخير غالبة على قلبه وإذا لم يغلب على قلبه إلا نعيم الدنيا لم تحضره هذه النيات وإن ذكرت له لم ينبعث لها قلبه فلا يكون معه منها إلا حديث النفس وليس ذلك من النية في شيء والمباحات كثيرة ولا يمكن إحصاء النيات فيها فقس بهذا","english":"And God Most High said: {«وَلاَ تَسُبُّوا الَّذِينَ يَدْعُونَ مِن دُونِ اللَّهِ فَيَسُبُّوا اللَّهَ عَدْوًا بِغَيْرِ عِلْمٍ»} (And do not revile those whom they invoke besides God, lest they revile God in enmity without knowledge — Al-An`am 6:108). By this He indicated that causing oneself to be a means to evil is itself evil. And likewise, if one intends by it the treatment of his brain so that his acumen and intelligence might increase, and so that the comprehension of the important matters of his religion might become easier for him through reflection — for al-Shafi`i, may God have mercy on him, said: \"Whose scent is pleasant, his intellect increases\" — then this and its like among intentions are not beyond the capacity of the jurist (faqīh) when the commerce of the Hereafter and the pursuit of good are predominant over his heart. But if nothing predominates over his heart save the pleasures of this world, these intentions will not present themselves to him; and even if they are mentioned to him, his heart will not be stirred to them. Nothing will remain with him of them but the whisper of the self (ḥadīth al-nafs), and that is not part of intention (niyyah) at all.","book_title":"Intention, Sincerity and Truthfulness","section_title_ar":"Section 1214","section_title_en":"On the Refinement of Intentions in Permissible Acts and the Vigilance of the Heart","section_index":1214,"anchors":"- Root: نوى (n-w-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning of intention, purpose, and resolve — the inward direction of the heart toward an aim.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance — niyyah is the spiritual vector distinguishing worship (`ibādah) from mere habit (`ādah); it is the inner quality that converts the permissible (mubāḥ) into the obedient (ṭā`ah).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal vs Metaphorical — literally \"to intend/aim\"; metaphorically, the heart's orientation that renders an act either proximate to God (qurbah) or bereft of reward.\n- Root: خطر (kh-t-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To occur to the mind, to cross one's thought; also danger/peril.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The passing thought (khaṭir) as the first stirring of intention — the moment where accountability begins.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — what crosses the mind; metaphorical — the perilous crossing between obedience and heedlessness.\n- Root: غرر (gh-r-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Deception, delusion, heedless self-security.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Ghurūr is the soul's false assurance that veils one from the true reckoning of deeds.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal — being deceived; metaphorical — the state of the one who trusts in outward appearances while inward realities are corrupt.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The periodic sentence structure in the hadith \"ألقوا عليه من سيآتهم\" demonstrates the interposition of the prepositional phrase for rhetorical emphasis, governing the flow of divine judgment narrative.","content_type":"spiritual","topic":"Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Intention, Sincerity and Truthfulness'","badge":"Insight"},{"arabic":"تعالى ولا تسبوا الذي يدعون من دون الله فيسبوا الله عدوا بغير علم وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم كيف ترون من يسب أبويه فقالوا وهل من أحد يسب أبويه فقال نعم يسب أبوي غيره فيسبون أبويه (5) وقد روي عن أنس بن مالك رضي الله عنه أن رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم كلم إحدى نسائه فمر به رجل فدعاه رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم وقال يا فلان هذه زوجتي صفية {فقال} يا رسول الله من كنت أظن فيه فإني لم أكن أظن فيك فقال إن الشيطان يجري من ابن آدم مجرى الدم (1) وزاد في رواية إني خشيت أن يقذف في قلوبكما شيئا وكانا رجلين فقال على رسلكما إنها صفية ا","english":"**First**: That you greet him with peace (*salām*) when you meet him, and respond to his invitation when he invites you, and say \"may Allah have mercy on you\" (*tashmīt*) when he sneezes, and visit him when he is ill, and attend his funeral procession when he dies, and fulfill his oath when he swears by you, and give him sincere counsel when he seeks your advice, and protect him in his absence when he is away from you, and love for him what you love for yourself, and dislike for him what you dislike for yourself. (1)","book_title":"Duties of Brotherhood","section_title_ar":"Section 433","section_title_en":"The Rights of Brotherhood — Section 433: The Duties of a Muslim Toward His Fellow Muslims","section_index":433,"anchors":"- Root: سلم (s-l-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of safety, security, freedom from harm; the root from which *islām* (submission/surrender) and *salām* (peace) derive\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): *Salām* as the theological state of being secure from harm — both giving and receiving safety; *islām* as the total surrender of the heart to Allah\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (*ḥaqīqah*): physical safety from tongue and hand; Metaphorical (*majāz*): the comprehensive spiritual safety that defines true Muslim brotherhood\n- Root: عون (ʿ-w-n)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Assistance, aid, helping another in need\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Mutual assistance as a divine ordinance among believers\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical aid; Metaphorical: intercession (*shafāʿah*) and advocacy on behalf of others\n- Root: ستر (s-t-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Covering, concealing, veiling; protection from exposure\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): *Sitr* as the divine attribute of concealment — Allah's covering of sins in this world and the next\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: covering a physical defect; Metaphorical: concealing another's faults and sins — a cardinal virtue of brotherhood\n- Root: عذر (ʿ-dh-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Excuse, pardon, acceptance of apology\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): *ʿAfw* (pardon) as the higher station of forgiveness that elevates the forgiver\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: releasing from obligation; Metaphorical: the noble trait of overlooking others' offenses\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The repeated use of restrictive constructions (e.g., \"المسلم من سلم المسلمون\") establishes definitional boundaries through predicate restriction, indicating that the *ḥaqīqah* of faith is manifested through specific observable actions.\n\n---\n\n##","content_type":"spiritual","topic":"Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Duties of Brotherhood'","badge":"Insight"}],"111":[{"arabic":"ولا تقولن لشيء إني فاعل ذلك غدا إلا أن يشاء الله ثم لم يقتصر على ذلك فيما لا يشك فيه بل قال تعالى لتدخلن المسجد الحرام إن شاء الله آمنين محلقين رءؤسكم ومقصرين وكان الله سبحانه عالما بأنهم يدخلون لا محالة وأنه شاءه ولكن المقصود تعليمه ذلك فتأدب رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم في ما كان يخبر عنه معلوما كان أو مشكوكا حتى قال صلى الله عليه وسلم لما دخل المقابر السلام عليكم دار قوم مؤمنين وإنا إن شاء الله بكم لاحقون (1) واللحوق بهم غير مشكوك فيه ولكن مقتضى الأدب ذكر الله تعالى وربط الأمور به وهذه الصيغ","english":"If you say: \"What is the meaning of the Salaf's statement, 'I am a believer, if God wills' — given that the exception (istithnā') implies doubt, and doubt in faith is unbelief? Yet they all refrained from giving an unqualified answer affirming faith and were cautious about it.\"","book_title":"Remembrance of Death and Afterlife","section_title_ar":"Section 102","section_title_en":"On the Meaning of the Salaf's Statement \"I am a Believer, If God Wills\" — Section 102","section_index":102,"anchors":"- Root: نفس (n-f-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, the soul; that which is breathed; the innermost reality of a person.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): النفس is used for the rūḥ (spirit), for the dhāt (essence/self), and for the seat of intention and knowledge. When one says \"he knows in himself (nafsihi) that he is a believer,\" it denotes certitude residing in the very core of one's being, not a corporeal faculty.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) usage refers to the soul's reflexive self-awareness; metaphorical (majāz) usage extends to \"blood\" (dam) or \"the eye\" (ʿayn) of envy. Here, \"in himself\" (fī nafsihi) is literal self-knowledge, not mere outward profession.\n- Root: وجه (w-j-h)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Face, countenance; the foremost part; a mode, aspect, or direction.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The face is the most noble apparent part of a thing; hence it is used for the \"aspect\" or \"manner\" (wajh) of an affair. \"Four aspects\" (wujūh) means four distinct modes of interpretation or rational grounds.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the face of a person or object. Metaphorical: \"the face of the matter\" (wajh al-amr) — its most salient and honorable dimension. Here, the \"aspects\" are hermeneutical angles, not physical parts.\n- Root: ايم (a-y-m)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The unmarried state (aym); one who has no spouse. Not applicable here directly, but the root's semantic field of \"being alone/without attachment\" contrasts with īmān's root (a-m-n) — security and trust — which is the operative term in this text.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Not directly relevant; the operative root is أمن (a-m-n), meaning security from fear, and by extension, faith (īmān) as the state of being secure in God's truth.\n- Root: كان (k-w-n)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To be, to exist; the copula of predication.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Kāna denotes existence and becoming; in theological usage, it grounds the distinction between what is (mawjūd) and what is merely asserted. The question \"Are you a believer?\" (a-mu'min anta?) uses kāna implicitly to ask about actual, present state — not future contingency.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: existence. Metaphorical: the \"being-so\" of a predicate. The istithnā' (exception) with \"in shā' Allāh\" suspends the copula's certainty, shifting from assertive existence to contingent hope.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الاستثناء والتعليق بالمشيئة (The Chapter of Exception and Suspension by the Divine Will) — When an exception clause (istithnā') follows a positive assertion, it grammatically suspends the universality or certainty of the predication. In theological discourse, this suspension may be semantic (doubt) or pragmatic (deference, etiquette, or self-deprecation), and Sibawayh's parsing of conditional suspension (taʿlīq) shows that the apodosis need not be negated — only its absolute, unqualified force is tempered.","content_type":"spiritual","topic":"Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'","badge":"Insight"}],"115":[{"arabic":"في قوله تعالى وتمت كلمات ربك صدقا وعدلا صدقا لمن مات على الإيمان وعدلا لمن مات على الشرك وقد قال تعالى {ولله عاقبة الأمور} فمهما كان الشك بهذه المثابة كان الاستثناء واجبا لأن الإيمان عبارة عما يفيد الجنة كما أن الصوم عبارة عما يبرىء الذمة وما فسد قبل الغروب لا يبرىء الذمة فيخرج عن كونه صوما فكذلك الإيمان بل لا يبعد أن يسأل عن الصوم الماضي الذي لا يشك فيه بعد الفراغ منه فيقال أصمت بالأمس فيقول نعم إن شاء الله تعالى إذ الصوم الحقيقي هو المقبول والمقبول غائب عنه لا يطلع عليه إلا الله تع","english":"I say: The fear of an evil end (sū' al-khātimah) is so severe that one of the early scholars (salaf) said: \"Were I to stand at the door of the Prophet's chamber — peace be upon him — and be given the choice between death and affirming God's Oneness (al-tawḥīd) at that very door, I would choose death over affirming His Oneness there, for I do not know what change might befall my heart, turning me from tawḥīd [to its opposite] by the time I reached the door of the house.\"","book_title":"Foundations of Belief","section_title_ar":"Section 105","section_title_en":"The Book of the Foundations of Belief — Section 105: The Justification of Exception (Istithnā') in Matters of Faith","section_index":105,"anchors":"- Root: صوم (ṣ-w-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is \"abstention\" (imsāk) from any act — food, speech, or movement.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الصوم في الأصل: الإمساك عن الفعل مطعما كان، أو كلاما، أو مشيا\" — Abstention from any act, whether food, speech, or walking; the horse that refrains from movement is called ṣā'im.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): The month of fasting. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"صام الفرس على آريه إذا لم يعتلف\" — The horse \"fasts\" when it refuses its fodder.\n- Root: حسن (ḥ-s-n)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Beauty, goodness, excellence in form and act.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحسن: عبارة عن كل مبهج مرغوب فيه\" — That which pleases and is desired, whether by intellect, passion, or sense.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: Physical beauty and adornment. Metaphorical: \"فلان لا يحسن شيئا، وقيمة المرء ما يحسن\" — \"So-and-so does not do anything well; a man's worth is what he does well.\"\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The permissibility of interposing elements between a governing word and its governed prepositional phrase, contingent upon the semantic completeness of the intervening clause.","content_type":"afterlife","topic":"Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Foundations of Belief'","badge":"Afterlife"}],"116":[{"arabic":"فإنك إن تطع أكثر من في الأرض يضلوك على سبيل الله وحكايات المجتهدين غير محصورة وفيما ذكرناه كفاية للمعتبر وإن أردت مزيدا فعليك بالمواظبة على مطالعة كتاب حلية الأولياء فهو مشتمل على شرح أحوال الصحابة والتابعين ومن بعدهم وبالوقوف عليه يستبين لك بعدك وبعد أهل عصرك من أهل الدين فإن حدثتك نفسك بالنظر إلى أهل زمانك وقالت إنما تيسر الخير في ذلك الزمانلكثرة الأعوان والآن فإن خالفت أهل زمانك رأوك مجنونا وسخروا بك فوافقهم فيما هم فيه وعليه فلا يجرى عليك إلا ما يجرى عليهم والمصيبة إذا عمت طابت","english":"So upon you — if you are among those who stand watch, those who keep vigil over their own souls (al-murāqibīn li-nafsik) — to scrutinize the states of the men and women among the strivers (al-mujtahidīn), that your zeal may be stirred and your eagerness increased. And beware of looking to the people of your own age, for if you obey most of those on earth, they will lead you astray from the path of God {«وإن تطع أكثر من في الأرض يضلوك عن سبيل الله»}.","book_title":"Monitoring and Accounting","section_title_ar":"Section 1250","section_title_en":"Section 1250 — The Sixth Vigil: The Rebuke and Admonishment of the Soul","section_index":1250,"anchors":"- Root: نفس (n-f-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, soul, spirit; that which animates and is the seat of desire and volition.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-nafs denotes the immaterial reality of the human (al-rūḥ), the locus of divine address and moral accountability; its states range from al-ammārah (inciting to evil) to al-lawwāmah (self-reproaching) to al-muṭmaʾinnah (tranquil).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) usage refers to the soul itself; metaphorical (majāz) usage extends to blood (nafs sāʾilah) or the eye of envy — but here it is the true essence of man.\n- Root: جهل (j-h-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Ignorance as the absence of knowledge; also foolish conduct contrary to right action.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Jahl is threefold: (1) the soul's emptiness of knowledge (the root), (2) belief contrary to reality, (3) acting contrary to what is due — whether with correct or corrupt belief.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: ignorance and folly; metaphorical: the wind \"ignoring\" the branch (i.e., agitating it violently) — here, the soul's self-deception is exposed as true ignorance.\n- Root: اهل (a-h-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Those united by kinship, religion, or dwelling; one who is worthy or fit for something.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Ahl al-rajul: those joined by lineage, faith, or craft; Ahl al-Bayt in the Qurʾān {«إنما يريد الله ليذهب عنكم الرجس أهل البيت»} — and by extension, what one merits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: family or inhabitants; metaphorical: \"he is ahl (worthy) for such-and-such\" — i.e., deserving of it, as in the supplication \"tawallā min amrī mā anta ahluh\" (undertake my affair as befits Thee).\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — \"إنما\" restricts the predicate exclusively to the subject, as in the soul's whisper \"إنما تيسر الخير في ذلك الزمان لكثرة الأعوان\" — a false restriction used to delude, and the divine reply \"وذكر فإن الذكرى تنفع المؤمنين\" — where the command is unrestricted.","content_type":"spiritual","topic":"Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Monitoring and Accounting'","badge":"Insight"}],"120":[{"arabic":"ظاهرة وباطنة ثم قال وذروا ظاهر الإثم وباطنه فترك باطن الإثم مما لا يعرفه الخلق من الحسد وسوء الظن والبدعة وإضمار الشر للناس إلى غير ذلك من آثام القلوب هو الشكر للنعم الباطنة وترك الإثم الظاهر بالجوارح شكر للنعمة الظاهرة بل أقول كل من عصى الله تعالى ولو فى طريفة واحدة بأن فتح جفنه مثلا حيث يجب غض البصر فقد كفر كل نعمة لله تعالى عليه في السموات والأرض وما بينهما فإن كل ما خلقه الله تعالى حتى الملائكة والسموات والأرض والحيوانات والنبات بجملته نعمة على كل واحد من العباد قد نم به انتفاعه وإن ان","english":"If you say: Why were these acts not delegated to a single angel, without my needing seven angels? — For the wheat also requires one to grind it first, then one to separate the bran from it and cast away the refuse second, then one to pour water upon it third, then one to knead it fourth, then one to cut it into round balls fifth, then one to roll it into broad flat loaves sixth, then one to affix it to the oven seventh — yet a single man may undertake all of that and suffice for it. So why were not the works of the angels inwardly like the works of men outwardly? — Know that the constitution (khilqah) of the angels differs from the constitution of men. There is not a single one of them but is singular in attribute (waḥdānī al-ṣifah), having in him no admixture or composition whatsoever; hence there cannot issue from each of them save a single act. And to this points His saying, the Exalted: {«وما منا إلا له مقام معلوم»} (And there is not one of us but has a known station) {Q. 37:164}. Therefore there is among them no rivalry and no combat. Rather, their example, in the determination of the rank of each one of them and his act, is the example of the five senses: for sight does not vie with hearing in the perception of sounds, nor does smell vie with them, nor do the two contend with smell over it. And it is not like the hand and the foot — for you may grasp feebly with the toes of your foot and thereby vie with the hand — nor like when you strike another with your head and thereby vie with the hand which is the instrument of striking. Nor is it like the single man who undertakes by himself the grinding, the kneading, and the baking, for this is a kind of crookedness and deviation from equity, whose cause is the diversity of man's attributes and the diversity of his impulses: for he is not singular in attribute, and therefore he is not singular in act. For this reason we see man obeying Allah at one time and disobeying Him at another, due to the diversity of his impulses and attributes. And that is not possible in the nature of the angels; rather, they are created disposed to obedience — there is no scope for disobedience in their case. Hence, necessarily: {«لا يعصون الله ما أمرهم ويفعلون ما يؤمرون»} (They do not disobey Allah in what He commands them, and they do what they are commanded) {Q. 66:6}, and {«يسبحون الليل والنهار لا يفترون»} (They glorify night and day without ceasing) {Q. 21:20}. The one who bows among them is bowing forever, the one who prostrates among them is prostrating forever, and the one who stands is standing forever — there is no variation in their acts and no weariness, and for each one is a known station (maqām maʿlūm) which he does not overstep. And their obedience to Allah the Exalted — inasmuch as there is no scope for opposition in them — may be likened to the obedience of your limbs to you: for whenever you have resolved with firm will upon opening the eyelids, the sound eyelid has no hesitation or variation — obeying you at one time and disobeying you at another — but rather it is as though it awaits your command and your prohibition, opening and closing continuously at your signal. This resembles it in one respect but differs from it in another: for the eyelid has no knowledge of what issues from it of movement, opening and shutting, whereas the angels are living beings, knowing what they do.","book_title":"Patience and Gratitude","section_title_ar":"Section 1012","section_title_en":"The Division and Distribution of Nutritive Substance — The Earthly Angels and the Hierarchy of Divine Bounty","section_index":1012,"anchors":"- Root: ملك (malaka)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الملك: هو المتصرف بالأمر والنهي\" — the one who governs by command and prohibition; dominion and sovereign power.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الملك: هو المتصرف بالأمر والنهي في الجمهور\" — the angel (malak) as a designated agent of divine governance, distinct from human composite agency.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"لله الملك والملكوت\" — absolute sovereignty. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"ملك عليه أمره إذا استولى عليه\" — not applicable here; the term is used in its literal theological sense of delegated divine administration.\n- Root: وحد (waḥada)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الواحد: ما لا جزء له\" — that which has no part; indivisible unity.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الواحد في الحقيقة هو الشيء الذي لا جزء له البتة\" — the truly one is that which has no composition whatsoever; hence \"وحداني الصفة\" (singular in attribute) means uncompounded in nature.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"هو واحد، وهم وحدان\" — numerical unity. Here applied analogically to angelic nature: absolute simplicity without admixture.\n- Root: نعم (naʿima)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"النعمة: الحالة الحسنة\" — the goodly state; benefaction.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النعمة: الحالة الحسنة... والنعمة للجنس تقال للقليل والكثير\" — bounty as a genus encompassing both apparent (ẓāhirah) and inward (bāṭinah) blessings.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"جلت نعمة الله ونعماؤه\" — manifest divine beneficence. Metaphorical: \"خفّت نعامتهم: ذهبوا\" — not applicable; the term is literal throughout.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — governing the periodic suspension of clauses (e.g., \"فإن قلت... فاعلم أن\") where the protasis is held in suspense before the apodosis, requiring the reader to grasp the full syntactic arc before resolution.","content_type":"ethics","topic":"Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Patience and Gratitude'","badge":"Ethics"}],"122":[{"arabic":"مطمع في هذا النور فيبقى كمن مثله في الظلمات ليس بخارج منها بل كظلمات في بحر لجى يغشاه موج من فوقه موج من فوقه سحاب ظلمات بعضها فوق بعض فإذن لا سعادة إلا في معرفة الله تعالى وأما ما عدا ذلك من المعارف فمنها ما لا فائدة له أصلا كمعرفة الشعر وأنساب العرب وغيرهما ومنها ما له منفعة في الإعانة على معرفة الله تعالى كمعرفة لغة العرب والتفسير والفقه والأخبار فإن معرفة لغة العرب تعين على معرفة تفسير القرآن ومعرفة التفسير تعين على معرفة ما في القرآن من كيفية العبادات والأعمال التي تفيد تزكية النفس ومعرفة طري","english":"All of these divisions are comprised within knowledge of God — of what is necessary for Him, what is impossible in His attributes, and what is permissible in His acts. Thus, knowledge of God — exalted be He — of His attributes, His acts, His wisdom in the dominion (malakūt) of the heavens and the earth, the ordering of this world and the Hereafter, and what pertains to that — this is the true perfection that draws near to God — exalted be He — the one who is described by it, and it remains a perfection for the soul after death. And this knowledge becomes a light for the knowers after death: {their light running before them and on their right hands, they saying: \"Our Lord, perfect for us our light\"} {«ربنا أتمم لنا نورنا»} — that is, this knowledge is the capital that leads to the disclosure of what was not disclosed in this world. Just as one who carries a hidden lamp — it is possible that this becomes the cause of increased light by another lamp from which he kindles, so that the hidden light is completed by way of supplementation — while one who has no lamp at all has no hope of that. So too, one who has no root of knowledge of God — exalted be He — has no hope in that light; he remains like one whose similitude is in darkness, not emerging from it — nay, {like darknesses in a vast deep sea, covered by wave above wave, above which is cloud: darknesses, one above another} {«كظلمات في بحر لجي يغشاه موج من فوقه موج من فوقه سحاب ظلمات بعضها فوق بعض»}. Therefore, there is no happiness save in knowledge of God — exalted be He.","book_title":"Condemnation of Status and Ostentation","section_title_ar":"Section 799","section_title_en":"The True Perfection of Knowledge and Its Degrees of Proximity to God","section_index":799,"anchors":"- Root: علم (ʿ-L-M)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core sense of \"knowing\" as the impression of a thing's reality upon the mind; the root also carries the sense of a \"mark\" or \"sign\" by which something is distinguished and recognized.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Knowledge (ʿilm) is \"the apprehension of a thing by its true reality\" — divided into apprehension of the essence of a thing, and judgment upon a thing regarding what is or is not predicated of it. It is the broadest epistemic term, applicable to God and creatures alike.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) usage: \"He is a sign (ʿalam) of knowledge\" — knowledge is that which marks and distinguishes. The metaphorical (majāz) extension: \"I knew of your news\" = \"I perceived it\" — indicating that knowledge as proximity to God is not mere information but a transformative mark upon the soul.\n- Root: عرف (ʿ-R-F)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Recognition through traces and effects; the root denotes \"coming to know by way of discernment of distinguishing features.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Maʿrifah is \"apprehension of a thing through reflection and deliberation upon its effect\" — more specific than ʿilm. It is the term used for the creature's knowledge of God, since it proceeds from contemplating His works, not from grasping His essence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"I will make you recognize (la-ʾuʿarrifanna-ka) what you did\" = \"I will requite you for it.\" This underscores that maʿrifah is knowledge that returns upon the knower as a state of recompense or illumination.\n- Root: نور (N-W-R)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Light as that which renders visible; the root also yields \"beacon\" (manār) — a landmark set upon a path for guidance.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Light is the diffused radiance that aids vision\" — of two kinds: the sensory (perceived by the eye) and the intellectual (perceived by the inner eye of insight), the latter being \"the light of the intellect and the light of the Qur'an.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Metaphorical (majāz): \"The matter became luminous (nāra)\" = \"it became clear.\" Light is thus the very clarity of knowledge that guides the knower through the darkness of ignorance.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Restriction and Specification (al-ḥaṣr wa al-taqyīd) — governing the use of \"إنما\" (innamā) to restrict perfection exclusively to knowledge of God, and the conditional-connective structure (fa-innahu, fa-kullamā) that links degrees of knowledge to degrees of proximity.","content_type":"spiritual","topic":"Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Condemnation of Status and Ostentation'","badge":"Insight"},{"arabic":"أوحينا إليك روحا من أمرنا} وقال سبحانه أومن كان ميتا فأحييناه وجعلنا له نورا يمشي به في الناس وحيث ذكر النور والظلمة أراد به العلم والجهل كقوله {يخرجهم من الظلمات إلى النور} وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم يا أيها الناس اعقلوا عن ربكم وتواصوا بالعقل تعرفوا ما أمرتم به وما نهيتم عنه واعلموا أنه ينجدكم عند ربكم واعلموا أن العاقل من أطاع الله وإن كان دميم المنظر حقير الخطر دنيء المنزلة رث الهيئة وأن الجاهل من عصى الله تعالى وإن كان جميل المنظر عظيم الخطر شريف المنزلة حسن الهيئة فصيحا نطوقا فالقردة والخنازير أعقل عند الله تعالى ممن عصاه ولا تغتر ب","english":"The Book of Seclusion ('Uzlah) — its explanation, God Most High willing. For this reason, Yūsuf ibn Asbāṭ wrote to Ḥudhayfah al-Marʿashī: \"What is your opinion of one who remains, finding no one with whom to remember God Most High except that he is either sinful, or his remembrance in company is itself an act of disobedience — and that because he finds no one of his own kind?\" And indeed he spoke truly, for mixing with people is never free from backbiting (ghībah), or listening to backbiting, or remaining silent upon wrongdoing (munkar). And the best of his states is that he either imparts knowledge or acquires it. Yet were this poor wretch to reflect, and to know that his imparting of knowledge is never free from the taints of ostentation (riyā'), the seeking of followers, and the desire for leadership (ri'āsah), he would know that the one who acquires knowledge from him only seeks to make it an instrument for pursuing the world and a means toward evil — so that the teacher becomes his helper therein, his support and his backer, preparing its causes for him, like one who sells a sword to a highway robber. For knowledge is like the sword: its soundness for good is like the soundness of the sword for warfare. And for this reason, it is not permitted to sell it to one whom one knows, by the indications of his circumstances, intends to use it to aid in cutting off the road.","book_title":"Patience and Gratitude","section_title_ar":"Section 65","section_title_en":"The Book of Seclusion ('Uzlah) — Section 65, and The Seventh Book: On the Intellect, Its Nobility, Its Reality, and Its Divisions","section_index":65,"anchors":"- Root: عقل (ʿaql)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes binding, restraining, and holding back; the intellect is that which restrains the soul from base desires and prevents it from straying.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العقل يقال للقوة المتهيئة لقبول العلم، ويقال للعلم الذي يستفيده الإنسان بتلك القوة عقل\" — The intellect is said of the faculty prepared for the reception of knowledge, and it is also said of the knowledge which man acquires by means of that faculty.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): intellectual apprehension and understanding. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"نخلة لا تعقل الإبار\" — a palm tree that does not accept pollination, i.e., does not grasp or take in.\n- Root: علم (ʿilm)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Knowledge as the mark or sign by which a thing is known; the trace or landmark that guides.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العلم: إدراك الشيء بحقيقته\" — Knowledge is the apprehension of a thing in its true reality; it is of two kinds: apprehension of the essence of a thing, and judgment upon a thing regarding what exists for it or is negated of it.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: certain awareness. Metaphorical: \"هو من أعلام العلم الخافقه\" — he is among the lofty banners of knowledge; knowledge as a guiding landmark (maʿlam).\n- Root: نور (nūr)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Light; the first to set up beacons (manār) on his path in his campaigns to guide his return.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النور: الضوء المنتشر الذي يعين على الإبصار\" — Light is the diffused radiance that aids vision; it is of two kinds: that perceived by the eye of inner insight (baṣīrah) — such as the light of the intellect and the light of the Qur'an — and that perceived by the eye of the head, such as the celestial bodies.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: illumination. Metaphorical: \"نور الأمر: بينه\" — he illuminated the matter: he made it clear; \"وهذا أنور من ذاك: أبين\" — this is more luminous than that: more evident.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Restriction and Focused Predication (al-ḥaṣr wa al-taqyīd bi-innamā) — governing the exclusive attribution of a predicate to a subject, as in the author's restriction of the scholar's condition to one of two men, and the exclusion of the third.","content_type":"spiritual","topic":"Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Patience and Gratitude'","badge":"Insight"}],"125":[{"arabic":"ومن يرد أن يضله يجعل صدره ضيقا حرجا كأنما يصعد في السماء إن ينصركم الله فلا غالب لكم وإن يخذلكم فمن ذا الذي ينصركم من بعده فهو الهادي والمضل يفعل ما يشاء ويحكم ما يريد لا راد لحكمه ولا معقب لقضائه خلق الجنة وخلق لها أهلا فاستعملهم بالطاعة وخلق النار وخلق لها أهلا فاستعمله بالمعاصي وعرف الخلق علامة أهل الجنة وأهل النار فقال إن الأبرار لفي نعيم وإن الفجار لفي جحيم ثم قال تعالى فيما روى عن نبيه صلى الله عليه وسلم هؤلاء في الجنة ولا أبالي وهؤلاء في النار ولا أبالي (1) فتعالى الله الملك الحق لا يسئل عما ي","english":"I say: Know that when the Shayṭān prevails and the heart inclines to its own kind among the parties of the Shayṭān, turning away from the party of God—Exalted is He—and His saints, and aiding the party of the Shayṭān and His enemies, then there flows upon its bodily members, by virtue of the prior decree, that which is the cause of its distance from God—Exalted is He. But if the angelic attributes are predominant over the heart, the heart does not lend its ear to the Shayṭān's seduction, nor to his incitement toward the immediate world, nor to his belittling of the matter of the Hereafter. Rather, it inclines to the party of God—Exalted is He—and obedience appears upon its members in accordance with what has preceded in the decree. Thus, the heart of the believer is between two fingers of the fingers of the All-Merciful—that is, between the mutual tugging of these two hosts—and this is the meaning of \"turning\" (taqlīb): the transition from one party to another. As for perpetual constancy, whether with the party of the angels or with the party of the Shayṭān, that is rare on both sides. These acts of obedience and disobedience emerge from the treasuries of the Unseen into the world of witness by way of the treasury of the heart, for it is among the treasuries of the Malakūt. And when they do emerge, they become signs by which the masters of hearts recognize the prior decree. For whomever is created for Paradise, the means of obedience are made easy for him; and whomever is created for the Fire, the means of disobedience are made easy for him, and evil companions are given sway over him, and the rule of the Shayṭān is cast into his heart. For by various kinds of deceptions he beguiles the foolish, saying: \"God is Merciful, so do not care\"; and \"All people do not fear God, so do not oppose them\"; and \"Life is long, so be patient until you repent tomorrow.\" He promises them and fills them with desires—{يَعِدُهُمْ وَيُمَنِّيهِمْ وَمَا يَعِدُهُمُ الشَّيْطَانُ إِلَّا غُرُورًا} \"He promises them and fills them with desires, and the Shayṭān promises them nothing but delusion\" {النساء/120}. He promises them repentance and fills them with hopes of forgiveness, and thus destroys them, by God's permission, through these ruses and what runs in their course. So he expands their hearts to accept delusion and constricts them from accepting the truth. And all of that is by God's decree and destiny: {فَمَن يُرِدِ اللَّهُ أَن يَهْدِيَهُ يَشْرَحْ صَدْرَهُ لِلْإِسْلَامِ وَمَن يُرِدْ أَن يُضِلَّهُ يَجْعَلْ صَدْرَهُ ضَيِّقًا حَرَجًا كَأَنَّمَا يَصَّعَّدُ فِي السَّمَاءِ} \"Whomsoever God wishes to guide, He expands his breast to Islam; and whomsoever He wishes to lead astray, He makes his breast narrow and constricted, as though he were climbing into the sky\" {الأنعام/125}. {إِن يَنصُرْكُمُ اللَّهُ فَلَا غَالِبَ لَكُمْ وَإِن يَخْذُلْكُمْ فَمَن ذَا الَّذِي يَنصُرُكُم مِّن بَعْدِهِ} \"If God helps you, none can overcome you; and if He forsakes you, who is there to help you after Him?\" {آل عمران/160}. He is the Guide and the Misleader; He does what He wills and decrees what He desires. There is no repeller of His judgment and no reverser of His decree. He created Paradise and created for it its people, and employed them in obedience; and He created the Fire and created for it its people, and employed them in disobedience. And He made known to creation the mark of the people of Paradise and the people of the Fire, saying: {إِنَّ الْأَبْرَارَ لَفِي نَعِيمٍ وَإِنَّ الْفُجَّارَ لَفِي جَحِيمٍ} \"Verily, the righteous shall be in bliss, and verily, the wicked shall be in the Blazing Fire\" {الانفطار/13-14}. Then He—Exalted is He—said, as related from His Prophet, peace be upon him: \"These are in Paradise, and I do not care; and these are in the Fire, and I do not care.\" Exalted is God, the King, the Truth! {لَا يُسْأَلُ عَمَّا يَفْعَلُ وَهُمْ يُسْأَلُونَ} \"He is not questioned about what He does, but they shall be questioned\" {الأنبياء/23}.","book_title":"Condemnation of Delusion","section_title_ar":"Section 611","section_title_en":"The Explanation of the Heart's Rapid Fluctuation and the Division of Hearts Between Change and Constancy","section_index":611,"anchors":"- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of turning, overturning, and revolving a thing from one face to another; the heart is so named for its constant oscillation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart's essence is its perpetual turning and redirection; its fluctuation is not accidental but constitutive of its nature.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): physical overturning. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — the heart's movement between the two hosts (jundān) is a real, dynamic turnover of allegiance.\n- Root: شيط (sh-y-t)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Burning, scorching, or spoiling meat by over-smoking; blood going to waste.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The Shayṭān is so named for his being burned and banished from mercy.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Metaphorical: \"شاط دمه إذا بطل\" — the Shayṭān's work is to render the servant's spiritual life wasted and nullified, as blood spilled in vain.\n- Root: حزب (ḥ-z-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: A group or party with cohesion and strength; al-Ḥayzabūn (the old woman) shows the root's extension to a gathered mass.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحزب: جماعة فيها غلظ\" — the party of God (ḥizb Allāh) versus the party of the Shayṭān (ḥizb al-Shayṭān) are two opposing, organized camps in cosmic conflict.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: a band of partisans. Metaphorical: \"قرأ حزبه من القرآن\" — a portion assigned; here, the heart is assigned to one of the two parties by divine decree.\n- Root: خلق (kh-l-q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Measuring and smoothing; creating as precise estimation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الخلق أصله: التقدير المستقيم\" — creation is origination upon a just measure; God's creative act entails the pre-ordainment of ends.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: shaping leather before cutting. Metaphorical: \"خلق الله الخلق: أوجده على تقدير أوجبته الحكمة\" — God's creation of Paradise and Hellfire and their inhabitants is an act of wise, pre-eternal determination.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The phrase \"إنما ذكرنا منه ما يحتاج إليه\" restricts the author's purpose: only the necessary measure of the heart's wonders is expounded, not exhaustive treatment, signaling a deliberate boundary of the science of practical religion (ʿilm al-muʿāmalah).","content_type":"afterlife","topic":"Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Condemnation of Delusion'","badge":"Afterlife"},{"arabic":"الحق وكل ذلك بقضاء من الله وقدر فمن يرد الله أن يهديه يشرح صدره للإسلام ومن يرد أن يضله يجعل صدره ضيقا حرجا كأنما يصعد في السماء إن ينصركم الله فلا غالب لكم وإن يخذلكم فمن ذا الذي ينصركم من بعده فهو الهادي والمضل يفعل ما يشاء ويحكم ما يريد لا راد لحكمه ولا معقب لقضائه خلق الجنة وخلق لها أهلا فاستعملهم بالطاعة وخلق النار وخلق لها أهلا فاستعمله بالمعاصي وعرف الخلق علامة أهل الجنة وأهل النار فقال إن الأبرار لفي نعيم وإن الفجار لفي جحيم ثم قال تعالى فيما روى عن نبيه صلى الله عليه وسلم هؤلاء في الجنة ولا أبالي وهؤلاء في النار ولا أبالي (1) فتعالى الله الملك الحق لا يسئل عما ي","english":"I say: Know that when the Shayṭān prevails and the heart inclines to its own kind among the parties of the Shayṭān, turning away from the party of God—Exalted is He—and His saints, and aiding the party of the Shayṭān and His enemies, then there flows upon its bodily members, by virtue of the prior decree, that which is the cause of its distance from God—Exalted is He. But if the angelic attributes are predominant over the heart, the heart does not lend its ear to the Shayṭān's seduction, nor to his incitement toward the immediate world, nor to his belittling of the matter of the Hereafter. Rather, it inclines to the party of God—Exalted is He—and obedience appears upon its members in accordance with what has preceded in the decree. Thus, the heart of the believer is between two fingers of the fingers of the All-Merciful—that is, between the mutual tugging of these two hosts—and this is the meaning of \"turning\" (taqlīb): the transition from one party to another. As for perpetual constancy, whether with the party of the angels or with the party of the Shayṭān, that is rare on both sides. These acts of obedience and disobedience emerge from the treasuries of the Unseen into the world of witness by way of the treasury of the heart, for it is among the treasuries of the Malakūt. And when they do emerge, they become signs by which the masters of hearts recognize the prior decree. For whomever is created for Paradise, the means of obedience are made easy for him; and whomever is created for the Fire, the means of disobedience are made easy for him, and evil companions are given sway over him, and the rule of the Shayṭān is cast into his heart. For by various kinds of deceptions he beguiles the foolish, saying: \"God is Merciful, so do not care\"; and \"All people do not fear God, so do not oppose them\"; and \"Life is long, so be patient until you repent tomorrow.\" He promises them and fills them with desires—{يَعِدُهُمْ وَيُمَنِّيهِمْ وَمَا يَعِدُهُمُ الشَّيْطَانُ إِلَّا غُرُورًا} \"He promises them and fills them with desires, and the Shayṭān promises them nothing but delusion\" {النساء/120}. He promises them repentance and fills them with hopes of forgiveness, and thus destroys them, by God's permission, through these ruses and what runs in their course. So he expands their hearts to accept delusion and constricts them from accepting the truth. And all of that is by God's decree and destiny: {فَمَن يُرِدِ اللَّهُ أَن يَهْدِيَهُ يَشْرَحْ صَدْرَهُ لِلْإِسْلَامِ وَمَن يُرِدْ أَن يُضِلَّهُ يَجْعَلْ صَدْرَهُ ضَيِّقًا حَرَجًا كَأَنَّمَا يَصَّعَّدُ فِي السَّمَاءِ} \"Whomsoever God wishes to guide, He expands his breast to Islam; and whomsoever He wishes to lead astray, He makes his breast narrow and constricted, as though he were climbing into the sky\" {الأنعام/125}. {إِن يَنصُرْكُمُ اللَّهُ فَلَا غَالِبَ لَكُمْ وَإِن يَخْذُلْكُمْ فَمَن ذَا الَّذِي يَنصُرُكُم مِّن بَعْدِهِ} \"If God helps you, none can overcome you; and if He forsakes you, who is there to help you after Him?\" {آل عمران/160}. He is the Guide and the Misleader; He does what He wills and decrees what He desires. There is no repeller of His judgment and no reverser of His decree. He created Paradise and created for it its people, and employed them in obedience; and He created the Fire and created for it its people, and employed them in disobedience. And He made known to creation the mark of the people of Paradise and the people of the Fire, saying: {إِنَّ الْأَبْرَارَ لَفِي نَعِيمٍ وَإِنَّ الْفُجَّارَ لَفِي جَحِيمٍ} \"Verily, the righteous shall be in bliss, and verily, the wicked shall be in the Blazing Fire\" {الانفطار/13-14}. Then He—Exalted is He—said, as related from His Prophet, peace be upon him: \"These are in Paradise, and I do not care; and these are in the Fire, and I do not care.\" Exalted is God, the King, the Truth! {لَا يُسْأَلُ عَمَّا يَفْعَلُ وَهُمْ يُسْأَلُونَ} \"He is not questioned about what He does, but they shall be questioned\" {الأنبياء/23}.","book_title":"Disciplining the Soul","section_title_ar":"Section 611","section_title_en":"The Explanation of the Heart's Rapid Fluctuation and the Division of Hearts Between Change and Constancy","section_index":611,"anchors":"- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of turning, overturning, and revolving a thing from one face to another; the heart is so named for its constant oscillation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart's essence is its perpetual turning and redirection; its fluctuation is not accidental but constitutive of its nature.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): physical overturning. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — the heart's movement between the two hosts (jundān) is a real, dynamic turnover of allegiance.\n- Root: شيط (sh-y-t)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Burning, scorching, or spoiling meat by over-smoking; blood going to waste.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The Shayṭān is so named for his being burned and banished from mercy.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Metaphorical: \"شاط دمه إذا بطل\" — the Shayṭān's work is to render the servant's spiritual life wasted and nullified, as blood spilled in vain.\n- Root: حزب (ḥ-z-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: A group or party with cohesion and strength; al-Ḥayzabūn (the old woman) shows the root's extension to a gathered mass.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحزب: جماعة فيها غلظ\" — the party of God (ḥizb Allāh) versus the party of the Shayṭān (ḥizb al-Shayṭān) are two opposing, organized camps in cosmic conflict.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: a band of partisans. Metaphorical: \"قرأ حزبه من القرآن\" — a portion assigned; here, the heart is assigned to one of the two parties by divine decree.\n- Root: خلق (kh-l-q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Measuring and smoothing; creating as precise estimation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الخلق أصله: التقدير المستقيم\" — creation is origination upon a just measure; God's creative act entails the pre-ordainment of ends.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: shaping leather before cutting. Metaphorical: \"خلق الله الخلق: أوجده على تقدير أوجبته الحكمة\" — God's creation of Paradise and Hellfire and their inhabitants is an act of wise, pre-eternal determination.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The phrase \"إنما ذكرنا منه ما يحتاج إليه\" restricts the author's purpose: only the necessary measure of the heart's wonders is expounded, not exhaustive treatment, signaling a deliberate boundary of the science of practical religion (ʿilm al-muʿāmalah).","content_type":"afterlife","topic":"Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Disciplining the Soul'","badge":"Afterlife"},{"arabic":"الذين أراد الله أن يهديهم فشرح صدورهم للإسلام والهدى والمغترون هم الذين أراد الله أن يضلهم فجعل صدرهم ضيقا حرجا كأنما يصعد في السماء والمغرور هو الذي لم تنفتح بصيرته ليكون بهداية نفسه كفيلا وبقي في العمى فاتخذ الهوى قائدا والشيطان دليلا {ومن كان في هذه أعمى فهو في الآخرة أعمى وأضل سبيلا} وإذا عرف أن الغرور 2 هو أم الشقاوات ومنبع المهلكات فلا بد من شرح مداخله ومجاريه وتفصيل ما يكثر من وقوع الغرور فيه ليحذره المريد بعد معرفته فيتقيه فالموفق من العباد من عرف مد","english":"Thus the shrewd ones (akyās) and the possessors of inner sight (arbāb al-baṣā'ir) — their hearts are like a niche (mishkāh) in which is a lamp (miṣbāḥ), the lamp in a glass, the glass as though it were a pearly star, kindled from a blessed olive tree, neither of the east nor of the west, whose oil would almost glow even if no fire touched it — light upon light (nūrun 'alā nūr). But the self-deceived (maghrūrūn) — their hearts are {«كَظُلُمَاتٍ فِي بَحْرٍ لُجِّيٍّ يَغْشَاهُ مَوْجٌ مِنْ فَوْقِهِ مَوْجٌ مِنْ فَوْقِهِ سَحَابٌ ظُلُمَاتٌ بَعْضُهَا فَوْقَ بَعْضٍ إِذَا أَخْرَجَ يَدَهُ لَمْ يَكَدْ يَرَاهَا وَمَنْ لَمْ يَجْعَلِ اللَّهُ لَهُ نُورًا فَمَا لَهُ مِنْ نُورٍ»} (Q. 24:40) — like darknesses in a vast deep sea, covered by a wave, above which is another wave, above which is a cloud — darknesses, one above another; when he stretches out his hand, he can scarcely see it. And he to whom God grants no light, for him there is no light.","book_title":"Condemnation of Delusion","section_title_ar":"Section 883","section_title_en":"The Book of Censure of Self-Deception (Kitāb Dhamm al-Ghurūr) — Book Ten of the Quarter of the Destroyers from The Revival of the Religious Sciences","section_index":883,"anchors":"- Root: غرر (gh-r-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core sense of an overlooked flaw, a heedless state, or a deceptive glitter; the \"white blaze\" on a horse's forehead that draws the eye but conceals the animal's true nature.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Ghurūr\" is a heedlessness (ghaflah) in wakefulness, rooted in the pursuit of a perceived gain (ghurrah) that is in fact a loss; it is the state of being deluded by the apparent good while the inner reality is ruinous, as in {«مَا غَرَّكَ بِرَبِّكَ الْكَرِيمِ»} (Q. 82:6).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): to approach someone off-guard (ghirrah). Metaphorical (majāz): the \"white-blazed day\" (yawm agharr) of intense heat — a dazzling surface that misleads; the deluded man is like a gazelle that grazes in moonlight, deceived into thinking it is daylight, until the predator seizes it.\n- Root: نور (n-w-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Light that renders things visible; the first thing created by God to dispel darkness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Two kinds: sensory light (perceived by the eye) and intellectual/divine light (perceived by the inner eye of insight, baṣīrah) — the latter being the light of reason and of the Qur'an, as in {«قَدْ جَاءَكُمْ مِنَ اللَّهِ نُورٌ وَكِتَابٌ مُبِينٌ»} (Q. 5:15).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: illumination of a lamp or fire. Metaphorical: clarification and manifestation — \"nawwara al-amr\" (he made the matter clear); the light of guidance is that which makes the path of salvation evident to the heart.\n- Root: صدر (ṣ-d-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The chest, the seat of the heart; also the \"foremost\" part of anything.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Literally the bodily chest; metaphorically extended to the \"front\" of a thing (ṣadr al-majlis, the head of the assembly). When used of the heart's expansion (sharḥ al-ṣadr), it denotes the spiritual opening of the breast to receive divine light and guidance, as in {«رَبِّ اشْرَحْ لِي صَدْرِي»} (Q. 20:25).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to return from water (ṣadara). Metaphorical: the beginning or origin of an affair — \"akhadha al-amra bi-ṣadrihi\" (he took the matter from its very outset); the \"sources and returns\" (mawārid wa maṣādir) of all things.\n- Root: ربع (r-b-')\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Four; the fourth part; a place of dwelling and spring pasturage.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The root of \"four\" and its derivatives; used for the four categories or classes into which a thing is divided.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to dwell (raba'a bi-l-makān). Metaphorical: to raise and restore (rabba'ahu Allāh — God raised him up); here, the \"Quarter of the Destroyers\" (rub' al-muhlikāt) is the fourth part of the Iḥyā' devoted to the vices that ruin the soul.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Interposition of the Prepositional Phrase (bāb al-faṣl bayna al-jār wa al-majrūr) — The periodic sentence structure in the opening doxology (\"الحمد لله الذي بيده مقاليد الأمور\") places the prepositional phrase \"bi-yadihi\" (in His hand) before its governing noun to achieve rhetorical emphasis (takhṣīṣ) and to signal the theological priority of divine agency over all affairs.","content_type":"spiritual","topic":"Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Condemnation of Delusion'","badge":"Insight"},{"arabic":"الله صلى الله عليه وسلم قوله تعالى فمن يرد الله أن يهديه يشرح صدره للإسلام فقيل له ما هذا الشرح فقال إن النور إذا قذف في القلب انشرح له الصدر وانفسح قيل فهل لذلك من علامة قال صلى الله عليه وسلم نعم التجافي عن دار الغرور والإنابة إلى دار الخلود والاستعداد للموت قبل نزوله (2) ومنها أن يكون أكثر بحثه عن علم الأعمال وعما يفسدها ويشوش القلوب ويهيج الوسواس ويثير الشر فإن أصل الدين التوقي من الشر ولذلك قيل عرفت الشر لا للشر لكن لتوقيه .","english":"As for the fear (al-khashyah), it is from His saying, the Exalted: {«إِنَّمَا يَخْشَى اللَّهَ مِنْ عِبَادِهِ الْعُلَمَاءُ»} — \"Only those of His servants who possess knowledge fear Allah\" (Fāṭir 35:28). For khashyah is a fear suffused with veneration, and it arises only from knowledge of the Mighty and Majestic — for whoever knows Him fears Him, and whoever does not know Him does not fear Him.","book_title":"Poverty and Abstinence","section_title_ar":"Section 61","section_title_en":"The Book of God's Book — Fear, Humility, and the Preference for the Hereafter","section_index":61,"anchors":"- Root: خشع (Khasha'a)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of lowliness, submissiveness, and being lowered to the ground.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الخشوع: الضراعة\" — Khushū' is a state of supplication and humility; most often used for what appears on the limbs, while ḍarā'ah is for the heart. When the heart humbles, the limbs follow.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"خشع له وتخشع: ذل وتطامن\" — to be lowly and submissive. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"أرض خاشعة\" — a land that is low-lying; \"خشعت الأصوات\" — voices lowered in awe.\n- Root: خشي (Khashiya)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Fear mixed with awe and reverence.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الخشية: خوف يشوبه تعظيم\" — Khashyah is fear suffused with veneration, and it most often arises from knowledge of what is feared.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Distinguished from mere fear (khawf) by its component of reverential awe.\n- Root: خلق (Khuluq)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Inherent disposition, character, nature.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الخلق أصله: التقدير المستقيم\" — Its root is right measure and proportion; used for the creation of things and for the moral constitution of the soul.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"خلق الخراز الأديم\" — the leather-worker cutting and measuring hide before cutting. Metaphorical: \"خلق الله الخلق\" — God originated creation upon a measure required by wisdom; \"وله خلق حسن\" — he has beautiful character.\n- Root: زهد (Zuhd)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Abstinence, renunciation, disinclination.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الزاهد في الشيء: الراغب عنه والراضي منه بالزهيد\" — The ascetic is one who turns away from a thing and is content with little of it.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"زهد في الشيء: رغب عنه\" — to turn away from something. Metaphorical: \"واد زهيد\" — a valley that takes little water; \"رجل زهيد\" — a man of little good.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Restriction and Focused Predication (باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما) — The particle \"innamā\" restricts the predicate to the subject alone, establishing a definitive causal linkage between knowledge and reverential fear.","content_type":"spiritual","topic":"Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Poverty and Abstinence'","badge":"Insight"}],"128":[{"arabic":"علامات المرض وبهذا يعرف أن القلوب كلها مريضة إلا ما شاء الله إلا أن من الأمراض ما لا يعرفها صاحبها ومرض القلب مما لا يعرفه صاحبه فلذلك يغفل عنه وإن عرفه صعب عليه الصبر على مرارة دوائه فإن دواءه مخالفة الشهوات وهو نزع الروح فإن وجد من نفسه قوة الصبر عليه لم يجد طبيبا حاذقا يعالجه فإن الأطباء هم العلماء وقد استولى عليهم المرض فالطبيب المريض قلما يلتفت إلى علاجه فلهذا صار الداء عضالا والمرض مزمنا واندرس هذا العلم وأنكر بالكلية طب القلوب وأنكر مرضها وأقبل الخلق على حب الدنيا وعلى أعمال ظاهرها عبادات وباطنها","english":"I say: The soul (nafs) that belongs to the human being — that by which he is distinguished from the beasts — for he is not distinguished from them by the power of eating, copulation, sight, or any other such thing. Rather, he is distinguished by the knowledge of things as they truly are. And the Origin of all things, their Existentiator and their Inventor, is God, Mighty and Majestic — He who made them things. So if one knew everything but did not know God, Mighty and Majestic, it would be as though he knew nothing at all. And the sign of knowledge is love: whoever knows God Most High loves Him. And the sign of love is that one does not prefer over Him this world nor any other beloved thing. As God Most High said: {«Say: If your fathers, your sons, your brothers, your spouses, your clans — the wealth you have acquired, commerce whose decline you fear, and dwellings you find pleasing — are more beloved to you than God and His Messenger and striving in His cause, then wait until God brings about His command»} [Q 9:24]. So whoever possesses something more beloved to him than God — his heart is sick. Just as any stomach to which clay has become more beloved than bread and water, or whose appetite for bread and water has fallen away — it is sick. These are the signs of sickness. And by this it is known that all hearts are sick — except what God wills. Yet among sicknesses there are those the sufferer does not recognize; and the sickness of the heart is among those the sufferer does not recognize. For this reason he is heedless of it. And even if he recognized it, patience upon the bitterness of its medicine would be difficult for him — for its medicine is opposing the appetites, and that is the tearing out of the spirit (nafs). And if he found in himself the strength of patience upon it, he would not find a skillful physician to treat him — for the physicians are the scholars, and sickness has overcome them. So the sick physician rarely attends to his own cure. For this reason the malady has become chronic and the sickness chronic. This science has become effaced, and the medicine of hearts is denied entirely, and the sickness of the heart is denied. And creation has turned to the love of this world and to deeds whose outward form is acts of worship but whose inward reality is habit and ostentation. These are the signs of the roots of sicknesses.","book_title":"Remembrance of Death and Afterlife","section_title_ar":"Section 624","section_title_en":"The Soul's Distinction, the Sickness of the Heart, and the Path of Moderation","section_index":624,"anchors":"- Root: نفس (n-f-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, soul, or spirit; that by which a thing is distinguished in its essential reality\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The soul (nafs) as the principle of rational discrimination and knowledge, distinguishing humans from beasts\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the self or person; Metaphorical: the rational soul as the seat of knowledge and moral responsibility\n- Root: مرض (m-r-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Departure from the state of health and equilibrium\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Sickness of the heart as a metaphor for moral and spiritual vices — ignorance, cowardice, avarice, hypocrisy\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: bodily illness; Metaphorical: spiritual malady whereby the heart inclines toward what is harmful and away from what is beneficial\n- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The heart; that which is ever-turning and fluctuating between states\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The heart as the seat of understanding and moral perception; its \"turning\" (taqlīb) signifies its instability between soundness and sickness\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the physical organ; Metaphorical: the spiritual faculty of cognition and moral discernment\n- Root: خلق (kh-l-q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Measured, proportioned disposition; character and temperament\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Moral character as the inward form (ṣūrah bāṭinah) corresponding to the outward physical form\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: measuring and proportioning; Metaphorical: the ingrained disposition that governs action\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The use of restrictive constructions to isolate the defining characteristic of the human soul as knowledge of God, not animal appetites","content_type":"spiritual","topic":"Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'","badge":"Insight"}],"129":[{"arabic":"أولياء وقال تعالى وكذلك نولي بعض الظالمين بعضا وفي الخبر إن الله تعالى أخذ الميثاق على كل مؤمن أن يبغض كل منافق وعلى كل منافق أن يبغض كل مؤمن (3) وقال عليه السلام المرء مع من أحب (4) وقال من أحب قوما ووالاهم حشر معهم يوم القيامة (5) وقال عليه السلام أوثق عرى الإيمان الحب في الله والبغض في الله (6) وشواهد هذا قد ذكرناها في بيان الحب والبغض في الله تعالى من كتاب آداب الصحبة وفي كتاب الأمر بالمعروف والنهي عن المنكر فلا نعيده فإن قلت فقد وردت الآيات والأخبار بالرضا بقضاء الله تعالى (7) فإن كانت المعاصي بغير","english":"...as a partner in his killing. And God—exalted be He—has commanded envy and competition in good things and the warding off of evils, for He said—exalted be He—{وَفِي ذَٰلِكَ فَلْيَتَنَافَسِ الْمُتَنَافِسُونَ} [\"So for this let the competitors compete\"] (Al-Muṭaffifīn 83:26). And the Prophet—peace be upon him—said: \"There is no envy except in two: a man whom God has given wisdom, and he disseminates it among the people and teaches it; and a man whom God has given wealth, and he has been empowered over it to spend it in the truth.\" In another wording: \"And a man whom God has given the Qur'an, and he stands with it through the hours of the night and the day; then a man says: 'If God had given me the like of what He gave this one, I would have done the like of what he does.'\"","book_title":"Remembrance of Death and Afterlife","section_title_ar":"Section 1196","section_title_en":"On the Reconciliation Between Hatred of Sinners and Satisfaction with Divine Decree — Section 1196","section_index":1196,"anchors":"- Root: بغض (b-gh-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning of aversion, repugnance, and the soul's flight from what it disdains; al-baghḍā' as intense enmity.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"البغض: نفار النفس عن الشيء الذي ترغب عنه، وهو ضد الحب\" — hatred is the soul's aversion from what it shuns, the exact opposite of love's attraction; used theologically for God's repudiation of the disobedient.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) usage denotes personal antipathy; metaphorical (majāz) extension applies to religiously mandated aversion (al-bughḍ fī Allāh) as an act of devotion, not private spite.\n- Root: وجه (w-j-h)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The face as the foremost and noblest part; by extension, an aspect, direction, or respect under which a thing is considered.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"ولما كان الوجه أول ما يستقبلك، وأشرف ما في ظاهر البدن استعمل في مستقبل كل شيء\" — the face is that which first confronts you, hence used for every aspect or angle of consideration.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the bodily face; metaphorical: \"هذا وجه الثوب\" (the face/right side of a garment) — i.e., a facet or dimension of a single act that may be evaluated differently.\n- Root: كره (k-r-h)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Dislike, detestation, and unwillingness; karāhah as the soul's resistance to what befalls it.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Karāhah is the soul's repugnance toward something, distinguished from ridā (satisfaction) which is the soul's acquiescence and embrace.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): The rhetorical key here is that karāhah and ridā are contraries only when they \"تواردا على شيء واحد من جهة واحدة\" — converge on one thing from one single respect; from different respects, no contradiction arises.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The resolution of the apparent contradiction hinges on precise restriction (taqyīd): the act is hated \"من حيث إنه وصف العبد وكسبه\" (insofar as it is the servant's attribute and acquisition) and loved \"من حيث إنه مراد الله\" (insofar as it is God's will and decree). The predicate is qualified by its aspect, eliminating the logical clash.","content_type":"spiritual","topic":"Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'","badge":"Insight"}],"132":[{"arabic":"يقدر على الإخبار عنه لاشتغال همه به عن ثوبه وعن الحاضرين حواليه ولكل درجات مما عملوا فحظ كل واحد من صلاته بقدر خوفه وخشوعه وتعظيمه فإن موضع نظر الله سبحانه القلوب دون ظاهر الحركات ولذلك قال بعض الصحابة رضي الله عنهم يحشر الناس يوم القيامة على مثال هيئتهم في الصلاة من الطمأنينة والهدوء ومن وجود النعيم بها واللذة ولقد صدق فإنه يحشر كل على ما مات عليه ويموت على ما عاش عليه ويراعى في ذلك حال قلبه لا حال شخصه فمن صفات القلوب تصاغ الصور في الدار الآخرة ولا ينجو إلا من أتى الله بقلب سليم نسأل الله حسن التوفيق بلطفه وكرمه","english":"The bond linking all these causes is faith (al-īmān) and certainty (al-yaqīn)—by which I mean these very knowledges we have mentioned. The meaning of their being \"certain\" is the absence of doubt and their full dominion over the heart, as has preceded in the exposition of certainty in the Book of Knowledge. And in proportion to one's certainty, the heart becomes humble (yakhsha'). For this reason, ʿĀ'ishah—may God be pleased with her—said: \"The Messenger of God—may God's peace and blessings be upon him—would converse with us and we with him; but when the time of prayer arrived, it was as though he knew us not, nor we him.\" It has also been related that God—Glory be to Him—revealed to Moses—peace be upon him: \"O Moses, when you remember Me, remember Me while your limbs tremble, and be, at the remembrance of Me, humble and tranquil. When you remember Me, place your tongue behind your heart. And when you stand before Me, stand as a lowly servant stands, and call upon Me with a fearful heart and a truthful tongue.\" It was also related that God—Exalted is He—revealed to him: \"Say to the disobedient ones of your community: Let them not remember Me, for I have sworn upon Myself that whoever remembers Me, I will remember him; so if they remember Me, I will remember them with a curse.\" This concerns a disobedient one who is not heedless in his remembrance—how then when heedlessness and disobedience are combined?","book_title":"Mysteries of Prayer","section_title_ar":"Section 135","section_title_en":"Exposition of the Inner Meanings by Which the Life of Prayer is Perfected","section_index":135,"anchors":"- Root: خوف (kh-w-f)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core sense of fear, dread, apprehension of harm or punishment.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Fear (al-khawf) is the disturbance of the heart due to anticipation of something detestable; its theological counterpart is hope (al-rajā'), both being states consequent upon knowledge (ma'rifah).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqiqah) usage denotes fear of a tangible threat; metaphorical (majaz) usage extends to reverential awe (khashyah) of God's majesty, which is a state of the heart, not a bodily reaction.\n- Root: رجو (r-j-w)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To hope, expect, desire something good; the opposite of despair (ya's).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Hope (al-rajā') is the heart's tranquil expectation of a beloved matter after its causes have been secured; it is not mere wishful thinking (tamannī) but arises necessarily from certainty in God's promise and knowledge of His bounty.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal usage is anticipation of a favorable outcome; metaphorical usage in theology denotes the soul's reliance on God's grace (karam) as a motivating spiritual state.\n- Root: حيي (ḥ-y-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Life; also shame, modesty (al-ḥayā'), derived from the sense of recoiling from base acts as if by a vital instinct.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Shame (al-ḥayā') is a state of the soul that restrains one from blameworthy acts, arising from the perception of one's own deficiency before the majesty of another; it is a moral faculty, not a mere emotion.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal usage is bashfulness before people; metaphorical usage is the reverential awe before God, born of the awareness of His omniscience over hidden thoughts.\n- Root: خشع (kh-sh-')\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To be lowly, submissive, humbled; physical stillness and lowering of the gaze.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Khushū' is the heart's humility and stillness before God, manifesting in the body's tranquility; it is the fruit of certainty (yaqīn).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal usage is bodily stillness; metaphorical usage is the heart's submissiveness, which is the true locus of prayer's life.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of 'Aṭf al-Bayān (Appositional Clarification) — The phrase \"أعني به\" (I mean by it) functions as explanatory apposition, specifying that the preceding term (al-īmān) is identical to the enumerated knowledges (al-ma'ārif), ensuring precise referential identity without ambiguity.","content_type":"spiritual","topic":"Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Mysteries of Prayer'","badge":"Insight"}],"134":[{"arabic":"بني آدم إن كنتم تعقلون فعدوا أنفسكم من الموتى والذي نفسي بيده إن ما توعدون لآت وما أنتم بمعجزين (3) وعن ابن عباس رضي الله عنهما إن رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم كان يخرج يهريق الماء فيتمسح بالتراب فأقول له يا رسول الله إن الماء منك قريب فيقول ما يدريني لعلي لا أبلغه (4) والبزاز بسند ضعيف وروي أنه صلى الله عليه وسلم أخذ ثلاثة أعواد فغرز عودا بين يديه والآخر إلى جنبه وأما الثالث فأبعده فقال هل تدرون ما هذا قالوا الله ورسوله أعلم قال هذا الإنسان وهذا الأجل وذاك الأمل يتعاطاه ابن آدم ويختلجه الأجل دون الأمل (5) وقال عليه السلام مثل ابن آدم وإلى","english":"The Messenger of God, may God's blessings and peace be upon him, said to ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar: \"When you reach the morning, do not speak to yourself of the evening; and when you reach the evening, do not speak to yourself of the morning. Take from your life for your death, and from your health for your sickness. For you, O ʿAbd Allāh, do not know what your name will be tomorrow.\" {«إذا أصبحت فلا تحدث نفسك بالمساء وإذا أمسيت فلا تحدث نفسك بالصباح وخذ من حياتك لموتك ومن صحتك لسقمك فإنك يا عبد الله لا تدري ما اسمك غدا»}","book_title":"Remembrance of Death and Afterlife","section_title_ar":"Section 1286","section_title_en":"The Cutting of Hope (Qaṣr al-Amal) — Section 1286","section_index":1286,"anchors":"- Root: أمل (amal)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الأمل: رجاء الشيء وتوقع حصوله، والأمل: امتداد النفس في طلب الخير المستقبل؛ ومنه قولهم: أَمَلَ يَأْمُلُ أَمْلاً، وهو تمديد النظر إلى ما يُستبعد حصوله.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الأمل: توقع حصول الأمر المستقبل، وهو مذموم إذا جاوز الحد المشروع لأنه يلهي عن الاستعداد للآخرة؛ والأجل: غاية العمر المضروب، والفرق بينهما أن الأجل محتوم والأمل ممدود.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"Literal (Haqiqah): الأمل تمديد النفس إلى بلوغ المراد. Metaphorical (Majaz): طول الأمل كناية عن الغفلة عن قرب الأجل والانكباب على الدنيا، وقصر الأمل عبارة عن الاستعداد للموت والزهد.\"\n- Root: أجل (ajal)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الأجل: غاية الوقت في الموت وغيره، والأجل المسمى: الوقت المضروب لانقضاء العمر.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الأجل: مدة الشيء المضروبة لانتهائه، وأجل الإنسان: الوقت الذي ينتهي فيه عمره، وهو بيد الله وحده.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"Literal: الأجل نهاية العمر المحتومة. Metaphorical: جعل الأجل محيطًا بالإنسان كالخط المحيط بالمركز.\"\n- Root: مني (munya)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"المنية: الموت، وأصلها من التمني لأن الموت يتمنى أو لأنه مقدر مكتوب.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"المنية: الموت المحتوم، وجمعها منايا، وهي الآفات المهلكة التي تترصد الإنسان.\"\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Used in the prophetic statement \"إنما يلقاك غدا ندمك\" to restrict the outcome exclusively to regret, and \"إنما يكون الأمان غدا لمن خاف واتقى\" to confine safety solely to the God-fearing.","content_type":"afterlife","topic":"Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'","badge":"Afterlife"}],"144":[{"arabic":"الله تعالى بين من افترى على الله كذبا وبين من كذب بالحق فقال تعالى ومن أظلم ممن افترى على الله كذبا أو كذب بالحق لما جاءه وقال تعالى فمن أظلم ممن كذب على الله وكذب بالصدق إذ جاءه ومنها الرياء وملاحظة الخلق والجهد في استمالة قلوبهم وصرف وجوههم والرياء هو الداء العضال الذي يدعو إلى أكبر الكبائر كما سيأتي في كتاب الرياء والمناظر لا يقصد إلا الظهور عند الخلق وانطلاق ألسنتهم بالثناء عليه فهذه عشر خصال من أمهات الفواحش الباطنة سوى ما يتفق لغير المتماسكين منهم من الخصام المؤدي إلى الضرب","english":"**QUAD_ANCHORS:**\n- Root: نظر (naẓara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to look, to examine, to consider; al-munāẓarah is mutual examination/argumentation between two parties.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: naẓar as intellectual contemplation (taʾammul bi-l-baṣīrah); munāẓarah as dialectical exchange.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: sitting face-to-face in debate; Metaphorical: disputation for victory.\n- Root: غلب (ghalaba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to overcome, to conquer, to prevail.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ghalabah as seeking to dominate another in argument.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: victory in combat; Metaphorical: intellectual supremacy.\n- Root: حسد (ḥasada)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to envy, to wish removal of blessings from another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: envy as wishing deprivation of blessings from the deserving — among the basest traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: envy of another's blessing; Metaphorical: consuming fire of the soul.\n- Root: كبر (kabura)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be great, to be haughty.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-kibr as self-elevation above others; condemned by God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: greatness; Metaphorical: arrogance and pride.\n- Root: غيب (ghāba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be absent, to be hidden.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ghībah as mentioning another with what he dislikes in his absence — likened to eating carrion.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: backbiting; Metaphorical: consuming the flesh of one's brother.\n- Root: راءى (rāʾā)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to show off, to display.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-riyāʾ as performing acts for the sake of being seen — hidden shirk.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: showing good deeds; Metaphorical: seeking reputation.\n- Root: نفس (nafs)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: self, soul, spirit.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: tazkiyat al-nafs as self-praise and self-purification — forbidden by God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: self; Metaphorical: self-commendation.\n- Root: حقd (ḥaqida)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to bear malice, to harbor resentment.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ḥiqd as nursing enmity in the heart — negated from the believer.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: bearing grudge; Metaphorical: hidden rancor.\n- Root: نفاق (nifāq)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: hypocrisy, showing contrary to what one conceals.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: hypocrisy in deeds — outward display contrary to inward state.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: hypocrisy in faith; Metaphorical: hypocrisy in character.\n- Root: علم (ʿalima)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to know, to perceive.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: knowledge as apprehension of reality — its pursuit for other than God is destructive.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: knowledge; Metaphorical: the dangerous burden of the scholar.\n- Root: جدل (jadala)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to argue, to dispute.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-jidāl as contention; al-mirāʾ as disputation with falsehood — condemned.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: argumentation; Metaphorical: obstinate disputation.\n- Root: شرك (sharaka)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to associate, to share.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-riyāʾ as hidden shirk — associating others with God in intention.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: polytheism; Metaphorical: hidden association in deeds.\n- Root: موت (māta)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to die.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: death as separation of soul from body; spiritual death as ruin of the soul.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical death; Metaphorical: spiritual destruction.\n- Root: حيى (ḥayiya)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to live.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: life as the power of perception; eternal life as the reward of the righteous.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical life; Metaphorical: spiritual vitality.\n- Root: هلك (halaka)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to perish, to be destroyed.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-halāk as ruin; eternal destruction as everlasting punishment.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: destruction; Metaphorical: eternal ruin.\n- Root: رياء (riyāʾ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: showing off, ostentation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: riyāʾ as the incurable disease calling to the greatest sins.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: ostentation; Metaphorical: spiritual disease.\n- Root: جاه (jāh)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: rank, status, prestige.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: love of status (ḥubb al-jāh) among the destructive traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: position; Metaphorical: seeking prestige.\n- Root: مباهاة (mubāhāh)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: mutual boasting, vying in splendor.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: mubāhāh as each seeking to display superiority over the other.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: mutual boasting; Metaphorical: rivalry in honor.\n- Root: مماراة (mumārāh)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: disputation, wrangling.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-mirāʾ as contentious argument — condemned by the Prophet.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: disputation; Metaphorical: obstinate wrangling.\n- Root: فخر (fakhara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to boast, to pride oneself.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-fakhr as self-glorification — among the blameworthy traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: boasting; Metaphorical: vainglory.\n- Root: عجب (ʿajiba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to wonder, to be amazed.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ʿujb as self-admiration — among the destructive traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder; Metaphorical: self-conceit.\n- Root: بغض (baghiḍa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to hate, to detest.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-baghdāʾ as mutual hatred — contrary to brotherhood.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: hatred; Metaphorical: mutual enmity.\n- Root: غضب (ghaḍiba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be angry.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ghaḍab as anger — among the blameworthy traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: anger; Metaphorical: rage.\n- Root: طمع (ṭamiʿa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to covet, to desire.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ṭamaʿ as covetousness — among the destructive traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: desire; Metaphorical: greed.\n- Root: أشر (ashira)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be exultant, to be insolent.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ashar as excessive joy and insolence — blameworthy.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: exultation; Metaphorical: insolence.\n- Root: بطر (baṭira)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be arrogant, to be ungrateful.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-baṭar as arrogance in prosperity — among the destructive traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: arrogance; Metaphorical: ingratitude for blessings.\n- Root: خيلاء (khuyalāʾ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: pride, haughtiness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-khuyalāʾ as pride and conceit — blameworthy.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: pride; Metaphorical: haughty bearing.\n- Root: خشية (khashyah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: fear, awe.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-khashyah as reverential fear — among the traits of the believers.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: fear; Metaphorical: reverential awe.\n- Root: رحمة (raḥmah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: mercy, compassion.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-raḥmah as tenderness of heart — among the traits of the believers.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: mercy; Metaphorical: compassion.\n- Root: غفلة (ghaflah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: heedlessness, inattention.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ghaflah as heedlessness — contrary to vigilance.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: inattention; Metaphorical: spiritual heedlessness.\n- Root: خشوع (khushūʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: humility, submissiveness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-khushūʿ as humility — among the traits of the believers.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: humility; Metaphorical: devotional submission.\n- Root: تذكير (tadhkīr)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: reminding, admonishing.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-tadhkīr as reminding of God — a righteous act.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: reminding; Metaphorical: preaching.\n- Root: وعظ (waʿaẓa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to admonish, to exhort.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-waʿẓ as exhortation to obedience and warning against disobedience.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: admonition; Metaphorical: sermonizing.\n- Root: فتوى (fatwā)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: legal ruling, formal opinion.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-fatwā as clarification of legal rulings.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: legal opinion; Metaphorical: religious authority.\n- Root: قضاء (qaḍāʾ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: judgment, decree.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-qaḍāʾ as judgment between people.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: judgment; Metaphorical: judicial authority.\n- Root: وقف (waqafa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to stop, to stand.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-waqf as endowment of property for charitable purposes.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: standing; Metaphorical: religious endowment.\n- Root: ثواب (thawāb)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: reward, recompense.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-thawāb as divine recompense for righteous deeds.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: reward; Metaphorical: divine recompense.\n- Root: عذاب (ʿadhāb)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: punishment, torment.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ʿadhāb as divine punishment for the disobedient.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: punishment; Metaphorical: divine chastisement.\n- Root: ملك (malaka)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to possess, to rule.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-mulk as sovereignty; al-mulk al-muʾabbad as eternal sovereignty in Paradise.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: dominion; Metaphorical: eternal kingdom.\n- Root: نعيم (naʿīm)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: bliss, delight.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-naʿīm as bliss; al-naʿīm al-sarmad as everlasting bliss.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: delight; Metaphorical: eternal bliss.\n- Root: رخصة (rukhaṣah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: concession, dispensation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-rukhaṣah as legal concession.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: concession; Metaphorical: legal dispensation.\n- Root: رياسة (riyāsah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: leadership, headship.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: love of leadership among the destructive traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: leadership; Metaphorical: seeking authority.\n- Root: اندراس (indirās)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: effacement, obliteration.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: indirās al-ʿulūm as the obliteration of the sciences.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: effacement; Metaphorical: extinction of knowledge.\n- Root: صبيان (ṣibyān)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: boys, children.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: children's motivation for learning through play.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: boys; Metaphorical: pupils.\n- Root: مكتب (maktab)\n  * Lisan / Ayn- Root: مكتب (maktab)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: place of instruction, school for boys.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: the elementary school where children are taught.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: school; Metaphorical: place of elementary learning.\n- Root: خلاق (khilāq)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: portion, share, lot.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: \"no portion\" (lā khilāq) in the Hereafter — utter deprivation of divine reward.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: share; Metaphorical: spiritual allotment in the afterlife.\n- Root: فاجر (fājir)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: wicked, immoral, profligate.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: the dissolute one who commits sins openly.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: sinner; Metaphorical: one through whom God nonetheless supports religion.\n- Root: شمع (shamʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: candle, wax taper.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: the candle as parable — consuming itself while giving light to others.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: candle; Metaphorical: one who destroys himself but benefits others.\n- Root: نار (nār)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: fire.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: fire as parable — consuming itself and all around it.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: fire; Metaphorical: one who destroys himself and others.\n- Root: سعد (saʿida)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be fortunate, to prosper.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: saʿādah as felicity — success in the Hereafter.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: good fortune; Metaphorical: eternal felicity.\n- Root: شقي (shaqiya)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be wretched, to be miserable.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: shaqāwah as wretchedness — ruin in the Hereafter.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: misery; Metaphorical: eternal ruin.\n- Root: أخلص (akhlaṣa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to purify, to be sincere.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: ikhlāṣ as purifying intention for God alone — the condition of acceptance.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: sincerity; Metaphorical: pure devotion.\n- Root: ريب (rāba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to doubt, to suspect.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-rayb as doubt and uncertainty — to be dispelled by evidence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: doubt; Metaphorical: skepticism.","book_title":"Book of Knowledge","section_title_ar":"Section 39","section_title_en":"Section 39","section_index":39,"anchors":"*(Anchors established)*","content_type":"ethics","topic":"Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Book of Knowledge'","badge":"Ethics"}],"148":[{"arabic":"أيضا {لو شاء الله ما أشركنا ولا آباؤنا} فانظر كيف كانوا صادقين في كلامهم وكيف هلكوا بصدقهم فسبحان من إذا شاء أهلك بالصدق وإذا شاء أسعد بالجهل {يضل به كثيرا ويهدي به كثيرا} فهؤلاء لما ظنوا أنهم استخدموا لأجل المساكين والفقراء أو جل الله تعالى ثم قالوا لا حظ لنا في المساكين ولا حظ لله فينا وفي أموالنا سواء أنفقنا أو أمسكنا هلكوا كما هلك الصبي لما ظن أن مقصود الوالد استخدامه لأجل","english":"Likewise, if it were said: \"Is oxymel (sikanjabīn) better, or the drink of the water-lily (sharāb al-līnūfar)?\" — no unqualified answer could be correct at all. Indeed, if it were said to us: \"Is oxymel better, or the absence of yellow bile?\" — we would say: the absence of yellow bile, for oxymel is sought for the sake of something else, and that which is sought for its own sake is necessarily better than that which is sought for the sake of another.","book_title":"Patience and Gratitude","section_title_ar":"Section 1025","section_title_en":"The Superiority of Knowledge Over Action — The Parable of the Physician and the Child","section_index":1025,"anchors":"- Root: فضل (faḍl)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Excess or increase beyond what is required; superiority in degree.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الفضل: الزيادة عن الاقتصاد\" — excess beyond moderation, divided into praiseworthy (like excess of knowledge) and blameworthy; comparative superiority of one thing over another by genus, species, or merit.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"فاضلني فلان ففضلته\" — to surpass in excellence. Here used comparatively: one act or state exceeds another in rank.\n- Root: علم (ʿilm)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Knowledge as the apprehension of a thing's reality; the mark or sign by which something is known.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العلم: إدراك الشيء بحقيقته\" — apprehension of a thing's true reality, whether by direct perception of its essence or by judgment concerning it.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"هو من أعلام العلم الخافقه\" — knowledge as a lofty, manifest sign; distinguished from mere action which is its instrument.\n- Root: صدق (ṣidq)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Truthfulness primarily in speech — correspondence of report with reality; may become blameworthy when it leads to ruin, as with the disbelievers who spoke truly about God's independence yet drew false conclusions.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"صدقته الحديث\" — truthful in statement. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"رجل صادق الحملة\" — truthful in resolve. Here: the disbelievers were literally truthful in their words yet destroyed by that very truthfulness.\n- Root: هلك (halak)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Destruction on three levels: loss of something from one's possession, corruption and dissolution, and death. Used here for spiritual ruin through misapplied reasoning.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"وقعوا في المهلكة\" — falling into destruction. Metaphorical: \"تهالك على الشيء\" — intense craving. Here: literal perdition of the soul through false inference.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The conditional structure \"فإن قلت... فاعلم\" (if you say... then know) governs the entire periodic sentence, requiring the apodosis to resolve the protasis with logical necessity, as in the physician analogy where praise of the medicine does not entail its intrinsic superiority.","content_type":"general","topic":"Classical commentary from Al-Ghazali's Ihya Ulum al-Din, from 'Patience and Gratitude'","badge":"Commentary"}],"149":[{"arabic":"هل عندكم من سلطان بهذا أي حجة وبرهان وقال تعالى قل فلله الحجة البالغة وقال تعالى ألم تر إلى الذي حاج إبراهيم في ربه إلى قوله فبهت الذي كفر إذ ذكر سبحانه احتجاج إبراهيم ومجادلته وإفحامه خصمه في معرض الثناء عليه وقال عز وجل وتلك حجتنا آتيناها إبراهيم على قومه وقال تعالى قالوا يا نوح قد جادلتنا فأكثرت جدالنا وقال تعالى في قصة فرعون وما رب العالمين إلى قوله أولوا جئتك بشيء مبين وعلى الجملة فالقرآن من أوله إلى آخره محاجة مع الكفار فعمدة أدلة المتكلمين في التوحيد قوله تعالى {لو كان فيهما آلهة إلا الله لفسدتا} وفي النبوة","english":"The mention of the proof (ḥujjah), the demand for it, and the search after it — how could these be prohibited, when God — may He be exalted — has said: {«قُلْ هَاتُوا بُرْهَانَكُمْ»} (Say: Bring forth your proof — Qur'an 2:111), and He — mighty and majestic — has said: {«لِيَهْلِكَ مَنْ هَلَكَ عَنْ بَيِّنَةٍ وَيَحْيَا مَنْ حَيَّ عَنْ بَيِّنَةٍ»} (So that he who perishes may perish upon clear evidence, and he who lives may live upon clear evidence — Qur'an 8:42), and He — may He be exalted — has said: {«هَلْ عِنْدَكُمْ مِنْ سُلْطَانٍ بِهَذَا»} (Have you any authority for this? — Qur'an 10:68), meaning a proof and demonstration, and He — may He be exalted — has said: {«قُلْ فَلِلَّهِ الْحُجَّةُ الْبَالِغَةُ»} (Say: To God belongs the conclusive proof — Qur'an 6:149), and He — may He be exalted — has said: {«أَلَمْ تَرَ إِلَى الَّذِي حَاجَّ إِبْرَاهِيمَ فِي رَبِّهِ»} (Have you not considered him who disputed with Abraham concerning his Lord? — Qur'an 2:258) up to His words: {«فَبُهِتَ الَّذِي كَفَرَ»} (So the one who disbelieved was confounded — Qur'an 2:258).","book_title":"Foundations of Belief","section_title_ar":"Section 77","section_title_en":"The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 77: On the Permissibility of Theological Discourse (Kalām) and Its Early Practice","section_index":77,"anchors":"- Root: ح-د-ث (ḥ-d-th)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Al-ḥudūth: the coming-to-be of a thing after it was not; al-ḥadath: the novel event; al-ḥīdāth: origination.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-ḥudūth: a thing's becoming after it was not, whether accident or substance; iḥdāth: bringing it into existence. The origination of substances belongs to God alone.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"Literal: 'He is a recent event among events.' Metaphorical: 'They became topics of conversation (aḥādīth).'\"\n- Root: ح-ا-ج (ḥ-ā-j)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Al-ḥājah: need of a thing, with desire for it; its plural: ḥājāt and ḥawā'ij.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-ḥājah ilā al-shay': poverty toward it with love of it. God said: {«إِلَّا حَاجَةً فِي نَفْسِ يَعْقُوبَ»} (except a need in Jacob's soul — Qur'an 12:68).\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"Literal: need and want. The 'ḥājah' is that which one seeks to obtain.\"\n- Root: ك-و-ن (k-w-n)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Kāna: to be, to exist; takwīn: bringing into being from non-existence.\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-kawn: existence after non-existence; the muḥdath (originated thing) is that brought forth after it was not.\"\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"Literal: being and becoming. Metaphorical: the continuous states of affairs.\"\n- Sibawayh Rule: Al-Faṣl bayna al-Jār wa-al-Majrūr (Prepositional Interposition) — The permissibility of separating a preposition from its object when the intervening clause is complete in meaning, as in the verse's structure of argumentation where the proof (ḥujjah) stands independently before its conclusion.","content_type":"spiritual","topic":"Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Foundations of Belief'","badge":"Insight"}],"151":[{"arabic":"ولأجل غيرة الله تعالى حرم الفواحش ما ظهر منها وما بطن ولا أحد أحب إليه العذر من الله ولذلك بعث المنذرين والمبشرين ولا أحد أحب إليه المدح من الله ولأجل ذلك وعد الجنة وقال رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم رأيت ليلة أسري بي في الجنة قصرا وبفنائه جارية فقلت لمن هذا القصر فقيل لعمر فأردت أن أنظر إليها فذكرت غيرتك يا عمر فبكى عمر وقال أعليك أغار يا رسول الله (6) وكان الحسن يقول أتدعون نساءكم ليزاحمن العلوج في الأسواق قبح الله من لا يغار وقال عليه الصلاة والسلام إن من الغيرة ما يحبه الله ومنها ما يبغ","english":"The first point of etiquette is the wedding feast (walīmah), and it is recommended (mustaḥabbah). Anas, may God be pleased with him, said: The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, saw upon ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, may God be pleased with him, a trace of yellow dye, so he said: {«What is this?»} He replied: \"I have married a woman for the weight of a date-stone in gold.\" He said: \"May God bless you. Hold a wedding feast, even if with a single sheep.\" {«...»} And the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, held a wedding feast for Ṣafiyyah with dates and sawīq (barley porridge). {«...»} And he, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: \"The food of the first day is an obligation (ḥaqq), the food of the second day is a Sunnah, and the food of the third day is for show (sumʿah); and whoever seeks to be heard, God will make his deeds heard.\" {«...»} None raised this hadith except Ziyād ibn ʿAbd Allāh, and it is anomalous (gharīb). It is recommended to offer congratulations to him, and the one who enters upon the husband should say: \"May God bless you and shower blessings upon you, and may He bring you both together in goodness.\" {«...»} Abū Hurayrah, may God be pleased with him, reported that he, may God bless him and grant him peace, commanded this. It is also recommended to make the marriage public (iẓhār al-nikāḥ). He, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: \"The distinction between the lawful and the unlawful is the drum and the voice.\" {«...»} The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: \"Announce this marriage, hold it in the mosques, and beat drums for it.\" {«...»} And on the authority of al-Rubayyiʿ bint Muʿawwidh, she said: The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, came and entered on the morning of my wedding night, and he sat upon my bedding, and we had young girls with us who were beating their drums and lamenting those of my forefathers who had been killed—until one of them said: \"And among us is a Prophet who knows what will be tomorrow.\" He said to her: \"Stop this, and say what you were saying before.\" {«...»}","book_title":"Etiquette of Marriage","section_title_ar":"Section 303","section_title_en":"On What is Incumbent Upon the Husband and What is Incumbent Upon the Wife","section_index":303,"anchors":"- Root: و ل م (w-l-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The core meaning pertains to the wedding feast (walīmah), derived from the concept of gathering and union.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological usage, it signifies the communal meal marking the consummation of a marriage contract, a recommended act of public gratitude.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage denotes the specific feast; metaphorical (Majaz) extension applies to any act of publicizing a joyous occasion.\n- Root: ع ش ر (ʿ- sh-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes association, companionship, and intimacy; the tenth (ʿasharah) of a thing is related, but the primary social sense is cohabitation and mutual dealing.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In the Quranic context of {وَعَاشِرُوهُنَّ بِالْمَعْرُوفِ}, it signifies a sustained, equitable mode of social intercourse grounded in moral rectitude, not mere physical proximity.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is the act of living together; metaphorical (Majaz) usage elevates it to a standard of ethical conduct (maʿrūf) that defines the marital bond.\n- Root: ق س م (q-s-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root indicates division, apportionment, and distribution; in the marital context, it refers to the equitable division of time among co-wives.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theologically, it is tied to justice (ʿadl) as a divine attribute; for the husband, it is a legal obligation to maintain fairness in material and temporal allotments.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is the physical act of dividing; metaphorical (Majaz) usage applies to the moral duty of impartiality in affection and provision.\n- Root: غ ي ر (gh-y-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root conveys alteration and change, but in the moral register, it denotes protective jealousy (ghayrah), a state of aversion to the violation of honor.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In Kalam, ghayrah is a praiseworthy emotion when aligned with divine law—jealousy for the sanctity of the family—and blameworthy when it stems from unjust suspicion (sūʾ al-ẓann).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is the emotional state; metaphorical (Majaz) usage is attributed to God (al-Ghayyār) as a figure for His prohibition of indecencies, distinguishing human passion from divine will.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Used to isolate specific obligations (e.g., \"the third is that he should increase...\") and to distinguish categories of jealousy and pride, ensuring precise legal and moral demarcation.","content_type":"hadith","topic":"Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Etiquette of Marriage'","badge":"Hadith"}],"161":[{"arabic":"محمد صلى الله عليه وسلم وملة أبينا إبراهيم حنيفا وما كان من المشركين (9) اللهم بك أصبحنا وبك أمسينا وبك نحيا وبك نموت وإليك المصير (10) اللهم إني أسألك أن تبعثنا في هذا اليوم إلى كل خير ونعوذ بك أن نجترح فيه سوءا أو نجره إلى مسلم فإنك قلت {وهو الذي يتوفاكم بالليل ويعلم ما جرحتم بالنهار ثم يبعثكم فيه ليقضى أجل مسمى} (11) اللهم فالق الإصباح وجاعل الليل سكنا والشمس والقمر حسبانا أسألك خير هذا اليوم وخير ما فيه وأعوذ بك من شره وشر ما فيه (12) بسم الله ما شاء الله لا قوة إلا بالله ما شاء الله","english":"When you rise in the morning and hear the call to prayer (adhān), it is recommended for you to respond to the caller of prayer (mu'adhdhin) — and we have already mentioned this, along with the supplications for entering and leaving the privy, and the supplications of ablution (wuḍū'), in the Book of Purification. When you set out toward the mosque, say: \"O Allah, place light (nūr) in my heart, and light in my tongue, and place light in my hearing, and place light in my sight, and place light behind me, and light before me, and place light above me. O Allah, grant me light.\" And also say: \"O Allah, I ask You by the right of those who ask of You, and by the right of this my walking unto You — for I have not gone out in insolence, nor in vanity, nor for show, nor for reputation. I have gone out seeking protection from Your wrath and seeking Your good pleasure. So I ask You to deliver me from the Fire and to forgive me my sins — verily, none forgives sins but You.\"","book_title":"On Invocations and Supplications","section_title_ar":"Section 254","section_title_en":"Section 254 — Supplications for the Hours of Day and Night: From Dawn to Sleep","section_index":254,"anchors":"- Root: ن و ر (n-w-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: النور: الضوء المنتشر الذي يُبصر به، وهو ضد الظلمة\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): النور في القرآن يقع على ضروب: منها المعقول كالإيمان والحكمة، قال تعالى: {قد جاءكم من الله نور وكتاب مبين}، ومنها المحسوس كضوء الشمس والقمر\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): النور حقيقة في الضوء المحسوس، ومجاز في الهدى والإيمان والعلم — فدعاء النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم بطلب النور يشمل المعنيين جميعاً\n- Root: س ج د (s-j-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: السجود: وضع الجبهة على الأرض تعظيماً، وأصله التطامن والانخفاض\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): السجود ضربان: سجود عبادة وهو وضع الجبهة على الأرض تقرباً، وسجود طاعة وهو الانقياد والخضوع — قال تعالى: {ولله يسجد من في السموات والأرض طوعاً وكرهاً}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): سجد حقيقة في وضع الجبهة، ومجاز في الخضوع والانقياد — وقوله \"سجد لك سوادي وخيالي\" من المجاز المرسل\n- Root: ذ ك ر (dh-k-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: الذكر: نقيض النسيان، والذكر: الصوت الذي يُذكر به الشيء، والذكر: الشرف والصيت\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): الذكر ضربان: ذكر بالقلب وهو التفكر في عظمة الله، وذكر باللسان وهو التلفظ بأسمائه — وأعلى مراتبه ما اجتمع فيه القلب واللسان\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): الذكر حقيقة في استحضار الشيء بالقلب أو اللسان، ومجاز في الصلاة والدعاء والثناء\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — جواز تقديم المعمول وتأخيره مع بقاء العامل عاملاً في المعنى، كقوله \"لك الحمد\" حيث تقدم الجار والمجرور على المبتدأ للاختصاص والاهتمام","content_type":"dua","topic":"Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'On Invocations and Supplications'","badge":"Du'a"}],"162":[{"arabic":"اللهم رب الحمد لك الحمد كما تقول وخيرا مما نقول لك صلاتي ونسكي ومحياي ومماتي وإليك مآبي وإليك ثوابي اللهم إني أعوذ بك من وساوس الصدر وشتات الأمر وعذاب القبر اللهم إني أعوذ بك من شر ما يلج في الليل ومن شر ما يلج في النهار ومن شر ما تهب به الرياح ومن شر بوائق الدهر اللهم إني أعوذ بك من تحول عافيتك وفجأة نقمتك وجميع سخطك اللهم اهدني بالهدى واغفر لي في الآخرة والأولى يا خير مقصود وأسنى منزول به وأكرم مسئول ما لديه أعطني العشية أفضل ما أعطيت أحدا من خلقك وحجاج بيتك يا أرحم الراحمين اللهم يا رفيع الدرجات ومنزل البركات ويا فاط","english":"On the Day of 'Arafah, the first thing with which one supplicates, let him say: There is no god but God alone, with no partner; His is the sovereignty and His is the praise; He gives life and causes death, and He is Living, He does not die; in His hand is all good, and He is over all things Omnipotent. O God, place in my heart a light, and in my hearing a light, and in my sight a light, and in my tongue a light. O God, expand for me my breast and make easy for me my affair. And let him say: O God, Lord of the praise, to You is the praise as You say, and better than what we say. To You is my prayer, my sacrifice, my living, and my dying; to You is my return, and to You is my recompense. O God, I seek refuge in You from the whisperings of the breast, the scattering of affairs, and the torment of the grave. O God, I seek refuge in You from the evil of what enters in the night, and from the evil of what enters in the day, and from the evil of what the winds bring, and from the evil of the calamities of time. O God, I seek refuge in You from the withdrawal of Your well-being, the suddenness of Your retribution, and all of Your wrath. O God, guide me with guidance, and forgive me in the Hereafter and the First. O Best of all sought goals, and Most Exalted of all that is alighted upon, and Most Generous of all who are asked — whatever is with You — grant me this evening the best of what You have given to any of Your creatures and pilgrims of Your Sacred House, O Most Merciful of the merciful. O God, O Raiser of degrees and Sender of blessings, O Originator of the earths and the heavens, voices have clamored to You in diverse languages, asking You their needs. And my need of You is that You not forget me in the abode of trial when the people of this world have forgotten me. O God, You hear my speech and see my place and know my secret and my public, and nothing of my affair is hidden from You. I am the wretched one, the poor one, the seeker of aid, the seeker of refuge, the fearful, the apprehensive, the confessor of his sin. I ask You with the asking of the destitute, and I supplicate to You with the supplication of the abased sinner, and I call upon You with the call of the fearful and distressed — the call of one whose neck has bowed to You, whose tears have flowed for You, whose body has humbled before You, and whose nose has been rubbed in the dust for You. O God, do not make me, by my supplication to You, O Lord, wretched, and be to me kind and merciful, O Best of those who are asked and Most Generous of those who give. My God, whoever has praised his own soul to You — for I am one who blames his own soul. My God, sins have silenced my tongue, so I have no means of intercession other than deeds, and no intercessor other than hope. My God, I know that my sins have left me no standing with You, nor any face for excuse — but You are the Most Generous of the generous. My God, if I am not worthy to attain Your mercy, then Your mercy is worthy to attain me, for Your mercy encompasses all things, and I am a thing. My God, though my sins be great, yet they are small beside Your pardon, so forgive them for me, O Generous One. My God, You are You, and I am I. I am the one who returns to sins, and You are the One who returns to forgiveness. My God, if You show mercy only to the people of obedience, then to whom shall the sinners flee? My God, I turned away from Your obedience deliberately, and I headed toward Your disobedience intentionally. Glory be to You — how great is Your proof against me, and how generous is Your pardon toward me! By the necessity of Your proof against me, and the severance of my excuse from You, and my poverty toward You, and Your richness from me — forgive me, O Best of those whom a caller has called, and Most Excellent of those whom a hope has hoped in. By the sanctity of Islam and by the covenant of Muhammad, may God bless him and grant him peace, I seek intercession with You, so forgive me all my sins, and turn me away from this standing place of mine with my needs fulfilled, and grant me what I have asked, and realize my hope in what I have wished. My God, I have called upon You with the supplication that You taught me, so do not deprive me of the hope that You made known to me. My God, what will You do this evening with a servant who confesses to You his sin, who is humble before You in his abasement, who is submissive in his offense, who pleads to You concerning his deed, who repents to You from his transgression, who seeks Your forgiveness for his wrong, who supplicates to You for pardon of him, who asks of You the success of his needs, who hopes in You at his standing place — despite the abundance of his sins? O Refuge of every living thing, and Protector of every believer — whoever does well, it is by Your mercy that he triumphs, and whoever errs, it is by his error that he perishes. O God, to You we have come forth, and in Your courtyard we have alighted, and You alone we hope, and what is with You we seek, and to Your beneficence we expose ourselves, and Your mercy we hope for, and from Your chastisement we are fearful, and to You with the heavy burdens of sins we have fled, and to Your Sacred House we have performed the pilgrimage. O You who owns the needs of those who ask, and knows the consciences of the silent — O You who has no lord with Him who is invoked, and no creator above Him who is feared, and no minister to whom one comes, and no chamberlain who is bribed — O You who increases not by the abundance of asking except in bounty and generosity, nor by the abundance of needs except in favor and beneficence. O God, You have made for every guest a hospitality, and we are Your guests, so make Your hospitality to us Paradise. O God, for every delegation there is a gift, and for every visitor there is an honor, and for every asker there is a giving, and for every one who hopes there is a reward, and for every seeker there is with You a recompense, and for every...","book_title":"Mysteries of Hajj","section_title_ar":"Section 206","section_title_en":"The Supplications of the Day of 'Arafah — Section 206","section_index":206,"anchors":"- Root: دعو (d-'-w)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to call, summon, invoke; du'a is the act of calling upon God with humble petition.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological usage, du'a is the essence of worship ('ibadah), the creature's acknowledgment of need (iftiqar) before the Creator, and a means of drawing near (taqarrub).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqiqah): vocal calling. Metaphorical (majaz): the soul's inward turning to God, even without utterance.\n- Root: نور (n-w-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Light; that by which one sees physically and, by extension, that by which the heart perceives truth.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Light (nur) is of two kinds: sensory (hissi) and intellectual ('aqli). The prophetic prayer for light in heart, hearing, sight, and tongue seeks the illumination of all faculties for obedience and gnosis (ma'rifah).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical luminosity. Metaphorical: guidance (huda) and clarity of perception in religious and moral matters.\n- Root: عفو ('-f-w)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To pardon, to efface, to leave unpunished; to exceed in generosity.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): 'Afw is the erasure of sin and the lifting of its consequence; it is an attribute of God's mercy (rahmah) that supersedes His justice when the servant repents.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to obliterate a trace. Metaphorical: God's complete remission of sin, leaving no mark of accountability.\n- Root: رجا (r-j-w)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Hope, expectation of good; the opposite of despair (ya's).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Raja' is the heart's confident expectation of God's bounty, paired with fear (khawf); it is a station (maqam) of the sincere.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to await. Metaphorical: the soul's reliance on divine mercy despite unworthiness.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Conjunction and Apposition (al-'Atf wa al-Badal) — The repeated vocatives \"Ilahi\" (My God) and \"Allahumma\" (O God) function as independent invocations, each followed by coordinated clauses that build a cumulative structure of petition, confession, and praise, requiring faithful preservation of each unit without conflation.","content_type":"afterlife","topic":"Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Mysteries of Hajj'","badge":"Afterlife"}],"164":[{"arabic":"وحسناته فإنه من كسبه وغير مؤاخذ بسيئاته فإنه لا تزر وازرة وزر أخرى ولذلك قال تعالى ألحقنا بهم ذرياتهم وما ألتناهم من عملهم من شيء أي ما نقصناهم من أعمالهم وجعلنا أولادهم مزيدا في إحسانهم الوجه الرابع أن يموت الولد قبله فيكون له شفيعا فقد روي عن رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم أنه قال إن الطفل يجر بأبويه إلى الجنة (5) وفي بعض الأخبار يأخذ بثوبه كما أنا الآن آخذ بثوبك (6) وقال أيضا صلى الله عليه وسلم إن المولود يقال ادخل الجنة فيقف على باب الجنة فيظل محبنطئا أي ممتلئا غيظا وغضبا ويقول لا أدخل الجنة إلا وأبواي معي فيقال أدخلوا","english":"وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم: \"لا يلقى الله أحد بذنب أعظم من جهالة أهله.\"","book_title":"Condemnation of Envy","section_title_ar":"Section 296","section_title_en":"Section 296","section_index":296,"anchors":"*(Anchors established)*","content_ty